(window.QUIZ_CHUNKS=window.QUIZ_CHUNKS||{})["e2b.779a327e24.js"]={"33":[{"id":"sdl33_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply knowledge of the clinical presentations, etiologies, pathology, pathogenesis, gross and microscopic changes, complications and management of Diabetes Mellitus, Pancreatic Endocrine Tumors and Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia.","batch":1,"stem":"Which pathway of hyperglycemic end-organ damage involves the non-enzymatic covalent modification of proteins and lipids, leading to crosslinking of basement membranes and reduced vascular compliance?","choices":{"B":"Advanced glycation end-product (AGE) formation by non-enzymatic glycation of proteins, crosslinking basement membranes and activating RAGE on macrophages","A":"Polyol pathway activation via aldose reductase converting glucose to sorbitol, depleting NADPH and causing osmotic cell swelling in Schwann cells and pericytes","C":"Protein kinase C (PKC) activation altering endothelial gene expression of VEGF, TGF-β, and fibronectin to increase vascular permeability and fibrosis","D":"Hexosamine pathway flux modifying transcription factors via glucosamine metabolites, altering gene expression patterns that regulate cell growth and apoptosis","E":"Reactive oxygen species generation from mitochondrial electron transport chain uncoupling, causing lipid peroxidation of endothelial cell membranes"},"explanation":"AGE formation occurs when glucose reacts non-enzymatically with amino groups of proteins and lipids. AGEs bind RAGE on macrophages, T cells, endothelium and vascular smooth muscle; Robbins (course reading) says AGE-RAGE signaling releases TGF-β, which leads to deposition of excess basement membrane material, along with VEGF, reactive oxygen species and procoagulant activity. The crosslinking of matrix proteins named in the stem is standard teaching that the course text does not describe. The four canonical pathways of hyperglycemic damage are AGEs (crosslink basement membrane), PKC (alter VEGF/TGF-β/fibrosis), polyol (sorbitol accumulation/osmotic stress), and hexosamine (transcription factor modification); each contributes to end-organ damage through distinct but converging mechanisms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"4 mechanisms of DM damage: AGEs (crosslink BM/activate RAGE), PKC (VEGF, fibrosis), polyol (sorbitol osmotic/NADPH depletion), hexosamine (TF modification).\" COMLEX: DM microangiopathy underlies nephropathy, retinopathy, and neuropathy — AGE-RAGE axis is the HY mechanistic bridge tested on boards.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 6 — DM complications pathophysiology establishes the molecular damage pathways (AGEs, PKC, polyol, hexosamine) expressed as retinopathy, nephropathy, neuropathy, and macrovascular disease","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B"},{"id":"sdl33_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply knowledge of the clinical presentations, etiologies, pathology, pathogenesis, gross and microscopic changes, complications and management of Diabetes Mellitus, Pancreatic Endocrine Tumors and Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia.","batch":1,"stem":"Patients with diabetes mellitus have enhanced susceptibility to infections of the skin and to tuberculosis, pneumonia, and pyelonephritis. Which set of factors best explains this susceptibility?","choices":{"C":"Impaired neutrophil chemotaxis, adherence, phagocytosis, and killing, less macrophage cytokine output, and poor delivery of immune cells","A":"Absent B lymphocytes from autoimmune destruction alongside beta cells, causing very low antibody levels in both type 1 and type 2 diabetes","B":"Glucose in sweat and urine that feeds microbes, while neutrophil and macrophage function remain fully normal in patients with diabetes","D":"Complement deficiency from glycated C3 that cannot be cleaved, so opsonization fails while phagocytes and blood supply stay intact","E":"Thymic atrophy from hyperglycemia, causing loss of T cells, which blocks cellular immunity while phagocyte function stays intact"},"explanation":"Robbins states that patients with diabetes exhibit enhanced susceptibility to infections of the skin and to tuberculosis, pneumonia, and pyelonephritis, and that such infections cause the deaths of about 5 percent of these patients. The basis is multifactorial: decreased neutrophil function, with altered chemotaxis, adherence to the endothelium, phagocytosis, and microbicidal activity; impaired cytokine production by macrophages; and vascular compromise that impairs delivery of immune cells and molecules to sites of infection. Type 2 diabetes has no autoimmune basis, and B cells are not destroyed in either type. Normal phagocyte function is contradicted by the source. Glycated C3 and hyperglycemic thymic atrophy are not the mechanisms described.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Diabetic infections: neutrophil dysfunction, impaired macrophage cytokines, and vascular disease that limits delivery of immune cells (Robbins). In a patient with neuropathy, a trivial toe infection can start a sequence of gangrene, bacteremia, and pneumonia. COMLEX: Lower extremity gangrene is about 100 times more common in diabetes than in the general population (Robbins).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 6 — pathophysiology of diabetes mellitus complications","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"Type 2 diabetes has no autoimm","B":"Normal phagocyte function is c","D":"Glycated C3 and hyperglycemic "}},{"id":"sdl33_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply knowledge of the clinical presentations, etiologies, pathology, pathogenesis, gross and microscopic changes, complications and management of Diabetes Mellitus, Pancreatic Endocrine Tumors and Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following best explains why diabetic retinopathy is the leading cause of blindness in working-age adults in developed countries?","choices":{"A":"Thickening of choroidal vasculature from AGE deposition blocks nutrient diffusion to the outer retina, causing photoreceptor apoptosis without neovascularization","B":"Chronic hyperglycemia directly damages photoreceptor outer segments through osmotic pressure from retinal sorbitol accumulation via the polyol pathway","C":"Retinal ischemia drives VEGF-mediated neovascularization, producing fragile new vessels that bleed into the vitreous and contract, causing tractional retinal detachment","D":"Autoimmune destruction of Müller glia cells triggered by retinal AGE-modified proteins produces a pattern similar to paraneoplastic retinopathy","E":"Pericyte loss from polyol pathway damage causes focal arteriolar occlusion producing non-proliferative microaneurysms that are the direct cause of irreversible blindness"},"explanation":"About 60–80% of patients with diabetes develop some form of retinopathy (Robbins Ch 24). First Aid describes nonproliferative disease with capillary damage, hemorrhages, microaneurysms and vision loss mainly from macular edema, and proliferative disease in which chronic hypoxia drives new blood vessel formation. The fragile new vessels bleed into the vitreous and contract, causing tractional retinal detachment — the vision-destroying event. Nonproliferative changes such as microaneurysms and macular edema also reduce vision, but the vitreous hemorrhage and tractional detachment of proliferative disease cause the most severe vision loss. Intravitreal anti-VEGF agents are now first-line for proliferative disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"DM retinopathy: pericyte loss → microaneurysms → VEGF → neovascularization → vitreous hemorrhage/tractional detachment.\" COMLEX: Diabetes is the leading cause of adult-onset blindness in the United States, so patients need regular dilated eye exams.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 6 — DM complications pathophysiology establishes the molecular damage pathways (AGEs, PKC, polyol, hexosamine) expressed as retinopathy, nephropathy, neuropathy, and macrovascular disease","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"E":"Nonproliferative changes such as microaneurysms"}},{"id":"sdl33_q04","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply knowledge of the clinical presentations, etiologies, pathology, pathogenesis, gross and microscopic changes, complications and management of Diabetes Mellitus, Pancreatic Endocrine Tumors and Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia.","batch":1,"stem":"A 3-cm well-differentiated pancreatic neuroendocrine neoplasm is resected. The pathologist notes that its behavior cannot be predicted reliably from its light microscopic appearance alone. Which features are used to grade this tumor?","choices":{"D":"Mitotic activity and the Ki-67 proliferative index, with a higher proliferation index predicting more aggressive behavior","A":"Tumor size and the hormone it secretes, with insulin-secreting tumors given the highest grade because of their hypoglycemia","B":"Degree of nuclear pleomorphism and amount of stromal amyloid, with amyloid-rich tumors graded as the most aggressive","C":"Number of chromogranin-positive cells and tumor location, with tumors in the pancreatic tail graded as more aggressive","E":"Serum hormone level and patient age, with tumors in older patients given a higher grade because they grow more quickly"},"explanation":"Robbins explains that, as with other endocrine neoplasms, the behavior of a pancreatic neuroendocrine neoplasm is difficult to predict from its light microscopic appearance, although tumors with a higher proliferation index are more likely to be aggressive. Well-differentiated tumors are graded on mitotic activity and expression of the cell cycle marker Ki-67, which gives a proliferative index; pancreatic neuroendocrine carcinomas are poorly differentiated, of small or large cell type, with a Ki-67 index above 20 percent. Functional status has some impact on prognosis: about 90 percent of insulin-producing tumors are benign, while 60 to 90 percent of other functioning and nonfunctioning tumors are malignant, so insulinomas are the least, not the most, aggressive. Pleomorphism and amyloid, chromogranin counts and location, and serum hormone levels with age are not the grading criteria.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pancreatic neuroendocrine neoplasms (Robbins): about 2 percent of pancreatic neoplasms; graded by mitoses and Ki-67; carcinomas have Ki-67 above 20 percent; about 90 percent of insulinomas are benign, while 60 to 90 percent of others are malignant. COMLEX: Functional syndromes: insulinoma (hypoglycemia), gastrinoma (Zollinger-Ellison), glucagonoma (necrolytic migratory erythema), somatostatinoma (diabetes, gallstones, steatorrhea, hypochlorhydria), VIPoma (watery diarrhea, hypokalemia, achlorhydria) (Robbins).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — somatostatin and other factors that decrease insulin secretion","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"Functional status has some imp"}},{"id":"sdl33_q05","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply knowledge of the clinical presentations, etiologies, pathology, pathogenesis, gross and microscopic changes, complications and management of Diabetes Mellitus, Pancreatic Endocrine Tumors and Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia.","batch":1,"stem":"Compared with a non-diabetic individual, a patient with long-standing type 2 DM has approximately which level of increased risk for myocardial infarction and stroke, and what is the primary mechanism driving this excess risk?","choices":{"C":"6- to 8-fold increased risk for both MI and stroke, driven principally by hypercoagulability from AGE-modified fibrinogen and platelet-rich thrombus formation","A":"No significant independent increase; cardiovascular risk in DM is fully explained by coexisting hypertension and dyslipidemia without additional DM-specific vascular mechanisms","B":"1.5- to 2-fold increased risk limited to coronary artery disease only, with no significant independent effect on cerebrovascular events or peripheral arterial disease","D":"2- to 4-fold increased risk for both MI and stroke, driven by accelerated atherosclerosis with endothelial injury, procoagulant activity, and smooth muscle proliferation","E":"10-fold increased risk for MI only, driven primarily by autonomic neuropathy of the cardiac conduction system rather than accelerated coronary atherosclerosis"},"explanation":"Robbins (course reading) says patients with diabetes have a two to four times greater incidence of coronary artery disease and a four-fold higher risk of dying from cardiovascular complications than age-matched people without diabetes, and that macrovascular disease causes accelerated atherosclerosis with increased risk of myocardial infarction, stroke and lower extremity ischemia. Robbins does not give a separate multiple for stroke. Among the vascular effects of AGE-RAGE signaling, Robbins lists reactive oxygen species in endothelial cells, increased procoagulant activity on endothelial cells and macrophages, and enhanced proliferation of vascular smooth muscle cells. An elevated cardiovascular risk is seen even in prediabetes, and cardiovascular disease is a leading cause of death in long-standing diabetes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"DM macrovascular: 2–4× ↑ MI and stroke; accelerated atherosclerosis via AGEs, PKC, oxidized LDL, platelet hyperactivation.\" COMLEX: DM is considered a CAD equivalent — treat with high-intensity statin therapy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 6 — DM complications pathophysiology establishes the molecular damage pathways (AGEs, PKC, polyol, hexosamine) expressed as retinopathy, nephropathy, neuropathy, and macrovascular disease","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D"},{"id":"sdl33_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply knowledge of the clinical presentations, etiologies, pathology, pathogenesis, gross and microscopic changes, complications and management of Diabetes Mellitus, Pancreatic Endocrine Tumors and Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia.","batch":1,"stem":"Approximately what proportion of patients with diabetes mellitus develop clinical evidence of nephropathy, and what is the earliest detectable laboratory sign that precedes overt proteinuria by years?","choices":{"A":"30–40% of diabetic patients develop nephropathy; microalbuminuria (urine albumin 30–300 mg/day) is the earliest clinical marker, preceding overt proteinuria by years","B":"10–15% of diabetic patients develop nephropathy; hyperfiltration with elevated GFR is the most clinically reliable first laboratory marker used for routine screening","C":"60–70% of diabetic patients develop nephropathy; hematuria is the initial sign caused by progressive glomerular basement membrane thinning and capillary rupture","D":"5–10% of diabetic patients develop nephropathy; nephrotic-range proteinuria appears abruptly without a detectable microalbuminuric prodrome in most cases","E":"50–60% of diabetic patients develop nephropathy; the initial sign is hyperkalemia from type 4 renal tubular acidosis before any proteinuria is detectable"},"explanation":"Diabetic nephropathy develops in 30–40% of diabetic patients and is the most common cause of ESRD in developed countries. The pathologic sequence is: glomerular hyperfiltration → microalbuminuria (30–300 mg/day, detected by spot urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio) → overt proteinuria (>300 mg/day), as mesangial expansion progresses to Kimmelstiel-Wilson nodules → declining GFR → ESRD. Hyperfiltration occurs early but is not used as a clinical screening marker. ACE inhibitors and ARBs are nephroprotective by reducing intraglomerular pressure. Type 4 RTA with hyperkalemia does occur later in DM nephropathy but is not the initial screening marker. Nephropathy develops in 30 to 40% of diabetic patients, not 60 to 70%, and microalbuminuria rather than hematuria is the earliest marker. Nephropathy affects 30 to 40% of patients and is preceded by years of microalbuminuria, not abrupt nephrotic-range proteinuria.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"DM nephropathy: 30–40% prevalence; microalbuminuria → overt proteinuria → ESRD; Kimmelstiel-Wilson on biopsy; ACEI/ARB nephroprotective.\" COMLEX: Annual urine ACR screening links SDL 33 pathology to clinical management and preventive care objectives.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 6 — DM complications pathophysiology establishes the molecular damage pathways (AGEs, PKC, polyol, hexosamine) expressed as retinopathy, nephropathy, neuropathy, and macrovascular disease","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"C":"Nephropathy develops in 30 to 40% of diabetic patients, not ","D":"Nephropathy affects 30 to 40% of patients and is preceded by"}},{"id":"sdl33_q07","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply knowledge of the clinical presentations, etiologies, pathology, pathogenesis, gross and microscopic changes, complications and management of Diabetes Mellitus, Pancreatic Endocrine Tumors and Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia.","batch":1,"stem":"A pathologist examining a resected pancreatic mass describes a well-encapsulated, 1.5 cm lesion with uniform polygonal cells and abundant amyloid deposits in the stroma on H&E. The mass was removed after a patient had recurrent fasting hypoglycemia with serum glucose 42 mg/dL and elevated C-peptide. Which of the following correctly characterizes this tumor?","choices":{"C":"The most common pancreatic neuroendocrine neoplasm, arising from beta cells; about 90% are benign, small (<2 cm), and encapsulated, with stromal amyloid deposition","A":"Malignant with greater than 50% rate of liver metastasis at diagnosis, located in the gastrinoma triangle; surgical cure is rarely achievable at presentation","B":"Derived from delta cells of the islets causing excess somatostatin secretion that inhibits gastrin, CCK, and secretin, producing DM, cholelithiasis, and steatorrhea","D":"Derived from alpha cells causing hyperglucagonemia with necrolytic migratory erythema, mild hyperglycemia, and normochromic anemia in postmenopausal women","E":"Associated with MEN-1 in approximately 80% of cases; usually multifocal and located in the duodenum rather than in the pancreatic parenchyma"},"explanation":"Insulinoma is the most common PanNEN, arising from beta cells. The classic presentation is Whipple's triad: symptomatic hypoglycemia (<50 mg/dL) + low glucose during the episode + relief with glucose administration. Elevated C-peptide confirms endogenous insulin secretion, distinguishing from exogenous insulin injection. Morphologically, insulinomas are typically small (<2 cm), solitary, well-encapsulated, pale-to-red-brown lesions with monotonous polygonal cells and characteristic amyloid deposition (islet amyloid polypeptide/amylin) in the stroma. Approximately 90% are benign; malignancy (~10%) is defined by metastases, not histologic features. Robbins says about 90% of insulin-producing tumors are benign.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"Insulinoma: most common PanNEN; beta cells; Whipple's triad; elevated C-peptide = endogenous; small/encapsulated/amyloid on histo; 90% benign.\" COMLEX: Distinguish insulinoma from factitious hypoglycemia: elevated C-peptide = endogenous (insulinoma or sulfonylurea); suppressed C-peptide = exogenous insulin.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 6 — DM complications pathophysiology establishes the molecular damage pathways (AGEs, PKC, polyol, hexosamine) expressed as retinopathy, nephropathy, neuropathy, and macrovascular disease","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C"},{"id":"sdl33_q08","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply knowledge of the clinical presentations, etiologies, pathology, pathogenesis, gross and microscopic changes, complications and management of Diabetes Mellitus, Pancreatic Endocrine Tumors and Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia.","batch":1,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman presents with multiple recurrent peptic ulcers refractory to twice-daily PPI therapy, including an ulcer in the jejunum. Fasting serum gastrin is 900 pg/mL (markedly elevated). Imaging localizes a tumor in the duodenal wall. Which statement best characterizes the natural history and treatment of her tumor?","choices":{"B":"More than 50% of gastrinomas are invasive or metastatic at diagnosis, about 25% occur in MEN-1, and treatment combines PPIs with surgical resection","A":"This tumor arises exclusively in the pancreatic body and tail; the duodenal lesion represents a lymph node metastasis rather than a primary tumor site","C":"Gastrinoma is the most common functional PanNEN and is universally benign, treated with surgical excision alone with cure rates exceeding 95%","D":"Gastrinomas never involve regional lymph nodes; the primary concern is direct portal vein invasion rather than lymphatic or hematogenous spread","E":"First-line medical therapy is H2 receptor blockade (ranitidine), which adequately controls the acid hypersecretion in greater than 90% of ZES patients"},"explanation":"Gastrinoma causes Zollinger-Ellison syndrome: autonomous gastrin hypersecretion → massive acid output → peptic ulcers in unusual locations (distal duodenum, jejunum). The \"gastrinoma triangle\" defines the anatomical zone (cystic duct confluence → 2nd/3rd duodenal junction → pancreatic neck/body) where most tumors arise; gastrinomas are just as likely to arise in the duodenum and peripancreatic soft tissue as in the pancreas. Over 50% are already invasive or metastasized at diagnosis. PPIs (irreversible H+/K+-ATPase inhibitors) are required for acid control; H2 blockers are inadequate for the degree of hypersecretion. Hepatic metastases portend poor prognosis within 10 years.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"ZES: gastrinoma triangle; >50% malignant at dx; 25% MEN-1; jejunal ulcers; secretin stimulation → ↑ gastrin; PPIs + surgery.\" COMLEX: ZES diagnostic triad = elevated fasting gastrin + basal acid hypersecretion + ulcers in unusual locations; secretin stimulation test confirms diagnosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 6 — DM complications pathophysiology establishes the molecular damage pathways (AGEs, PKC, polyol, hexosamine) expressed as retinopathy, nephropathy, neuropathy, and macrovascular disease","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"The \"gastrinoma triangle\" defi","C":"Over 50% are already invasive ","E":"PPIs (irreversible H+/K+-ATPas"}},{"id":"sdl33_q09","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply knowledge of the clinical presentations, etiologies, pathology, pathogenesis, gross and microscopic changes, complications and management of Diabetes Mellitus, Pancreatic Endocrine Tumors and Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia.","batch":1,"stem":"A postmenopausal woman presents with a chronic migratory rash featuring superficial erosions and crusting predominantly around the perineum and lower extremities, accompanied by mild hyperglycemia and normochromic anemia. Plasma glucagon is markedly elevated. Which set of features most accurately completes the clinical picture of her underlying tumor?","choices":{"A":"Glucagonoma arising from alpha cells; the rash is necrolytic migratory erythema from glucagon-driven hypoaminoacidemia, and the tumor usually lies in the body or tail","B":"VIPoma arising from non-beta islet cells causing WDHA syndrome (watery diarrhea, hypokalemia, achlorhydria) through vasoactive intestinal peptide-mediated intestinal secretion","C":"Somatostatinoma arising from delta cells causing the inhibitory syndrome of DM, cholelithiasis, steatorrhea, and hypochlorhydria by suppressing multiple GI hormones","D":"Insulinoma arising from beta cells causing hypoglycemia through autonomous insulin secretion; the rash results from glycogen deposition in the dermis from hyperinsulinism","E":"Gastrinoma arising from G cells causing Zollinger-Ellison syndrome; the rash represents a drug eruption from long-term PPI therapy required to control acid hypersecretion"},"explanation":"Glucagonoma arises from alpha cells of the pancreatic islets (typically body/tail) and occurs predominantly in postmenopausal women. The key mnemonic is the 4D syndrome: Dermatosis (necrolytic migratory erythema — blistering, crusting rash in perineal/periorificial/lower extremity distribution from glucagon-driven amino acid depletion in skin), Diabetes (mild, from glucagon-driven gluconeogenesis), DVT risk, and Depression. Compare with somatostatinoma (DM + cholelithiasis + steatorrhea + hypochlorhydria) and VIPoma (WDHA: watery diarrhea, hypokalemia, achlorhydria — Verner-Morrison syndrome). Each islet cell tumor produces a distinct clinical syndrome based on its secreted hormone. Insulinoma causes hypoglycemia; the migratory rash with hyperglycemia, anemia and markedly elevated glucagon is necrolytic migratory erythema of glucagonoma, not a glycogen deposition rash. Gastrinoma causes peptic ulcer disease with Zollinger-Ellison syndrome; it does not produce this rash, and the elevated plasma glucagon indicates glucagonoma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"Glucagonoma: alpha cells; 4D — Dermatosis (necrolytic migratory erythema), DM (mild), DVT, Depression; postmenopausal women; elevated glucagon.\" COMLEX: VIPoma = WDHA; Somatostatinoma = DM + gallstones + steatorrhea — memorize tumor-to-syndrome mapping for each islet cell type.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 6 — DM complications pathophysiology establishes the molecular damage pathways (AGEs, PKC, polyol, hexosamine) expressed as retinopathy, nephropathy, neuropathy, and macrovascular disease","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"D":"Insulinoma causes hypoglycemia; the migratory rash with hype","E":"Gastrinoma causes peptic ulcer disease with Zollinger-Elliso"}},{"id":"sdl33_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply knowledge of the clinical presentations, etiologies, pathology, pathogenesis, gross and microscopic changes, complications and management of Diabetes Mellitus, Pancreatic Endocrine Tumors and Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with a 25-year history of type 2 DM and long-standing hypertension has progressive proteinuria and a serum creatinine of 3.2 mg/dL. A renal biopsy is performed. Which vascular finding on the biopsy would point most specifically to diabetes, rather than hypertension, as a cause of his kidney disease?","choices":{"B":"Hyaline thickening of efferent as well as afferent arterioles, a change rarely if ever seen in people without diabetes","A":"Hyaline thickening limited to the afferent arterioles, with normal-appearing efferent arterioles at the glomerular hilum","C":"Concentric onion-skin proliferation of arteriolar smooth muscle cells, with luminal narrowing from the layered cells","D":"Atherosclerotic plaque narrowing the main renal artery, with lipid-laden foam cells beneath its thickened intima","E":"Cholesterol crystals lodged in small renal arteries after release from atherosclerotic plaques in the aorta"},"explanation":"Diabetes damages the kidney through glomerular lesions, renal vascular lesions (principally arteriolosclerosis), and pyelonephritis, including necrotizing papillitis. In diabetic nephropathy hyaline arteriolosclerosis affects not only the afferent but also the efferent arteriole, and such efferent arteriolosclerosis is rarely, if ever, encountered in individuals who do not have diabetes; both hilar arterioles show hyalinosis alongside the nodular glomerular lesions. Hyaline arteriolosclerosis of the afferent arteriole by itself is not specific, because hyaline arteriolosclerosis also occurs in hypertension. Onion-skin hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis is the lesion of severe hypertension. Larger renal arteries develop severe atherosclerosis in diabetes, but its morphology is indistinguishable from atherosclerosis in people without diabetes. Cholesterol emboli come from atherosclerotic plaques in large arteries such as the aorta and are not specific to diabetes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Diabetic kidney: capillary basement membrane thickening, diffuse mesangial sclerosis, and nodular (Kimmelstiel-Wilson) glomerulosclerosis, plus hyaline arteriolosclerosis of both afferent and efferent arterioles; efferent involvement is the diabetes-specific clue. Hyaline arteriolosclerosis in general occurs in hypertension and diabetes; hyperplastic onion-skin arteriolosclerosis occurs in severe hypertension. COMLEX: Renal failure is second only to myocardial infarction as a cause of death in diabetes, and pyelonephritis with papillary necrosis is more common in diabetic patients.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 6 — Four molecular pathways of hyperglycemic end-organ damage underlie all DM microvascular complications","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"Hyaline arteriolosclerosis of ","D":"Larger renal arteries develop "}},{"id":"sdl33_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply knowledge of the clinical presentations, etiologies, pathology, pathogenesis, gross and microscopic changes, complications and management of Diabetes Mellitus, Pancreatic Endocrine Tumors and Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old woman with a 22-year history of type 1 DM presents with inability to feel her feet, progressive hand clumsiness, orthostatic dizziness upon standing, and early satiety with postprandial bloating. Her HbA1c is 9.8%. Nerve conduction studies confirm a length-dependent sensorimotor polyneuropathy. Which of the following best explains the pathophysiologic relationship between chronic hyperglycemia and the full clinical syndrome she demonstrates?","choices":{"E":"Chronic hyperglycemia drives polyol-pathway oxidative stress in nerves along with a diabetic microangiopathy, producing length-dependent sensorimotor neuropathy and autonomic dysfunction (orthostatic hypotension, gastroparesis) concurrently through shared metabolic mechanisms","A":"Anti-GAD65 antibodies produced during T1D beta-cell destruction cross-react with peripheral nervous system antigens, causing immune-mediated demyelination of both sensory and autonomic nerves simultaneously","B":"Repeated episodes of diabetic ketoacidosis produce direct hippocampal and sympathetic ganglia neuronal death through glucolipotoxicity, accumulating over years to produce the described syndrome","C":"Exogenous insulin hyperinsulinemia directly inhibits autonomic neurotransmission at ganglionic synapses and creates a length-dependent axonal energy deficit by suppressing neuronal glucose uptake","D":"Multifocal cholesterol plaque occlusion of multiple vasa nervorum segments produces an ischemic mononeuropathy multiplex pattern simultaneously targeting autonomic, sensory, and motor fibers"},"explanation":"This patient demonstrates both distal symmetric sensorimotor polyneuropathy (impaired sensation, fine motor) and autonomic neuropathy (orthostatic hypotension from sympathetic failure, gastroparesis from vagal dysfunction). Robbins (course reading) says diabetic neuropathy can damage the central nervous system, peripheral sensorimotor nerves and the autonomic nervous system. In nerves, which do not need insulin for glucose uptake, aldose reductase converts excess glucose to sorbitol and depletes NADPH, increasing oxidative stress, and Robbins says the diabetic microangiopathy underlies some forms of neuropathy. Up to 80% of patients who have had diabetes for more than 15 years develop peripheral neuropathy (Robbins Ch 24). Autonomic neuropathy can be life-threatening: gastroparesis causes malnutrition, neurogenic bladder causes infections, and silent cardiac ischemia (from cardiac autonomic denervation) can lead to sudden death.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"DM autonomic neuropathy: gastroparesis (vagus nerve), orthostatic hypotension (sympathetic), neurogenic bladder — all from chronic hyperglycemia via polyol/AGE in autonomic neurons.\" COMLEX: DM autonomic neuropathy can mask angina — high clinical suspicion for silent MI in diabetic patients with unexplained fatigue or dyspnea.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 6 — Pathophysiology of DM complications: AGEs, PKC, polyol, and hexosamine pathways converge on microvascular and neural targets","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E"},{"id":"sdl33_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply knowledge of the clinical presentations, etiologies, pathology, pathogenesis, gross and microscopic changes, complications and management of Diabetes Mellitus, Pancreatic Endocrine Tumors and Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old woman with long-standing type 1 DM has a skin and skeletal muscle biopsy for an unrelated reason. Electron microscopy shows markedly thickened capillary basement membranes in both tissues. Which statement best describes this diabetic microangiopathy?","choices":{"D":"Basement membranes thicken widely, even in renal tubules, nerves, and placenta, yet the capillaries leak more plasma protein","A":"The thickened capillary basement membranes form a tighter barrier, so diabetic capillaries leak less plasma protein than normal","B":"The thickening comes from immune complexes deposited along the capillary basement membranes, where they activate complement","C":"It consists of lipid-laden foam cells beneath the capillary endothelium, the same lesion found in large-artery atherosclerosis","E":"It is a late lesion that first appears after about 20 years of diabetes and does not contribute to retinopathy or neuropathy"},"explanation":"One of the most consistent morphologic features of diabetes is diffuse thickening of basement membranes. It is most evident in the capillaries of the skin, skeletal muscle, retina, renal glomeruli and renal medulla, and it is also seen in nonvascular structures such as renal tubules, Bowman capsule, peripheral nerves and placenta. Despite the thicker basement membranes, diabetic capillaries are leakier than normal to plasma proteins. This microangiopathy underlies diabetic nephropathy, retinopathy and some forms of neuropathy. It begins early: glomerular basement membrane thickening starts as early as 2 years after the onset of type 1 diabetes and amounts to about a 30% increase by 5 years. Lipid-laden foam cells belong to atherosclerosis of the aorta and large and medium-sized arteries, the macrovascular disease of diabetes, whose morphology is the same as in people without diabetes. The basement membrane thickening of diabetes is not an immune complex lesion; it is part of the hyperglycemia-driven microangiopathy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Diabetic microangiopathy = diffuse basement membrane thickening (skin, muscle, retina, glomeruli, renal medulla; also tubules, Bowman capsule, peripheral nerves, placenta) with paradoxically leaky capillaries; it underlies nephropathy, retinopathy and some neuropathy. COMLEX: Macrovascular disease (accelerated atherosclerosis) makes myocardial infarction the most common cause of death in diabetes, and lower-extremity gangrene is about 100 times more common than in people without diabetes.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 6 — Pathophysiology of diabetes mellitus complications, including microvascular disease","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"Despite the thicker basement m"}},{"id":"sdl33_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply knowledge of the clinical presentations, etiologies, pathology, pathogenesis, gross and microscopic changes, complications and management of Diabetes Mellitus, Pancreatic Endocrine Tumors and Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old nurse has recurrent episodes of confusion and sweating. During a supervised fast she becomes confused with a plasma glucose of 38 mg/dL, and her symptoms resolve promptly with intravenous dextrose. Blood drawn during the episode shows a high insulin level and a suppressed C-peptide level, and a sulfonylurea screen is negative. Which is the most likely cause of her hypoglycemia?","choices":{"E":"Self-administered injections of exogenous insulin","A":"An insulin-secreting beta-cell tumor in the pancreas","B":"Covert ingestion of a sulfonylurea secretagogue drug","C":"Diffuse islet cell hyperplasia that oversecretes insulin","D":"Primary adrenal insufficiency causing low serum cortisol"},"explanation":"Insulin and C-peptide are cleaved from proinsulin and released by beta cells in equal amounts, so both are high when the excess insulin is endogenous, as with an insulinoma, islet hyperplasia or an insulin secretagogue. Exogenous insulin lacks C-peptide, and endogenous C-peptide is suppressed by the hypoglycemia, so a high insulin with a low C-peptide during hypoglycemia points to injected insulin (factitious hypoglycemia). An insulinoma produces inappropriately high insulin during hypoglycemia, and the elevated C-peptide confirms that the hyperinsulinism is endogenous. Sulfonylureas stimulate endogenous secretion and would raise C-peptide, and her screen is negative. Islet hyperplasia (formerly nesidioblastosis) is mainly a cause of hypoglycemia in neonates and infants and also raises C-peptide. Adrenal insufficiency is on the differential of hypoglycemia, but it does not produce a high insulin level.","boardPrep":"Step 1: High insulin + high C-peptide = endogenous hyperinsulinism (insulinoma, sulfonylurea or meglitinide); high insulin + low C-peptide = exogenous insulin. Whipple triad: low glucose, hypoglycemic symptoms, relief when glucose is normalized. COMLEX: Draw insulin, C-peptide and a sulfonylurea screen during the hypoglycemic episode; the differential also includes adrenal insufficiency, diffuse liver disease and self-injected insulin.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 1 — Beta-cell insulin secretion mechanism; insulinoma represents autonomous KATP-independent beta-cell hypersecretion","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"B":"Sulfonylureas stimulate endoge","C":"Islet hyperplasia (formerly ne"}},{"id":"sdl33_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply knowledge of the clinical presentations, etiologies, pathology, pathogenesis, gross and microscopic changes, complications and management of Diabetes Mellitus, Pancreatic Endocrine Tumors and Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman has 3 months of large-volume watery diarrhea that continues while she is fasting. Her serum potassium is 2.6 mEq/L, and gastric analysis shows no acid secretion. She has no rash, no gallstones and a normal fasting glucose. CT shows a 3-cm mass in the tail of the pancreas. Which tumor and mediator best explain her presentation?","choices":{"D":"Glucagonoma releasing glucagon, which depletes amino acids and damages the small intestinal mucosa","A":"VIPoma releasing vasoactive intestinal peptide, which drives intestinal water and electrolyte secretion","B":"Somatostatinoma releasing somatostatin, which suppresses many gut hormones and exocrine pancreatic secretion","C":"Gastrinoma releasing gastrin, which drives acid hypersecretion that overwhelms intestinal absorption","E":"Pancreatic carcinoid releasing serotonin, which stimulates intestinal motility and fluid secretion"},"explanation":"Watery diarrhea, hypokalemia and achlorhydria (the WDHA syndrome) are caused by the release of vasoactive intestinal peptide from a VIPoma, a non-alpha, non-beta islet cell tumor. VIP increases intestinal water and electrolyte secretion, relaxes intestinal smooth muscle and inhibits gastric acid secretion; some of these tumors are locally invasive and can metastasize. A VIP assay should be performed in any patient with severe secretory diarrhea. A somatostatinoma causes diabetes, cholelithiasis, steatorrhea and hypochlorhydria, and her normal glucose and absent gallstones argue against it. A gastrinoma causes acid hypersecretion with peptic ulcers, the opposite of her absent acid secretion. A glucagonoma causes necrolytic migratory erythema, mild diabetes and anemia. Pancreatic carcinoid tumors that produce serotonin are exceedingly rare.","boardPrep":"Step 1: VIPoma = WDHA (Watery Diarrhea, Hypokalemia, Achlorhydria); VIP increases intestinal water and electrolyte secretion and inhibits gastric acid; octreotide is used to control symptoms. COMLEX: Measure VIP in any patient with severe secretory diarrhea; neural crest tumors (neuroblastoma, ganglioneuroma) and pheochromocytoma can also cause the VIPoma syndrome.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 1 — Islet cell autonomous hormone secretion; gastrinoma parallels insulinoma as a constitutively hypersecretory neuroendocrine neoplasm","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"A somatostatinoma causes diabe","D":"A glucagonoma causes necrolyti"}},{"id":"sdl33_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply knowledge of the clinical presentations, etiologies, pathology, pathogenesis, gross and microscopic changes, complications and management of Diabetes Mellitus, Pancreatic Endocrine Tumors and Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old man is evaluated for recurrent kidney stones. Serum calcium is 11.8 mg/dL and PTH is 142 pg/mL. Workup also reveals prolactin 310 ng/mL and MRI shows a 12 mm anterior pituitary lesion. His father had a similar constellation of endocrine problems and died of metastatic pancreatic islet cell tumors. Which gene mutation most likely underlies his syndrome, and what functional pancreatic tumor would most commonly produce clinically significant symptoms in this setting?","choices":{"A":"Germline loss-of-function mutation in MEN1 (encoding menin, a tumor suppressor); gastrinoma causing Zollinger-Ellison syndrome is the most common functional pancreatic tumor to produce symptoms in MEN-1","B":"Germline gain-of-function mutation in the RET proto-oncogene encoding a receptor tyrosine kinase; insulinoma is the most common functional pancreatic tumor and presents with recurrent hypoglycemia in MEN-2A","C":"Germline loss-of-function mutation in the VHL tumor suppressor gene; pheochromocytoma is the primary pancreatic neuroendocrine manifestation and requires bilateral adrenalectomy in VHL disease","D":"Germline loss-of-function mutation in CDKN1B encoding cell-cycle checkpoint protein p27; parathyroid adenoma and prolactinoma occur without pancreatic involvement in this MEN-4 variant","E":"Germline loss-of-function mutation in PTEN causing Cowden syndrome; pancreatic polypeptide-secreting tumors are the most common functional PanNEN in PTEN hamartoma tumor syndrome"},"explanation":"This patient has MEN-1 (Wermer syndrome): primary hyperparathyroidism (80–95%, most common initial manifestation), pituitary prolactinoma (most common pituitary tumor in MEN-1), and family history of fatal pancreatic islet cell tumors. MEN-1 is caused by germline loss-of-function mutations in the MEN1 tumor suppressor gene, which encodes menin — a component of transcription factor complexes. Although pancreatic polypeptide is the most frequently secreted product overall (often non-functional), among functional tumors causing clinical syndromes in MEN-1, Robbins (course reading) names Zollinger-Ellison syndrome from gastrinomas and hypoglycemia from insulinomas as most common without ranking them, while First Aid lists Zollinger-Ellison syndrome first. Insulinoma is offered here only with RET and MEN-2A, so the MEN1 choice is the best answer either way. RET gain-of-function mutations cause MEN-2 (pheochromocytoma + medullary thyroid carcinoma ± parathyroid).","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"MEN-1 (Wermer): MEN1/menin TSG (loss-of-function); 3 P's — Parathyroid (most common, initial), Pancreas (gastrinoma/ZES most common functional), Pituitary (prolactinoma).\" COMLEX: Screen MEN-1 family members with periodic PTH, prolactin, and gastrin; genetic testing for MEN1 mutations offered to all first-degree relatives.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 2 — Medullary thyroid carcinoma is the hallmark of MEN-2A/2B; MEN-1 instead features gastrinoma, parathyroid adenoma, and prolactinoma","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"RET gain-of-function mutations"}},{"id":"sdl33_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply knowledge of the clinical presentations, etiologies, pathology, pathogenesis, gross and microscopic changes, complications and management of Diabetes Mellitus, Pancreatic Endocrine Tumors and Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 32-year-old woman is referred after her sister was diagnosed with medullary thyroid carcinoma. Germline genetic testing reveals a gain-of-function RET proto-oncogene mutation at codon 634. Further evaluation reveals bilateral adrenal masses with elevated 24-hour urinary metanephrines and serum calcium 11.2 mg/dL with PTH 138 pg/mL. She has no mucosal neuromas and no marfanoid features. Which of the following correctly identifies her syndrome and the prophylactic intervention most strongly indicated?","choices":{"E":"MEN-2A (Sipple syndrome) from a RET gain-of-function mutation, with medullary thyroid carcinoma, pheochromocytoma, and parathyroid hyperplasia; prophylactic thyroidectomy is recommended","A":"MEN-2B caused by RET gain-of-function mutation; prophylactic thyroidectomy is indicated but should be delayed until after age 30 because MEN-2B medullary carcinomas are less aggressive than MEN-2A tumors","B":"MEN-1 caused by germline MEN1/menin loss-of-function mutation; the elevated PTH reflects primary hyperparathyroidism and total parathyroidectomy is the highest priority intervention","C":"MEN-4 caused by CDKN1B/p27 mutations reducing cell-cycle arrest and phenocopying MEN-1; pheochromocytoma in this syndrome requires bilateral adrenalectomy as the initial intervention","D":"MEN-2B caused by a RET tyrosine kinase domain missense mutation constitutively activating the receptor; medullary carcinoma and pheochromocytoma without parathyroid disease and with mucosal neuromas"},"explanation":"MEN-2A (Sipple syndrome) presents with the triad: medullary thyroid carcinoma (MTC; ~100% penetrance, multifocal, C-cell hyperplasia precedes carcinoma), pheochromocytoma (40–50%, often bilateral), and parathyroid hyperplasia (10–20%). Caused by germline gain-of-function mutations in the RET proto-oncogene (receptor tyrosine kinase). The absence of mucosal neuromas and marfanoid features rules out MEN-2B. All RET mutation carriers are advised to undergo prophylactic thyroidectomy because MTC is life-threatening and preventable if removed before malignant transformation from C-cell hyperplasia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"MEN-2A (Sipple): RET gain-of-function; MTC (100%) + pheochromocytoma (40–50%) + parathyroid hyperplasia (10–20%); prophylactic thyroidectomy for all RET carriers.\" COMLEX: All MTC patients must undergo germline RET testing; genetic counseling and prophylactic thyroidectomy for mutation carriers prevent inevitable MTC.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 2 — Medullary thyroid carcinoma: calcitonin-secreting C-cell tumor; pathognomonic of MEN-2A and MEN-2B; multifocal with C-cell hyperplasia precursor","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"A":"The absence of mucosal neuroma","D":"The absence of mucosal neuroma"}},{"id":"sdl33_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply knowledge of the clinical presentations, etiologies, pathology, pathogenesis, gross and microscopic changes, complications and management of Diabetes Mellitus, Pancreatic Endocrine Tumors and Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia.","batch":2,"stem":"Two siblings are evaluated after their father died of metastatic medullary thyroid carcinoma. Sibling A, age 28, has bilateral pheochromocytomas, mild hypercalcemia, elevated PTH, and no neurologic symptoms. Sibling B, age 24, has bilateral pheochromocytomas, multiple visible nodules on the tongue and lips, a marfanoid body habitus, and normal serum calcium. Both carry germline RET gain-of-function mutations. Which statement correctly distinguishes the molecular basis and clinical features of these two siblings' syndromes?","choices":{"B":"Sibling A has MEN-2A (parathyroid hyperplasia present); Sibling B has MEN-2B (mucosal neuromas and marfanoid habitus, parathyroid disease absent); both caused by RET gain-of-function mutations, with the MEN-2B change distinct from the MEN-2A mutations","A":"Sibling A has MEN-2B; RET loss-of-function mutations in this sibling suppress C-cell proliferation, explaining the absence of medullary thyroid carcinoma reported here in MEN-2B","C":"Sibling B has MEN-2A; the marfanoid habitus and mucosal neuromas are features caused by primary hyperparathyroidism-related calcium dysregulation producing connective tissue abnormalities","D":"Sibling A has MEN-1; the pheochromocytoma represents unusual phenotypic overlap with MEN-2 features in patients who are heterozygous for both RET and MEN1 mutations simultaneously","E":"Both siblings have MEN-2B; the difference in parathyroid involvement is explained by variable penetrance of one identical RET tyrosine kinase domain missense mutation within the same family"},"explanation":"MEN-2A and MEN-2B both feature medullary thyroid carcinoma and pheochromocytoma from RET gain-of-function mutations, but differ critically: MEN-2A (Sipple) adds parathyroid hyperplasia (10–20%) — present in Sibling A; MEN-2B adds mucosal neuromas/ganglioneuromas (tongue, lips, GI), marfanoid habitus with long axial skeleton and hyperextensible joints, and crucially has NO parathyroid involvement. Molecular distinction: Robbins (course reading) says a germline missense mutation causing a single amino acid change in RET, distinct from the mutations seen in MEN-2A, is responsible for virtually all MEN-2B; it affects a critical region of the tyrosine kinase domain and activates RET without ligand. RET mutations are always gain-of-function in MEN syndromes; loss-of-function RET mutations cause Hirschsprung disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"MEN-2A vs 2B: Both RET gain-of-function + MTC + pheo; 2A adds parathyroid hyperplasia; 2B adds mucosal neuromas + marfanoid + NO parathyroid.\" COMLEX: the timing of prophylactic thyroidectomy depends on the specific RET mutation and is earliest for the highest-risk codons.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 3 — Pheochromocytoma clinical presentation, diagnosis, and preoperative alpha-blockade: shared adrenal lesion in both MEN-2A and MEN-2B","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"RET mutations are always gain-"}},{"id":"sdl33_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply knowledge of the clinical presentations, etiologies, pathology, pathogenesis, gross and microscopic changes, complications and management of Diabetes Mellitus, Pancreatic Endocrine Tumors and Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old man is found on genetic counseling to carry a germline RET proto-oncogene gain-of-function mutation at codon 634, identified after his mother's diagnosis of medullary thyroid carcinoma. Physical examination is unremarkable; there is no palpable neck mass, no hypertension, and no signs of pheochromocytoma. Serum calcitonin is mildly elevated at 28 pg/mL. Neck ultrasound shows no discrete thyroid mass. Which of the following best explains why prophylactic thyroidectomy is recommended despite the absence of an identifiable thyroid lesion on imaging?","choices":{"C":"Germline RET gain-of-function mutations constitutively activate C-cell proliferation, producing a predictable progression from C-cell hyperplasia to medullary thyroid carcinoma that is life-threatening once metastatic; prophylactic thyroidectomy prevents this inevitable transformation","A":"RET mutations activate Wnt/β-catenin signaling in follicular thyroid cells, causing accelerated papillary carcinoma that metastasizes silently before becoming detectable on neck ultrasound imaging","B":"The mildly elevated calcitonin confirms the presence of established microcarcinoma and surgical resection is curative in virtually all cases when performed before lymph node metastases develop","D":"RET mutations increase anaplastic transformation risk in pre-existing follicular adenomas; thyroidectomy removes these occult adenomas before they de-differentiate into aggressive anaplastic carcinoma","E":"Prophylactic thyroidectomy is recommended primarily to prevent parathyroid hyperplasia progression; the thyroidectomy requires simultaneous parathyroidectomy to achieve complete endocrine cure in MEN-2A"},"explanation":"In MEN-2A and MEN-2B, germline gain-of-function RET mutations constitutively activate the RET receptor tyrosine kinase in thyroid C cells (parafollicular cells), driving an inevitable progression: C-cell hyperplasia → microscopic MTC → macroscopic MTC → lymph node and distant metastatic disease. Calcitonin is the tumor marker for C-cell neoplasia; Robbins says C-cell hyperplasia is believed to be the precursor lesion in syndromic cases. Since MTC is life-threatening and Robbins says it can be prevented by early thyroidectomy, prophylactic thyroidectomy is performed before malignant transformation — with the timing set by the specific RET mutation. This is one of medicine's most powerful examples of genetic screening enabling cancer prevention. Post-thyroidectomy calcitonin monitoring tracks recurrence.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"MEN-2 prophylactic thyroidectomy: RET mutations → C-cell hyperplasia → inevitable MTC; calcitonin is tumor marker; screen family members with germline RET testing.\" COMLEX: Calcitonin + CEA monitored post-thyroidectomy; all patients with apparent sporadic MTC should undergo germline RET testing because occult germline mutations are common.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 2 — Medullary thyroid carcinoma: C-cell origin, calcitonin marker, RET mutations in sporadic and familial forms, multifocal with C-cell hyperplasia precursor stage","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"B":"Calcitonin is the tumor marker for C-cell neoplasia; Robbins"}},{"id":"sdl40_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply knowledge of the clinical presentations, etiologies, pathology, pathogenesis, gross and microscopic changes, complications and management of Diabetes Mellitus, Pancreatic Endocrine Tumors and Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the hallmark features that distinguish MEN syndrome-associated endocrine tumors from their sporadic counterparts?","choices":{"E":"MEN tumors arise at a younger age, affect multiple organs, are often multifocal, and recur more often","A":"MEN tumors are universally benign and carry a favorable prognosis relative to sporadic variants","B":"MEN tumors arise exclusively from acquired somatic mutations driven by hormonal hyperstimulation","C":"MEN tumors follow a unicentric pattern and arise in only one endocrine organ per affected individual","D":"MEN tumors are distinguished by late-life onset and a lower rate of recurrence after surgical resection"},"explanation":"MEN syndromes are inherited disorders causing proliferative lesions (hyperplasia, adenomas, carcinomas) of multiple endocrine organs. Compared with sporadic counterparts, MEN tumors occur at a younger age, are multifocal even within a single organ, arise synchronously or metachronously across multiple organs, are preceded by an asymptomatic hyperplasia stage, and are more aggressive with higher recurrence rates. These shared characteristics apply to MEN-1, MEN-2A, MEN-2B, and MEN-4. MEN syndromes are inherited germline disorders, not tumors arising exclusively from acquired somatic mutations.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MEN tumors = younger age, multifocal, multiorgan, more aggressive than sporadic. The asymptomatic hyperplasia precursor stage is a key concept tested in MEN pathogenesis questions. COMLEX: Hereditary endocrine neoplasia syndromes are CP3 named conditions — recognizing their inherited pattern prompts genetic counseling and family screening on COMLEX clinical reasoning questions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 1 — The receptor tyrosine kinase mechanism of hormone binding, receptor dimerization, and autophosphorylation is the molecular foundation for understanding how gain-of-function mutations in the RET proto-oncogene constitutively activate RTK signaling to drive the neoplastic transformation central to MEN-2 pathogenesis.","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"A":"Compared with sporadic counter","B":"MEN syndromes are inherited germline disorders, not tumors a","C":"Compared with sporadic counter","D":"Compared with sporadic counter"}},{"id":"sdl40_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply knowledge of the clinical presentations, etiologies, pathology, pathogenesis, gross and microscopic changes, complications and management of Diabetes Mellitus, Pancreatic Endocrine Tumors and Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia.","batch":1,"stem":"Before overt adenoma or carcinoma develops in MEN syndromes, what stage is typically observed in the endocrine organ that will be affected?","choices":{"B":"Asymptomatic hyperplasia of the cell of origin preceding frank neoplastic transformation","A":"Fibrotic replacement of the endocrine gland parenchyma without any preceding proliferative change","C":"Inflammatory lymphocytic infiltration of the gland creating a pre-neoplastic autoimmune milieu","D":"Progressive ischemic atrophy of endocrine tissue caused by vascular compression from adjacent tumors","E":"Cystic degeneration of normal follicular endocrine cells with colloid accumulation preceding transformation"},"explanation":"In MEN syndromes, the development of frank adenoma or carcinoma is typically preceded by an asymptomatic stage of hyperplasia involving the cell of origin. For example, in MEN-2, C-cell hyperplasia of the thyroid is virtually always present adjacent to medullary carcinomas, and individuals carrying germline RET mutations almost universally show C-cell hyperplasia before frank malignancy develops. This precursor stage is the biological rationale for prophylactic surgery in mutation carriers. Lymphocytic infiltration of the gland is the picture of autoimmune (Hashimoto) thyroiditis, not the precursor stage of MEN tumors. Robbins says MEN tumors are usually preceded by asymptomatic hyperplasia of the cell of origin, not fibrotic replacement. The precursor stage in MEN is hyperplasia, not ischemic atrophy. Cystic degeneration with colloid accumulation is not the precursor stage; the precursor in MEN is hyperplasia of the cell of origin.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MEN tumor precursor = asymptomatic hyperplasia of cell of origin (e.g., C-cell hyperplasia before medullary thyroid carcinoma in MEN-2). This is the basis for prophylactic thyroidectomy in RET mutation carriers. COMLEX: Genetic screening identifying RET mutation carriers before symptoms develop is a key preventive medicine principle tested in COMLEX CP3.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 1 — The receptor tyrosine kinase mechanism of hormone binding, receptor dimerization, and autophosphorylation is the molecular foundation for understanding how gain-of-function mutations in the RET proto-oncogene constitutively activate RTK signaling to drive the neoplastic transformation central to MEN-2 pathogenesis.","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"Robbins says MEN tumors are usually preceded by asymptomatic","D":"The precursor stage in MEN is hyperplasia, not ischemic atro","E":"Cystic degeneration with colloid accumulation is not the pre"}},{"id":"sdl40_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply knowledge of the clinical presentations, etiologies, pathology, pathogenesis, gross and microscopic changes, complications and management of Diabetes Mellitus, Pancreatic Endocrine Tumors and Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia.","batch":1,"stem":"MEN-1 syndrome is caused by germline mutations in the MEN1 gene. Which of the following correctly describes the mutation type and the function of the encoded protein?","choices":{"D":"Loss-of-function mutations in MEN1 encoding menin, a component of transcription factor complexes","A":"Gain-of-function mutations in MEN1 encoding a receptor tyrosine kinase for growth factor ligands","B":"Loss-of-function mutations in RET encoding a receptor tyrosine kinase that binds GDNF ligands","C":"Gain-of-function mutations in CDKN1B encoding p27, a cell cycle checkpoint kinase inhibitor","E":"Gain-of-function mutations in MEN1 encoding a kinase that phosphorylates cyclin-CDK complexes"},"explanation":"MEN-1 syndrome is caused by germline loss-of-function mutations in the MEN1 tumor suppressor gene. MEN1 encodes menin, a protein that is a component of several transcription factor complexes and, depending on its binding partner and cell context, may either promote or inhibit tumorigenesis. Loss of menin disrupts normal transcriptional regulation and allows uncontrolled proliferation in parathyroid, pancreatic, and pituitary cells. This contrasts with MEN-2, which involves gain-of-function mutations in the RET proto-oncogene. MEN1 encodes menin, a tumor suppressor in transcription factor complexes; a receptor tyrosine kinase with gain-of-function is RET (MEN-2). CDKN1B/p27 mutations cause MEN-4, and MEN-1 is caused by loss-of-function of MEN1, not gain-of-function of CDKN1B. MEN1 loss-of-function mutations disrupt menin; the gene does not encode a kinase that phosphorylates cyclin-CDK complexes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MEN1 = tumor suppressor gene, loss-of-function, encodes menin. MEN-2 = RET proto-oncogene, gain-of-function. The tumor suppressor vs oncogene distinction is classic board fodder. COMLEX: Loss of tumor suppressor function as a two-hit mechanism (Knudson hypothesis) applies to MEN1 and is a high-yield genetics integration concept.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 1 — The receptor tyrosine kinase mechanism of hormone binding, receptor dimerization, and autophosphorylation is the molecular foundation for understanding how gain-of-function mutations in the RET proto-oncogene constitutively activate RTK signaling to drive the neoplastic transformation central to MEN-2 pathogenesis.","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"MEN1 encodes menin, a tumor suppressor in transcription fact","C":"CDKN1B/p27 mutations cause MEN-4, and MEN-1 is caused by los","E":"MEN1 loss-of-function mutations disrupt menin; the gene does"}},{"id":"sdl40_q04","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply knowledge of the clinical presentations, etiologies, pathology, pathogenesis, gross and microscopic changes, complications and management of Diabetes Mellitus, Pancreatic Endocrine Tumors and Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia.","batch":1,"stem":"MEN-1, also called Wermer syndrome, is classically characterized by involvement of which three organ systems forming its diagnostic triad?","choices":{"A":"Parathyroid glands, endocrine pancreas, and anterior pituitary gland","B":"Adrenal medulla, medullary thyroid C-cells, and parathyroid glands exclusively","C":"Parathyroid glands, medullary thyroid carcinoma, and adrenocortical adenomas","D":"Anterior pituitary, adrenal medulla, and mucosal neuromas of the gastrointestinal tract","E":"Endocrine pancreas, adrenal cortex, and medullary thyroid parafollicular C-cells"},"explanation":"MEN-1 is characterized by the three Ps mnemonic: Parathyroid (primary hyperparathyroidism, the most common and earliest manifestation in 80-95% of patients), Pancreas (endocrine pancreatic tumors — the leading cause of MEN-1 morbidity/mortality), and Pituitary (most commonly prolactinoma). The spectrum extends beyond these three: duodenal gastrinomas, carcinoids, thyroid and adrenocortical adenomas, and lipomas also occur with increased frequency in MEN-1. Adrenal medulla and C cells with parathyroid describe MEN-2A, not MEN-1. Pituitary, adrenal medulla and mucosal neuromas are not the MEN-1 triad, which is parathyroid, pancreas and pituitary; mucosal neuromas indicate MEN-2B. MEN-1 involves parathyroid, pancreas and pituitary; medullary thyroid C-cell involvement belongs to MEN-2.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MEN-1 = 3 Ps: Parathyroid, Pancreas, Pituitary. Most common manifestation = primary hyperparathyroidism in 80-95%. Most common cause of death = malignant pancreatic endocrine tumors. COMLEX: The three Ps mnemonic is the most commonly tested MEN-1 fact on CP3 clinical pattern recognition questions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 1 — The six anterior pituitary hormones and their cell types are essential for recognizing which hypersecretion syndrome each MEN-1-associated pituitary tumor produces; prolactinomas and somatotroph tumors are the most frequent pituitary components of MEN-1 and manifest as the overproduction consequences catalogued in SDL 5.","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"Adrenal medulla and C cells with parathyroid describe MEN-2A","D":"Pituitary, adrenal medulla and mucosal neuromas are not the ","E":"MEN-1 involves parathyroid, pancreas and pituitary; medullar"}},{"id":"sdl40_q05","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply knowledge of the clinical presentations, etiologies, pathology, pathogenesis, gross and microscopic changes, complications and management of Diabetes Mellitus, Pancreatic Endocrine Tumors and Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia.","batch":1,"stem":"MEN-2A and MEN-2B are both genetically distinct from MEN-1. Which gene and mutation class underlies both MEN-2 subtypes?","choices":{"D":"Gain-of-function mutations in RET, a receptor tyrosine kinase that normally binds GDNF and related ligands","A":"Loss-of-function mutations in MEN1 encoding the menin transcription factor complex protein","B":"Loss-of-function mutations in RET, a receptor tyrosine kinase causing intestinal aganglionosis","C":"Gain-of-function mutations in CDKN1B encoding p27, a negative cell cycle checkpoint regulator","E":"Gain-of-function mutations in GNAS encoding the alpha subunit of the stimulatory G-protein"},"explanation":"MEN-2A and MEN-2B are both caused by germline gain-of-function mutations in the RET proto-oncogene, which encodes a receptor tyrosine kinase that normally binds glial-derived neurotrophic factor (GDNF) and related ligands. These activating mutations constitutively activate the RET receptor, driving proliferation in C-cells of the thyroid, adrenal chromaffin cells, and parathyroid cells. This is mechanistically opposite to MEN-1, where loss of the tumor suppressor menin drives tumorigenesis. Importantly, loss-of-function mutations in the same RET gene cause Hirschsprung disease. CDKN1B/p27 mutations cause MEN-4, not MEN-2; both MEN-2A and MEN-2B are caused by gain-of-function RET mutations. GNAS gain-of-function mutations cause McCune-Albright syndrome, not MEN-2, which is RET gain-of-function.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MEN-2 = RET proto-oncogene, gain-of-function mutations. Contrast: RET loss-of-function = Hirschsprung disease. All patients with medullary thyroid carcinoma should undergo germline RET testing. COMLEX: The RET gain-of-function vs loss-of-function distinction is a high-yield molecular genetics contrast testable on both boards.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 1 — The six anterior pituitary hormones and their cell types are essential for recognizing which hypersecretion syndrome each MEN-1-associated pituitary tumor produces; prolactinomas and somatotroph tumors are the most frequent pituitary components of MEN-1 and manifest as the overproduction consequences catalogued in SDL 5.","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"This is mechanistically opposi","B":"Importantly, loss-of-function ","C":"CDKN1B/p27 mutations cause MEN-4, not MEN-2; both MEN-2A and","E":"GNAS gain-of-function mutations cause McCune-Albright syndro"}},{"id":"sdl40_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply knowledge of the clinical presentations, etiologies, pathology, pathogenesis, gross and microscopic changes, complications and management of Diabetes Mellitus, Pancreatic Endocrine Tumors and Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia.","batch":1,"stem":"MEN-4 syndrome phenocopies MEN-1 clinically but is caused by a different germline mutation. Which mutation correctly defines MEN-4 and its functional consequence?","choices":{"C":"CDKN1B germline mutation causing reduced levels of the p27 cell cycle checkpoint inhibitor protein","A":"MEN1 germline loss-of-function mutation causing menin deficiency and impaired transcription regulation","B":"RET germline gain-of-function mutation causing constitutive receptor tyrosine kinase activation","D":"VHL germline inactivating mutation causing loss of the von Hippel-Lindau tumor suppressor protein","E":"PRKAR1A germline inactivating mutation causing disinhibited PKA and increased cAMP signaling"},"explanation":"MEN-4 patients have clinical features that phenocopy MEN-1 — similar endocrine tumor pattern involving parathyroid, pituitary, and pancreatic organs — but harbor germline mutations in CDKN1B rather than MEN1. CDKN1B encodes p27 (Kip1), a negative regulator of the cell cycle; reduced p27 levels remove a key checkpoint on cell proliferation. MEN1 mutations are absent in MEN-4. This distinction matters because genetic testing in a patient with MEN-1-like features and no MEN1 mutation should prompt CDKN1B sequencing. A RET gain-of-function mutation defines MEN-2, not the MEN-1 phenocopy MEN-4, which is caused by CDKN1B mutation. VHL inactivation causes von Hippel-Lindau disease; MEN-4 is caused by a germline CDKN1B mutation lowering p27. PRKAR1A inactivation causes Carney complex; MEN-4 is caused by CDKN1B mutation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MEN-4 = CDKN1B mutation, p27 deficiency, MEN-1-like phenotype without MEN1 mutation. This is an emerging entity tested on Step 1 genetics integration questions. COMLEX: When a patient phenocopies MEN-1 but tests negative for MEN1, CDKN1B should be the next gene considered — a clinical genetics reasoning step.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 1 — The six anterior pituitary hormones and their cell types are essential for recognizing which hypersecretion syndrome each MEN-1-associated pituitary tumor produces; prolactinomas and somatotroph tumors are the most frequent pituitary components of MEN-1 and manifest as the overproduction consequences catalogued in SDL 5.","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"MEN1 mutations are absent in M","B":"A RET gain-of-function mutation defines MEN-2, not the MEN-1","D":"VHL inactivation causes von Hippel-Lindau disease; MEN-4 is ","E":"PRKAR1A inactivation causes Carney complex; MEN-4 is caused "}},{"id":"sdl40_q07","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply knowledge of the clinical presentations, etiologies, pathology, pathogenesis, gross and microscopic changes, complications and management of Diabetes Mellitus, Pancreatic Endocrine Tumors and Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia.","batch":1,"stem":"A 36-year-old man with a family history of thyroid cancer is evaluated for MEN-2A. Which combination of findings best defines the classic MEN-2A clinical triad?","choices":{"D":"Medullary thyroid carcinoma, pheochromocytoma, and parathyroid hyperplasia with hypercalcemia","A":"Medullary thyroid carcinoma, mucosal neuromas of the lips and tongue, and marfanoid habitus","B":"Primary hyperparathyroidism, prolactinoma, and Zollinger-Ellison gastrinoma of the duodenum","C":"Insulinoma causing hypoglycemia, parathyroid adenoma, and adrenocortical adenoma secreting cortisol","E":"Papillary thyroid carcinoma, adrenocortical carcinoma, and bilateral pheochromocytoma syndrome"},"explanation":"MEN-2A (Sipple syndrome) is defined by three components: medullary thyroid carcinoma (MTC, occurring in almost 100% of patients, multifocal, virtually always with adjacent C-cell hyperplasia), pheochromocytoma (in 40-50% of patients, often bilateral, may also arise in extra-adrenal sites), and parathyroid hyperplasia (causing hypercalcemia and renal stones in 10-20%). Mucosal neuromas and marfanoid habitus distinguish MEN-2B, not MEN-2A. The three Ps of MEN-1 (parathyroid, pancreas, pituitary) are distinct from MEN-2A. Insulinoma, parathyroid adenoma and adrenocortical adenoma do not make up the MEN-2A triad, which is medullary thyroid carcinoma, pheochromocytoma and parathyroid hyperplasia. MEN-2A has medullary, not papillary, thyroid carcinoma, with pheochromocytoma and parathyroid hyperplasia; adrenocortical carcinoma is not part of it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MEN-2A triad = medullary thyroid carcinoma, pheochromocytoma, parathyroid hyperplasia. MEN-2B = MTC + pheochromocytoma + mucosal neuromas + marfanoid habitus (no parathyroid). COMLEX: Distinguishing MEN-2A from MEN-2B by the presence or absence of mucosal neuromas and marfanoid habitus is a classic CP3 clinical differentiation question.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 3 — The clinical presentation, biochemical diagnosis, and pre-operative management of pheochromocytoma established in SDL 19 apply directly to the pheochromocytoma component of MEN-2A and MEN-2B, where bilateral, extra-adrenal, and recurrent variants are more frequent than in sporadic cases.","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"B":"The three Ps of MEN-1 (parathy","C":"Insulinoma, parathyroid adenoma and adrenocortical adenoma d","E":"MEN-2A has medullary, not papillary, thyroid carcinoma, with"}},{"id":"sdl40_q08","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply knowledge of the clinical presentations, etiologies, pathology, pathogenesis, gross and microscopic changes, complications and management of Diabetes Mellitus, Pancreatic Endocrine Tumors and Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia.","batch":1,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman with MEN-1 presents with recurrent peptic ulcers refractory to proton pump inhibitors and episodic hypoglycemia with a documented glucose of 38 mg/dL. Which pair of pancreatic tumors best accounts for these two findings?","choices":{"A":"Gastrinoma causing Zollinger-Ellison syndrome and insulinoma causing symptomatic hypoglycemia","B":"Glucagonoma causing diabetes and skin rash and VIPoma causing watery diarrhea with hypokalemia","C":"Somatostatinoma causing steatorrhea with gallstones and glucagonoma causing necrolytic skin rash","D":"Two synchronous insulinomas at separate pancreatic sites each independently causing hypoglycemia only","E":"PPoma secreting pancreatic polypeptide alone without producing any recognizable endocrine syndrome"},"explanation":"In MEN-1, pancreatic endocrine tumors are a leading cause of morbidity and mortality. Functional pancreatic tumors produce recognizable hypersecretion syndromes: gastrinomas cause Zollinger-Ellison syndrome (intractable peptic ulcers from massively elevated gastrin), and insulinomas cause hypoglycemic episodes. The duodenum — not the pancreas — is actually the most common site of gastrinomas in MEN-1. Pancreatic polypeptide is the most commonly secreted product overall in MEN-1 pancreatic tumors, but many of these fail to produce a recognizable clinical syndrome. Glucagonoma (mild diabetes, necrolytic migratory erythema), VIPoma (watery diarrhea, hypokalemia, achlorhydria) and somatostatinoma (diabetes, gallstones, steatorrhea) cause neither peptic ulcers nor hypoglycemia. Two insulinomas would cause hypoglycemia only and would not explain the refractory peptic ulcers, which require a gastrinoma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MEN-1 pancreatic tumors: ZES from gastrinoma and hypoglycemia from insulinoma are the two most clinically recognized syndromes. Duodenum is the most common gastrinoma site in MEN-1. COMLEX: Recurrent peptic ulcers unresponsive to PPI therapy should trigger gastrin measurement and ZES workup — a high-yield COMLEX CP3 clinical decision question.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 3 — The clinical presentation, biochemical diagnosis, and pre-operative management of pheochromocytoma established in SDL 19 apply directly to the pheochromocytoma component of MEN-2A and MEN-2B, where bilateral, extra-adrenal, and recurrent variants are more frequent than in sporadic cases.","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"D":"Two insulinomas would cause hypoglycemia only and would not "}},{"id":"sdl40_q09","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply knowledge of the clinical presentations, etiologies, pathology, pathogenesis, gross and microscopic changes, complications and management of Diabetes Mellitus, Pancreatic Endocrine Tumors and Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia.","batch":1,"stem":"Which clinical features uniquely distinguish MEN-2B from MEN-2A and are absent in MEN-2A patients?","choices":{"B":"Mucosal neuromas and ganglioneuromas with a marfanoid habitus and hyperextensible joints","A":"Bilateral pheochromocytomas with elevated urinary metanephrines and paroxysmal hypertension episodes","C":"Multifocal medullary thyroid carcinoma with elevated calcitonin and adjacent C-cell hyperplasia","D":"Parathyroid hyperplasia causing hypercalcemia, nephrolithiasis, and elevated serum PTH levels","E":"Germline RET gain-of-function mutations constitutively activating the receptor tyrosine kinase"},"explanation":"MEN-2B shares with MEN-2A the presence of medullary thyroid carcinoma (more aggressive in MEN-2B) and pheochromocytoma. What uniquely distinguishes MEN-2B is the presence of neuromas and ganglioneuromas involving the skin, oral mucosa, eyes, respiratory tract, and GI tract, combined with a marfanoid body habitus (long axial skeleton, hyperextensible joints). Primary hyperparathyroidism is NOT a feature of MEN-2B (it is present in MEN-2A). Both MEN-2A and MEN-2B are caused by gain-of-function RET mutations, but MEN-2B typically results from a single specific missense mutation in the RET kinase domain. Pheochromocytoma occurs in both MEN-2A and MEN-2B, so it does not distinguish them; mucosal neuromas with marfanoid habitus do.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MEN-2B unique features = mucosal neuromas, marfanoid habitus, NO parathyroid disease. MEN-2B MTC is more aggressive than MEN-2A MTC. COMLEX: A young patient with bumpy tongue lesions, marfanoid body, and elevated calcitonin = MEN-2B until proven otherwise — a classic COMLEX physical exam recognition question.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 3 — The clinical presentation, biochemical diagnosis, and pre-operative management of pheochromocytoma established in SDL 19 apply directly to the pheochromocytoma component of MEN-2A and MEN-2B, where bilateral, extra-adrenal, and recurrent variants are more frequent than in sporadic cases.","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"Pheochromocytoma occurs in both MEN-2A and MEN-2B, so it doe","D":"Primary hyperparathyroidism is"}},{"id":"sdl40_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply knowledge of the clinical presentations, etiologies, pathology, pathogenesis, gross and microscopic changes, complications and management of Diabetes Mellitus, Pancreatic Endocrine Tumors and Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia.","batch":2,"stem":"A genetics lecturer uses two siblings to illustrate contrasting RET mutation consequences. The older sister carries a germline gain-of-function RET mutation and has MEN-2A with medullary thyroid carcinoma and pheochromocytoma. The younger brother carries a germline loss-of-function RET mutation and has Hirschsprung disease. Which statement correctly distinguishes the functional consequence of each RET mutation class?","choices":{"C":"Gain-of-function RET mutations constitutively activate RTK signaling driving endocrine neoplasia; loss-of-function mutations impair enteric neuronal migration causing aganglionosis","A":"Gain-of-function mutations silence RET kinase in C-cells promoting apoptosis and preventing carcinoma; loss-of-function mutations disinhibit RET causing adrenal chromaffin tumor formation","B":"Both mutation classes activate RET constitutively but target different downstream effectors depending on whether cAMP or MAPK signaling predominates in each tissue type","D":"Loss-of-function RET mutations cause medullary thyroid carcinoma by preventing GDNF-driven growth suppression in C-cells during thyroid development and differentiation","E":"Gain-of-function mutations activate RET exclusively in thyroid C-cells; loss-of-function mutations affect only colonic ganglion progenitors during fetal intestinal migration"},"explanation":"RET encodes a receptor tyrosine kinase that normally binds glial-derived neurotrophic factor. Gain-of-function mutations constitutively activate RET signaling — driving uncontrolled proliferation in C-cells, adrenal chromaffin cells, and parathyroid cells — producing the MEN-2 spectrum. Loss-of-function mutations in the same RET gene prevent neural crest-derived intestinal ganglion cell precursors from migrating appropriately into the bowel wall, resulting in intestinal aganglionosis (Hirschsprung disease). These two opposite mutation classes produce entirely different diseases through opposite molecular mechanisms in different developmental cell lineages. Gain-of-function RET mutations constitutively activate signaling and drive tumors, while loss-of-function mutations cause Hirschsprung disease; this option reverses them. Gain-of-function mutations activate RET constitutively, whereas loss-of-function mutations impair it and cause aganglionosis, so they are not both activating. Loss-of-function RET mutations cause Hirschsprung disease, not medullary thyroid carcinoma. Germline gain-of-function RET mutations cause pheochromocytoma and, in MEN-2A, parathyroid hyperplasia as well as medullary carcinoma, so they do not act only in C-cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: RET gain-of-function = MEN-2A, MEN-2B (endocrine neoplasia). RET loss-of-function = Hirschsprung disease (intestinal aganglionosis). High-yield contrast question on Step 1 genetics. COMLEX: The same gene producing opposite diseases depending on mutation type is a classic COMLEX molecular genetics teaching point connecting embryology, genetics, and clinical medicine.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 1 — Tyrosine kinase receptor mechanism including hormone binding, dimerization, and autophosphorylation","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"Gain-of-function RET mutations constitutively activate signa","B":"Gain-of-function mutations activate RET constitutively, wher","D":"Loss-of-function RET mutations cause Hirschsprung disease, n","E":"Germline gain-of-function RET mutations cause pheochromocyto"}},{"id":"sdl40_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply knowledge of the clinical presentations, etiologies, pathology, pathogenesis, gross and microscopic changes, complications and management of Diabetes Mellitus, Pancreatic Endocrine Tumors and Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia.","batch":2,"stem":"A pathology professor explains that MEN-1 involves a tumor suppressor gene while MEN-2 involves a proto-oncogene, yet both ultimately lead to uncontrolled endocrine cell proliferation. Which of the following best summarizes how these two distinct mechanisms converge on the same neoplastic outcome?","choices":{"A":"MEN1 loss removes a growth-suppressing transcriptional regulator; RET gain creates constitutive mitogenic receptor tyrosine kinase signaling","B":"MEN1 loss activates PI3K pathway signaling directly; RET gain activates transcriptional tumor suppressor cascades indirectly","C":"MEN1 gain amplifies cAMP-mediated proliferation in C-cells; RET loss removes inhibitory control of GDNF receptor binding","D":"MEN1 loss drives ACTH-mediated cortisol synthesis; RET gain activates calcitonin secretion from C-cell hyperplasia","E":"MEN1 gain disrupts menin-mediated DNA repair; RET loss triggers constitutive receptor dimerization in endocrine cells"},"explanation":"MEN-1 is caused by loss-of-function mutations in MEN1, a tumor suppressor whose protein product menin participates in transcription factor complexes that normally restrain cell growth. Without menin, normal growth suppression is lost. MEN-2 is caused by gain-of-function mutations in RET, a proto-oncogene encoding a receptor tyrosine kinase that, when constitutively activated, continuously drives mitogenic signaling. Although the genetic mechanism differs (loss of suppressor vs activation of oncogene), both converge on uncontrolled proliferation in the susceptible endocrine cell lineages. This two-pathway model is a high-yield board concept. Robbins describes menin as a component of transcription factor complexes, not a direct PI3K regulator, and RET as a receptor tyrosine kinase whose activation transmits growth signals. MEN-1 is caused by loss, not gain, of MEN1, and MEN-2 is caused by gain, not loss, of RET function. MEN1 loss disrupts transcriptional growth control by menin, not ACTH-mediated cortisol synthesis. MEN1 loss, not gain, causes MEN-1, and RET gain, not loss, causes MEN-2.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tumor suppressor loss (MEN1) and oncogene gain (RET) are the two classic mechanisms of endocrine neoplasia in MEN syndromes. Knowing MEN1 = suppressor and RET = oncogene is required for genetics questions. COMLEX: Understanding oncogene vs tumor suppressor mechanisms contextualizes why MEN-1 requires two hits while RET gain-of-function is dominant with one germline mutation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 1 — Tyrosine kinase receptor mechanism including hormone binding, dimerization, and autophosphorylation","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"Robbins describes menin as a component of transcription fact","C":"MEN-1 is caused by loss, not gain, of MEN1, and MEN-2 is cau","D":"MEN1 loss disrupts transcriptional growth control by menin, ","E":"MEN1 loss, not gain, causes MEN-1, and RET gain, not loss, c"}},{"id":"sdl40_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply knowledge of the clinical presentations, etiologies, pathology, pathogenesis, gross and microscopic changes, complications and management of Diabetes Mellitus, Pancreatic Endocrine Tumors and Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 43-year-old man presents with a kidney stone and is found to have serum calcium of 12.2 mg/dL, elevated PTH, and bilateral parathyroid enlargement. Further workup reveals a prolactinoma on pituitary MRI, elevated fasting gastrin of 1,340 pg/mL, and a 1.2 cm duodenal mass confirmed as a gastrinoma. A sibling has a similar endocrine tumor history. Which of the following best describes the expected initial and most common manifestation in this syndrome?","choices":{"E":"Primary hyperparathyroidism is the initial and most common manifestation appearing in 80 to 95 percent of MEN-1 patients","A":"Prolactinoma is the most common initial manifestation in MEN-1, followed later by parathyroid and then pancreatic lesions","B":"Zollinger-Ellison gastrinoma is the most common pancreatic tumor and is the first sign of MEN-1 in most affected families","C":"Insulinoma causing recurrent hypoglycemia is the most common pancreatic tumor and the initial presenting symptom","D":"Adrenocortical adenoma is the most frequent initial manifestation of MEN-1 preceding parathyroid involvement"},"explanation":"This presentation describes MEN-1 syndrome (three Ps: parathyroid, pancreas, pituitary). Primary hyperparathyroidism is the most common and typically the earliest manifestation of MEN-1, occurring in 80-95% of patients and appearing in almost all affected individuals by 40-50 years of age. The parathyroid abnormalities include both hyperplasia and adenomas. Prolactinoma is the most frequent pituitary lesion, but pituitary involvement is not the most common initial manifestation. Pancreatic polypeptide is the most commonly secreted pancreatic product; among tumors that cause a syndrome, Robbins names Zollinger-Ellison syndrome and insulinoma hypoglycemia together as most common, and First Aid lists Zollinger-Ellison syndrome first. Either way, neither pancreatic tumor is the initial manifestation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MEN-1 initial and most common manifestation = primary hyperparathyroidism in 80-95%. Most common pituitary tumor = prolactinoma. Most common functional pancreatic syndrome = ZES from gastrinoma. COMLEX: Hypercalcemia with elevated PTH in a young patient should trigger evaluation for MEN-1, especially with family history.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 1 — Six anterior pituitary hormones, their cell types, and primary target organs","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E"},{"id":"sdl40_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply knowledge of the clinical presentations, etiologies, pathology, pathogenesis, gross and microscopic changes, complications and management of Diabetes Mellitus, Pancreatic Endocrine Tumors and Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 39-year-old man with known MEN-1 has elevated fasting gastrin of 1,650 pg/mL and secretin stimulation confirms Zollinger-Ellison syndrome. Imaging with CT and endoscopic ultrasound fails to localize the tumor to the pancreas. Somatostatin receptor scintigraphy shows a focus in the duodenal wall. Which of the following best describes the usual location of gastrinomas in MEN-1?","choices":{"B":"The duodenum is the most common gastrinoma site in MEN-1, far more frequent than pancreatic gastrinomas","A":"The pancreatic head is the dominant gastrinoma site in MEN-1 with duodenal gastrinomas occurring rarely","C":"The gastric antrum is the predominant gastrinoma site in MEN-1 arising from G-cell antral hyperplasia","D":"Retroperitoneal lymph nodes harbor the primary gastrinoma in most MEN-1 ZES cases initially","E":"Hepatic parenchyma is the most frequent site due to early metastatic spread from a silent pancreatic primary"},"explanation":"In MEN-1, the duodenum is the most common site of gastrinomas — far in excess of the frequency of pancreatic gastrinomas. Synchronous duodenal and pancreatic gastrinomas may be present in the same individual. This is clinically important because gastrinomas in MEN-1 are often small, multiple, and distributed across the duodenal wall, making localization challenging. The high prevalence of duodenal gastrinomas in MEN-1 contrasts with sporadic gastrinomas, where the pancreatic head/proximal duodenum distribution is more variable. In MEN-1 the duodenum is the most common gastrinoma site, not the gastric antrum. Retroperitoneal nodes are not the usual primary site; the duodenum is the most common gastrinoma site in MEN-1. The liver is not the primary site of gastrinoma in MEN-1; the duodenum is the most common site.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MEN-1 gastrinoma location = duodenum most common (not pancreas). Multiple and small gastrinomas scattered in duodenum are characteristic. COMLEX: Failed pancreatic imaging in ZES with MEN-1 should prompt duodenal exploration and octreotide scanning — a CP3 clinical workup decision point.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 1 — Six anterior pituitary hormones, their cell types, and primary target organs","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"C":"In MEN-1 the duodenum is the most common gastrinoma site, no","D":"Retroperitoneal nodes are not the usual primary site; the du","E":"The liver is not the primary site of gastrinoma in MEN-1; th"}},{"id":"sdl40_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply knowledge of the clinical presentations, etiologies, pathology, pathogenesis, gross and microscopic changes, complications and management of Diabetes Mellitus, Pancreatic Endocrine Tumors and Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia.","batch":2,"stem":"A molecular oncologist explains that unlike MEN-2A, which can result from various activating RET mutations in exons 10 and 11, MEN-2B is caused almost exclusively by a single specific mutation type. Which of the following correctly describes the molecular characteristic distinguishing the MEN-2B-causing RET mutation?","choices":{"C":"A missense point mutation in the RET tyrosine kinase domain causes constitutive activation without ligand binding","A":"A large deletion spanning RET exons 10 and 11 removes the cysteine-rich extracellular domain of the receptor","B":"A promoter hypermethylation event silences RET in thyroid follicular cells causing C-cell compensatory overgrowth","D":"An inactivating frameshift insertion creates a truncated RET receptor that paradoxically activates kinase signaling","E":"A somatic copy number amplification of RET in C-cells drives overexpression without structural receptor alteration"},"explanation":"MEN-2B is caused by a germline missense point mutation in the RET proto-oncogene that leads to a single amino acid change in the tyrosine kinase domain. This substitution affects a critical region of the kinase domain and leads to constitutive activation of the RET receptor in the absence of its ligand — it does not require GDNF or related ligand binding to signal. This specific point mutation is responsible for virtually all cases of MEN-2B. Robbins says this MEN-2B change is distinct from the mutations seen in MEN-2A. Promoter hypermethylation silences a gene, whereas MEN-2B comes from a germline mutation that constitutively activates RET; loss of RET function causes Hirschsprung disease instead.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MEN-2B = specific RET kinase domain missense mutation (single amino acid change), constitutive activation without ligand. MEN-2A mutations are distinct from the MEN-2B change. COMLEX: The single specific mutation causing virtually all MEN-2B cases is a hallmark board fact distinguishing it from the more genetically diverse MEN-2A.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 1 — Six anterior pituitary hormones, their cell types, and primary target organs","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C"},{"id":"sdl40_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply knowledge of the clinical presentations, etiologies, pathology, pathogenesis, gross and microscopic changes, complications and management of Diabetes Mellitus, Pancreatic Endocrine Tumors and Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old woman presents with kidney stones (calcium oxalate), peptic ulcer disease refractory to omeprazole, and amenorrhea-galactorrhea for 6 months. Labs show calcium 12.4 mg/dL, elevated PTH, gastrin >1,400 pg/mL, and prolactin 240 ng/mL. Imaging reveals bilateral parathyroid enlargement, a 1.3 cm duodenal mass, and an 8 mm pituitary microadenoma. Her mother had similar tumors. Which diagnosis unifies all findings?","choices":{"B":"MEN-1 syndrome involving the parathyroid glands, duodenal gastrinoma, and a lactotroph pituitary adenoma","A":"MEN-2A syndrome with parathyroid hyperplasia, medullary thyroid carcinoma, and adrenal pheochromocytoma","C":"Sporadic synchronous endocrine adenomas developing by chance in three separate unrelated organs","D":"MEN-2B syndrome with mucosal neuromas, marfanoid skeletal features, and autonomous pituitary secretion","E":"Autoimmune polyendocrine syndrome causing parathyroid failure alongside pituitary lymphocytic inflammation"},"explanation":"This case illustrates all three Ps of MEN-1: primary hyperparathyroidism causing hypercalcemia and nephrolithiasis, a duodenal gastrinoma causing ZES with refractory peptic ulcers, and a pituitary lactotroph adenoma (prolactinoma) causing amenorrhea and galactorrhea. The family history and early age of onset strongly suggest the hereditary MEN-1 syndrome. Malignant behavior by one or more of these tumors is often the proximate cause of death in MEN-1. MEN-2A involves medullary thyroid carcinoma and pheochromocytoma, not gastrinoma or prolactinoma. Parathyroid, duodenal gastrinoma and prolactinoma with a family history and early onset indicate hereditary MEN-1, not chance sporadic tumors. MEN-2B features medullary thyroid carcinoma, pheochromocytoma and mucosal neuromas, whereas this patient has the MEN-1 triad of parathyroid, pancreas and pituitary. Her high PTH with hypercalcemia, high gastrin and high prolactin reflect overactive tumors, not autoimmune gland failure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MEN-1 triad vignette recognition = hyperparathyroidism plus ZES plus pituitary adenoma. This full clinical triad vignette is a classic Step 1 format. COMLEX: Recognizing MEN-1 from the clinical triad and correctly naming the genetic mutation (MEN1 loss-of-function encoding menin) is a complete COMLEX answer.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 3 — Clinical presentation, diagnosis, and treatment approach for pheochromocytoma","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"C":"Parathyroid, duodenal gastrinoma and prolactinoma with a fam","D":"MEN-2B features medullary thyroid carcinoma, pheochromocytom","E":"Her high PTH with hypercalcemia, high gastrin and high prola"}},{"id":"sdl40_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply knowledge of the clinical presentations, etiologies, pathology, pathogenesis, gross and microscopic changes, complications and management of Diabetes Mellitus, Pancreatic Endocrine Tumors and Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 21-year-old man presents with numerous small bumpy lesions on his tongue, lips, and eyelids. He is unusually tall with long limbs, hyperextensible joints, and a high-arched palate. Ophthalmology notes prominent corneal nerves. Serum calcitonin is markedly elevated and bilateral adrenal masses are identified on CT scan with plasma metanephrines more than four times the upper limit of normal. His father underwent thyroidectomy at age 18. Which diagnosis and molecular basis best describes this patient?","choices":{"A":"MEN-2B from a RET kinase domain missense mutation causing medullary thyroid carcinoma, pheochromocytoma, mucosal neuromas, and marfanoid habitus","B":"MEN-2A from RET gain-of-function mutation causing medullary thyroid carcinoma with parathyroid hyperplasia and hypercalcemia","C":"MEN-1 from MEN1 loss-of-function mutation causing pituitary adenoma with pancreatic neuroendocrine tumor and parathyroid disease","D":"Neurofibromatosis type 1 from NF1 loss-of-function mutation causing peripheral nerve sheath tumors with cafe-au-lait skin spots","E":"MEN-4 from CDKN1B germline mutation causing a MEN-1-like phenotype with pituitary and parathyroid involvement"},"explanation":"This is MEN-2B, the most complete and distinctive MEN-2 subtype. The defining features are medullary thyroid carcinoma (elevated calcitonin), pheochromocytoma (bilateral adrenal masses, elevated metanephrines), mucosal neuromas and ganglioneuromas (bumpy tongue, lip, eyelid lesions, prominent corneal nerves), and marfanoid habitus (tall stature, long limbs, hyperextensible joints, high palate). Primary hyperparathyroidism is absent in MEN-2B, distinguishing it from MEN-2A. The family history of early thyroidectomy indicates familial RET mutation transmission. MEN-2B is caused by a specific missense mutation in the RET kinase domain. MEN-1 involves parathyroid, pancreas and pituitary, whereas this patient has medullary thyroid carcinoma, pheochromocytoma and mucosal neuromas of MEN-2B. NF1 can cause pheochromocytoma but not medullary thyroid carcinoma; high calcitonin with mucosal neuromas and marfanoid habitus points to MEN-2B. MEN-4 phenocopies MEN-1 and does not cause medullary carcinoma with mucosal neuromas.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MEN-2B = mucosal neuromas, marfanoid habitus, MTC, pheochromocytoma, NO parathyroid disease. Classic board vignette: young patient with bumpy tongue lesions, tall stature, elevated calcitonin. COMLEX: Physical exam findings in MEN-2B (corneal nerve hypertrophy, mucosal neuromas) are testable clinical findings in COMLEX structured encounters.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 3 — Clinical presentation, diagnosis, and treatment approach for pheochromocytoma","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"C":"MEN-1 involves parathyroid, pancreas and pituitary, whereas ","D":"NF1 can cause pheochromocytoma but not medullary thyroid car","E":"MEN-4 phenocopies MEN-1 and does not cause medullary carcino"}},{"id":"sdl40_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply knowledge of the clinical presentations, etiologies, pathology, pathogenesis, gross and microscopic changes, complications and management of Diabetes Mellitus, Pancreatic Endocrine Tumors and Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 39-year-old woman with MEN-2A presents with episodic severe headaches, diaphoresis, and palpitations triggered by posture changes. Blood pressure during an episode reaches 210/115 mmHg. Adrenal CT shows bilateral adrenal masses. Plasma free metanephrines are markedly elevated bilaterally. Prior to any surgical intervention, which of the following best describes the expected characteristics of pheochromocytoma in MEN-2A compared with sporadic cases?","choices":{"C":"MEN-2A pheochromocytomas occur in 40 to 50 percent of carriers, are often bilateral, and may be extra-adrenal","A":"MEN-2A pheochromocytomas are always unilateral and histologically identical to all sporadic adrenal pheo variants","B":"MEN-2A pheochromocytomas exclusively arise from adrenocortical zona medullaris cells rather than chromaffin cells","D":"MEN-2A pheochromocytomas are invariably benign with no risk of malignant transformation or distant metastasis","E":"MEN-2A pheochromocytomas develop before medullary thyroid carcinoma and therefore define the initial screening trigger"},"explanation":"Pheochromocytoma in MEN-2A occurs in 40-50% of patients and has important differences from sporadic pheochromocytoma: it is often bilateral and may also arise in extra-adrenal chromaffin sites. Katzung says alpha blockade with phenoxybenzamine is used in the preoperative period, which may prevent blood pressure surges during tumor manipulation. Robbins says all pheochromocytomas should be considered potentially malignant, and the term metastatic pheochromocytoma is used for cases with metastatic disease, so they are not invariably benign.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MEN-2A pheochromocytoma = present in 40-50% of carriers, often bilateral, extra-adrenal possible, preoperative alpha-blockade required. Bilateral pheochromocytoma should always prompt MEN-2A workup and RET testing. COMLEX: Pre-operative alpha-blockade before pheochromocytoma surgery to prevent hypertensive crisis is a high-yield COMLEX clinical pharmacology and management question.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 3 — Clinical presentation, diagnosis, and treatment approach for pheochromocytoma","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C"}],"34":[{"id":"sdl34_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the pharmacokinetics and mechanism of action of sulfonylureas","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the immediate downstream consequence of sulfonylurea-mediated KATP channel closure on pancreatic beta cells?","choices":{"C":"Membrane depolarization triggering voltage-gated calcium channel opening and calcium-dependent exocytosis of insulin granules","A":"Decreased intracellular cAMP leading to reduced protein kinase A activity and inhibition of insulin vesicle trafficking to the plasma membrane","B":"Activation of PPAR-gamma transcription factors in the beta cell nucleus, upregulating insulin gene expression over several weeks","D":"Direct stimulation of SNARE complex assembly proteins to induce exocytosis, bypassing the need for calcium influx entirely","E":"Paracrine inhibition of glucagon secretion from alpha cells via somatostatin release from neighboring delta cells"},"explanation":"Sulfonylureas bind the SUR1 subunit of the KATP channel on pancreatic beta cells, causing channel closure regardless of intracellular ATP levels. Closure prevents potassium efflux, leading to membrane depolarization, which opens voltage-gated calcium channels. The resulting calcium influx triggers exocytosis of insulin-containing granules. This mechanism requires functional beta cells and explains why sulfonylureas lose efficacy as beta cell mass declines in advanced type 2 DM. The mechanism does not involve cAMP, PPAR-gamma, or direct SNARE activation. Sulfonylurea KATP closure causes beta cell depolarization, calcium influx and insulin exocytosis; the paracrine somatostatin effect on alpha cells is not the immediate consequence.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sulfonylurea = KATP closure on SUR1 subunit of beta cell, depolarization, Ca2+ influx, insulin release. Distinguishes from GLP-1 agonists (cAMP/PKA) and metformin (AMPK). COMLEX: Sulfonylurea-induced hypoglycemia in the elderly is a classic clinical scenario. Treat with IV dextrose; monitor for recurrence, especially with glyburide in CKD.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 1 — Glucose-stimulated insulin secretion via KATP channel closure in beta cells","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"The mechanism does not involve","D":"The mechanism does not involve","E":"Sulfonylurea KATP closure causes beta cell depolarization, c"}},{"id":"sdl34_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the pharmacokinetics and mechanism of action of sulfonylureas","batch":1,"stem":"Among the sulfonylureas, which agent is preferred in a patient with moderate renal impairment because it undergoes complete hepatic inactivation and produces pharmacologically inactive metabolites?","choices":{"E":"Glipizide, which is inactivated by hepatic hydroxylation to metabolites that have no hypoglycemic activity and are renally excreted without causing accumulation","A":"Glyburide, which is a potent second-generation sulfonylurea with well-characterized dose reduction guidelines for patients with chronic kidney disease","B":"Chlorpropamide, which has a predictable pharmacokinetic profile and is safely used in renal impairment when the total daily dose is reduced by half","C":"Tolazamide, which is a first-generation agent with lower intrinsic potency, producing less insulin secretion and therefore less hypoglycemia in renal disease","D":"Glimepiride, which is a second-generation agent metabolized hepatically but produces one minor active hydroxyl metabolite requiring careful monitoring in renal impairment"},"explanation":"Glipizide is preferred over glyburide in patients with renal impairment. Glyburide is metabolized to active hydroxylated compounds that are renally excreted and accumulate in renal failure, substantially prolonging hypoglycemic risk. Glipizide undergoes complete hepatic inactivation to inactive metabolites. Chlorpropamide relies heavily on renal excretion of the parent drug and is contraindicated in renal failure. Glimepiride also undergoes hepatic metabolism but produces a minor active metabolite (M1). Among second-generation sulfonylureas, glipizide has the safest renal profile. Katzung lists tolazamide among the older, lower-potency but more toxic first-generation sulfonylureas that are rarely used; glipizide is preferred in renal impairment for its completely inactive metabolites.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Glyburide = avoid in renal failure (active metabolite accumulation). Glipizide = preferred in CKD (inactive metabolites). Chlorpropamide = contraindicated in CKD. COMLEX: Elderly patient on glyburide with unrecognized CKD presenting with altered mental status and low glucose is a high-yield clinical vignette.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 1 — Glucose-stimulated insulin secretion via KATP channel closure in beta cells","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"A":"Glyburide is metabolized to ac","B":"Chlorpropamide relies heavily ","C":"Katzung lists tolazamide among the older, lower-potency but "}},{"id":"sdl34_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the pharmacokinetics and mechanism of action of sulfonylureas","batch":1,"stem":"A 70-year-old man with type 2 diabetes on chlorpropamide develops facial flushing, nausea, and palpitations within 30 minutes of drinking wine at a family event. Which mechanism best explains this adverse reaction?","choices":{"B":"Chlorpropamide inhibits aldehyde dehydrogenase, so acetaldehyde accumulates after alcohol ingestion, causing a disulfiram-like flushing reaction","A":"Chlorpropamide inhibits hepatic CYP2C9, reducing alcohol metabolism and causing sustained elevated ethanol levels with compensatory peripheral vasodilation","C":"Chlorpropamide potentiates ADH action on the renal collecting duct, causing inappropriate water retention and dilutional symptoms after alcohol ingestion","D":"Alcohol displaces chlorpropamide from plasma albumin binding sites, acutely increasing free drug concentration and augmenting insulin secretion","E":"Chlorpropamide activates parasympathetic ganglia in the head and neck, producing neurogenic flushing and tachycardia after alcohol exposure"},"explanation":"Chlorpropamide, like the other first-generation sulfonylureas, can cause a disulfiram-like reaction with alcohol. It inhibits aldehyde dehydrogenase, the enzyme that converts acetaldehyde (the first metabolic product of ethanol) to acetate. Acetaldehyde accumulates, causing facial flushing, tachycardia, nausea, and diaphoresis. This is a separate adverse effect from chlorpropamide-induced SIADH (potentiation of ADH on the collecting duct causing hyponatremia). First Aid limits the disulfiram-like reaction to first-generation sulfonylureas (chlorpropamide, tolbutamide), while Katzung (course reading) says the second-generation agent glyburide can occasionally cause flushing after ethanol ingestion; the classic reaction is the first-generation one.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Two classic chlorpropamide ADRs: (1) disulfiram-like reaction with alcohol via aldehyde dehydrogenase inhibition (shared by first-generation sulfonylureas only, per FA), (2) SIADH via ADH potentiation causing hyponatremia. COMLEX: Chlorpropamide is first-generation and largely replaced by second-generation agents; its unique ADRs remain board-tested.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 1 — Glucose-stimulated insulin secretion via KATP channel closure in beta cells","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B"},{"id":"sdl34_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the pharmacokinetics and mechanism of action of meglitinide analogs","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following best distinguishes the meglitinide analogs from the sulfonylureas with respect to their mechanism of action on pancreatic beta cells?","choices":{"D":"Meglitinides bind a distinct site on the SUR1 subunit of the KATP channel, giving faster onset and shorter insulin secretion than sulfonylureas","A":"Meglitinides activate PPAR-gamma nuclear receptors in beta cells to increase insulin gene expression rather than targeting ion channels directly","B":"Meglitinides stimulate insulin secretion in a fully glucose-dependent manner, never causing hypoglycemia regardless of the dose administered","C":"Meglitinides directly open voltage-gated calcium channels on the beta cell without first requiring KATP channel closure or membrane depolarization","E":"Meglitinides act primarily by inhibiting intestinal alpha-glucosidase as a secondary mechanism that accounts for their mealtime-specific dosing requirement"},"explanation":"Both meglitinides and sulfonylureas close the KATP channel on beta cells to stimulate insulin secretion; however, meglitinides bind a different receptor site on the SUR1 subunit. The pharmacokinetic distinction is that meglitinides have a rapid onset and short duration of action, making them ideal for mealtime use with lower risk of interprandial or nocturnal hypoglycemia. Like sulfonylureas, meglitinides can still cause hypoglycemia because neither class is strictly glucose-dependent. The glucose-dependent insulin secretion distinction belongs to GLP-1 agonists and DPP-4 inhibitors. Meglitinides regulate potassium efflux through the beta-cell ATP-sensitive potassium channel, sharing binding sites with sulfonylureas; PPAR-gamma is the thiazolidinedione target. Meglitinides act by closing the ATP-sensitive potassium channel, and the depolarization that follows is what opens voltage-gated calcium channels; they do not open calcium channels directly. Meglitinides act on the beta cell KATP channel, not on intestinal alpha-glucosidase; their mealtime dosing reflects rapid onset and short duration.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Meglitinides = same target as sulfonylureas (KATP-SUR1), different binding site, shorter duration. Nateglinide is faster and shorter than repaglinide. Both meglitinides = risk of hypoglycemia (not glucose-dependent). COMLEX: Skip meglitinide dose if skipping a meal to reduce hypoglycemia risk.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 1 — Meglitinides share the KATP channel mechanism with sulfonylureas but bind a distinct site on SUR1","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"Meglitinides regulate potassium efflux through the beta-cell","B":"Like sulfonylureas, meglitinid","C":"Meglitinides act by closing the ATP-sensitive potassium chan","E":"Meglitinides act on the beta cell KATP channel, not on intes"}},{"id":"sdl34_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the pharmacokinetics and mechanism of action of meglitinide analogs","batch":1,"stem":"A 71-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes and stage 3B chronic kidney disease is being treated with repaglinide. A nephrology consultant asks whether repaglinide is safe given her reduced kidney function. Which pharmacokinetic property makes repaglinide generally safer than sulfonylureas in this setting?","choices":{"C":"Repaglinide is metabolized by hepatic CYP3A4 and CYP2C8 enzymes and does not rely on renal excretion of active drug, so it can be used in patients with renal impairment","A":"Repaglinide is freely filtered by the glomerulus and reabsorbed in the proximal tubule at a rate proportional to remaining renal function, allowing predictable dose titration","B":"Repaglinide binds to plasma albumin with very low affinity, preventing the protein-bound drug accumulation that occurs with sulfonylureas in uremia","D":"Repaglinide undergoes intestinal glucuronidation before reaching systemic circulation, completely bypassing the need for renal or hepatic drug clearance","E":"Repaglinide stimulates insulin secretion only in response to elevated glucose concentrations, making accumulation-related hypoglycemia physiologically impossible"},"explanation":"Katzung (course reading) says repaglinide is cleared by hepatic CYP3A4 with a plasma half-life of 1 hour and can be used in patients with renal impairment and in the elderly; Katzung's biotransformation chapter also lists it as a CYP2C8 substrate. In contrast, glyburide produces active renal metabolites and chlorpropamide is primarily renally excreted, making both dangerous in CKD. A key drug interaction: Katzung lists gemfibrozil as a CYP2C8 inhibitor, so it can raise repaglinide levels.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Repaglinide = hepatic metabolism (CYP3A4 and CYP2C8) + fecal excretion = safe in renal failure. Drug interaction: gemfibrozil (CYP2C8 inhibitor) dramatically increases repaglinide levels. COMLEX: Meglitinides are taken just before each meal. Repaglinide can be used in renal failure; glyburide and chlorpropamide cannot.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 1 — Meglitinides share the KATP channel mechanism with sulfonylureas but bind a distinct site on SUR1","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C"},{"id":"sdl34_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the pharmacokinetics and mechanism of action of meglitinide analogs","batch":1,"stem":"A type 2 diabetic patient is switched from a sulfonylurea to nateglinide to address postprandial glucose excursions with minimal interprandial hypoglycemia. Compared to repaglinide, nateglinide is best characterized by which pharmacokinetic feature?","choices":{"E":"Nateglinide has a faster onset and shorter duration of insulin-stimulating action than repaglinide, providing more precise meal-linked secretion with less between-meal hypoglycemia risk","A":"Nateglinide has a longer duration of action than repaglinide, providing extended glycemic coverage and reducing the need for three-times-daily dosing","B":"Nateglinide requires dose adjustment in patients with renal impairment due to substantial accumulation of active renal metabolites in kidney disease","C":"Nateglinide produces more prolonged insulin secretion by having a higher binding affinity for the SUR1 receptor site than repaglinide","D":"Nateglinide carries a higher risk of fasting hypoglycemia compared to repaglinide because its extended half-life persists well beyond the meal window"},"explanation":"Among the meglitinides, nateglinide has the fastest onset (absorbed within about 20 minutes, peak under 1 hour) and the shortest duration of action (about 4 hours), while repaglinide peaks at about 1 hour and acts for 4–7 hours (Katzung Ch 41). This makes nateglinide more precisely meal-linked. Both drugs are taken just before a meal, and patients should skip the dose if they skip a meal to reduce hypoglycemia risk. Nateglinide is a phenylalanine derivative while repaglinide is a benzoic acid analog — both bind SUR1 at different sites from each other and from sulfonylureas. Nateglinide acts for about 4 hours versus 4–7 hours for repaglinide, so it is not the longer-acting meglitinide. Katzung states nateglinide can be used in patients with renal impairment, so renal accumulation requiring dose adjustment is not its distinguishing feature. Nateglinide gives rapid and transient insulin release lasting about 4 hours, not more prolonged secretion than repaglinide. Nateglinide has a half-life of about 1 hour and acts for about 4 hours, so it has no extended half-life persisting beyond the meal.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Nateglinide = faster, shorter than repaglinide. Both target SUR1 but at different sites than sulfonylureas. Pharmacokinetically: nateglinide is more meal-specific. COMLEX: Practical counseling point — skip meglitinide if skipping meal. This prevents hypoglycemia without adding dietary complexity.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 1 — Meglitinides share the KATP channel mechanism with sulfonylureas but bind a distinct site on SUR1","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"A":"Nateglinide acts for about 4 hours versus 4–7 hours for repa","B":"Katzung states nateglinide can be used in patients with rena","C":"Nateglinide gives rapid and transient insulin release lastin","D":"Nateglinide has a half-life of about 1 hour and acts for abo"}},{"id":"sdl34_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pharmacokinetics and mechanism of action of biguanides","batch":1,"stem":"Metformin is the first-line pharmacologic agent for type 2 diabetes. Its primary mechanism of action involves reducing hepatic glucose output. Which intracellular signaling pathway does metformin activate to achieve this effect?","choices":{"C":"Metformin increases hepatic AMP-activated protein kinase activity, and AMPK reduces hepatic gluconeogenesis and lipogenesis, lowering hepatic glucose output","A":"Metformin activates mTOR complex 1 signaling in hepatocytes, which phosphorylates and degrades gluconeogenic enzymes including phosphoenolpyruvate carboxykinase","B":"Metformin inhibits phosphodiesterase to increase hepatic cAMP levels, which in turn suppresses glucagon receptor signaling and gluconeogenic enzyme activity","D":"Metformin directly binds the insulin receptor tyrosine kinase domain, mimicking insulin signaling and activating IRS-1 downstream phosphorylation pathways","E":"Metformin inhibits pyruvate dehydrogenase kinase in hepatocyte mitochondria, shunting pyruvate toward oxidative metabolism and away from glucose synthesis"},"explanation":"Katzung (course reading) says metformin's therapeutic effects primarily derive from increasing hepatic AMP-activated protein kinase (AMPK) activity, which reduces hepatic gluconeogenesis and lipogenesis. First Aid instead gives inhibition of mitochondrial glycerol-3-phosphate dehydrogenase as the mechanism that inhibits hepatic gluconeogenesis; both sources agree the end effect is less hepatic glucose output. The net effect is reduced hepatic glucose output. Metformin also modestly increases glucose uptake in peripheral muscle and decreases intestinal glucose absorption, but the primary mechanism is hepatic gluconeogenesis suppression. It does not stimulate insulin secretion and therefore does not cause hypoglycemia as monotherapy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Metformin = complex I inhibition, AMPK activation, reduced hepatic gluconeogenesis. No hypoglycemia as monotherapy. Weight-neutral to weight-reducing. First-line for T2DM. COMLEX: Metformin contrast with insulin secretagogues on weight — sulfonylureas cause weight gain, metformin does not. Lactic acidosis in renal failure, heart failure, severe hepatic disease, hypoxic states.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 4 — Metformin counters type 2 DM insulin resistance by reducing hepatic gluconeogenesis via AMPK activation","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C"},{"id":"sdl34_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pharmacokinetics and mechanism of action of biguanides","batch":1,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman on metformin 1000 mg twice daily for 3 years presents with fatigue, glossitis, and peripheral paresthesias. Labs reveal macrocytic anemia with a low serum B12 level. Which mechanism best explains this finding in chronic metformin users?","choices":{"B":"Metformin causes small intestinal bacterial overgrowth that competitively consumes dietary vitamin B12 before it reaches the ileal absorption sites","A":"Metformin directly destroys gastric parietal cells through AMPK-mediated apoptosis, reducing intrinsic factor production and mimicking pernicious anemia","C":"Metformin impairs the calcium-dependent absorption of the vitamin B12-intrinsic factor complex in the terminal ileum, reducing B12 uptake over time","D":"Metformin increases renal clearance of vitamin B12 by inhibiting the megalin-cubilin tubular reabsorption pathway in the proximal nephron","E":"Metformin inhibits hepatic methylmalonyl-CoA mutase, the enzyme responsible for converting methylmalonic acid to succinyl-CoA using B12 as a cofactor"},"explanation":"Metformin impairs absorption of the vitamin B12-intrinsic factor complex in the distal ileum through a calcium-dependent mechanism. The ileal cubilin receptor for this complex requires calcium for normal binding, and metformin antagonizes this calcium-dependent uptake. This effect is reversible with oral calcium supplementation in many patients. Guidelines recommend periodic monitoring of serum B12 in patients on long-term metformin, especially those with symptoms of neuropathy or macrocytosis, as B12 deficiency can mimic or exacerbate diabetic neuropathy. Metformin does not destroy parietal cells; it impairs calcium-dependent ileal uptake of the B12-intrinsic factor complex. Katzung explains metformin B12 deficiency by interference with calcium-dependent absorption of the B12–intrinsic factor complex in the terminal ileum, not by bacterial overgrowth consuming B12. Metformin impairs ileal absorption of the B12-intrinsic factor complex; it is not described as increasing renal clearance through megalin-cubilin inhibition. Inhibiting methylmalonyl-CoA mutase would not lower serum B12; Katzung explains the deficiency by impaired calcium-dependent ileal absorption of the B12–intrinsic factor complex.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Metformin causes B12 deficiency via impaired ileal absorption — calcium-dependent mechanism, not parietal cell destruction. Monitor B12 periodically in long-term users. COMLEX: B12 deficiency neuropathy can be clinically indistinguishable from diabetic neuropathy. Katzung advises periodic B12 screening in long-term users, especially with neuropathy or macrocytic anemia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 4 — Metformin counters type 2 DM insulin resistance by reducing hepatic gluconeogenesis via AMPK activation","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"Metformin does not destroy parietal cells; it impairs calciu","B":"Katzung explains metformin B12 deficiency by interference wi","D":"Metformin impairs ileal absorption of the B12-intrinsic fact","E":"Inhibiting methylmalonyl-CoA mutase would not lower serum B1"}},{"id":"sdl34_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pharmacokinetics and mechanism of action of biguanides","batch":1,"stem":"A 55-year-old man with type 2 diabetes on metformin is scheduled for a CT scan with IV iodinated contrast. His physician holds metformin before the procedure and plans to restart it 48 hours later only after confirming stable renal function. Which complication is this precaution designed to prevent?","choices":{"E":"Contrast-induced acute kidney injury can cause metformin accumulation, and accumulated metformin impairs hepatic metabolism of lactic acid sufficiently to produce type B lactic acidosis","A":"Iodinated contrast directly displaces metformin from plasma protein binding sites, acutely increasing free drug levels and causing cardiovascular toxicity","B":"Metformin and iodinated contrast undergo a chemical interaction in the renal tubule that generates a nephrotoxic byproduct along with excess lactate","C":"Iodinated contrast inhibits hepatic AMPK signaling, reversing metformin's mechanism of action and causing rebound hyperglycemia after the procedure","D":"Metformin competitively blocks renal tubular secretion of iodinated contrast, leading to prolonged contrast retention and cumulative nephrotoxicity"},"explanation":"Metformin itself rarely causes lactic acidosis when renal function is normal. The danger arises when contrast nephropathy impairs renal elimination of metformin, causing drug accumulation. Katzung says that because metformin blocks gluconeogenesis it may impair hepatic metabolism of lactic acid, and that in renal insufficiency the drug accumulates and raises the risk of lactic acidosis. This type B lactic acidosis (occurring without tissue hypoxia) can be life-threatening with a mortality approaching 50%. Therefore, metformin is held before procedures using iodinated contrast and resumed a day or two later once creatinine is confirmed stable (Katzung Ch 41).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Metformin lactic acidosis = type B lactic acidosis (no tissue hypoxia). Risk factors: renal failure, heart failure, hepatic failure, alcohol use, IV contrast, major surgery, sepsis. COMLEX: Hold metformin at the time of iodinated contrast and resume it a day or two later once renal function is confirmed stable. Absolute contraindication when eGFR is below 30 mL/min.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 4 — Metformin counters type 2 DM insulin resistance by reducing hepatic gluconeogenesis via AMPK activation","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E"},{"id":"sdl34_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the pharmacokinetics and mechanism of action of thiazolidinediones","batch":1,"stem":"Pioglitazone is prescribed for a patient with type 2 diabetes and metabolic syndrome. The drug works primarily through agonism at a nuclear receptor that regulates adipogenesis and insulin sensitivity. Activation of which receptor mediates this effect?","choices":{"C":"Peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor gamma, a nuclear transcription factor controlling adipocyte differentiation, glucose transport, and lipid metabolism","A":"Liver X receptor alpha, a nuclear receptor that regulates hepatic cholesterol transport and bile acid synthesis in response to excess intracellular cholesterol","B":"Sterol regulatory element-binding protein 1c, a membrane-bound transcription factor that controls de novo lipogenesis in hepatocytes and adipose tissue","D":"Farnesoid X receptor, a bile acid-activated nuclear receptor that controls hepatic gluconeogenesis and insulin sensitivity via fibroblast growth factor signaling","E":"Glucocorticoid receptor, a ligand-activated nuclear receptor that translocates to the nucleus and promotes gluconeogenic gene expression in the liver"},"explanation":"Thiazolidinediones (TZDs) are full agonists at peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor gamma (PPAR-gamma), a nuclear receptor expressed most abundantly in adipose tissue, with lower expression in liver, muscle, and renal collecting duct. PPAR-gamma activation leads to transcription of genes involved in adipocyte differentiation, fatty acid storage, and glucose uptake (GLUT4 upregulation), collectively improving insulin sensitivity. The onset of action is delayed by several weeks because the mechanism requires gene transcription. Troglitazone, the first TZD, was withdrawn due to idiosyncratic hepatotoxicity; rosiglitazone and pioglitazone remain in use but have distinct risk profiles. Thiazolidinediones are ligands of PPAR-gamma, the nuclear receptor regulating lipid and glucose metabolism genes and adipocyte differentiation, not liver X receptor alpha. Thiazolidinediones are ligands of the nuclear receptor PPAR-gamma, not of the membrane-bound transcription factor SREBP-1c. FXR is the bile acid–activated nuclear receptor linked to colesevelam's glucose-lowering effect; thiazolidinediones are PPAR-gamma ligands. Glucocorticoid receptor activation promotes gluconeogenesis and insulin resistance, the opposite of the thiazolidinedione effect through PPAR-gamma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: TZD mechanism = PPAR-gamma agonism = insulin sensitizer (not secretagogue). Takes weeks for full effect. ADRs: weight gain, edema, heart failure (PPAR-gamma in renal collecting duct = ENaC upregulation), osteoporosis. Pioglitazone = bladder cancer risk. Rosiglitazone = increased LDL and MI risk. COMLEX: Contraindicated in NYHA III/IV heart failure.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 5 — TZDs target obesity-driven insulin resistance by activating PPAR-gamma in adipose tissue and liver","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"Thiazolidinediones are ligands of PPAR-gamma, the nuclear re","B":"Thiazolidinediones are ligands of the nuclear receptor PPAR-","D":"FXR is the bile acid–activated nuclear receptor linked to co","E":"Glucocorticoid receptor activation promotes gluconeogenesis "}},{"id":"sdl34_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the pharmacokinetics and mechanism of action of thiazolidinediones","batch":1,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes started on pioglitazone develops bilateral ankle edema and gains 4 kg after 8 weeks of therapy. An echocardiogram shows no structural heart disease. Which mechanism best explains the fluid retention caused by thiazolidinedione therapy?","choices":{"B":"PPAR-gamma activation in renal tubular cells, chiefly the collecting duct, increases sodium and water reabsorption and expands plasma volume without aldosterone","A":"Increased renin secretion from the juxtaglomerular apparatus due to pioglitazone-mediated afferent arteriolar dilation and a compensatory RAAS activation response","C":"Reduced hepatic albumin synthesis caused by PPAR-gamma-mediated transcriptional repression of the albumin gene, lowering plasma oncotic pressure","D":"Direct pioglitazone-mediated inhibition of aquaporin-2 channels in the collecting duct, paradoxically increasing free water reabsorption via an osmotic shift","E":"Activation of cardiac PPAR-gamma receptors by pioglitazone, reducing myocardial contractility and producing low-output congestive volume retention"},"explanation":"TZD-induced fluid retention results from PPAR-gamma activation in principal cells of the renal collecting duct. This upregulates expression of renal sodium transport, increasing sodium and water reabsorption independent of aldosterone. A 2013 review (Bełtowski et al., PPAR Research) notes that collecting-duct PPAR-gamma knockout blocks TZD fluid retention, but that the exact carrier is debated: ENaC was proposed first, yet deleting collecting-duct ENaC in mice did not prevent the fluid retention. The resulting volume expansion manifests as peripheral edema and weight gain. This mechanism is not related to primary cardiac dysfunction. TZDs are contraindicated in NYHA Class III and IV heart failure because volume expansion can precipitate decompensation even in patients without structural disease. Fluid retention can be partially managed with diuretics, but drug discontinuation is often required.","boardPrep":"Step 1: TZD edema = PPAR-gamma in renal collecting duct principal cells = increased renal sodium reabsorption (ENaC proposed, exact carrier debated) = sodium retention. Independent of cardiac dysfunction. Contraindicated in heart failure. COMLEX: TZD-induced edema plus bone density reduction (PPAR-gamma affects osteoblast differentiation) — increased fracture risk particularly in women.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 5 — TZDs target obesity-driven insulin resistance by activating PPAR-gamma in adipose tissue and liver","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"E":"This mechanism is not related "}},{"id":"sdl34_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the pharmacokinetics and mechanism of action of thiazolidinediones","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with type 2 diabetes has a history of bladder cancer treated with surgery 4 years ago, currently in remission. His endocrinologist is considering adding a thiazolidinedione. Which statement correctly identifies the most relevant drug-specific concern for this patient?","choices":{"A":"Pioglitazone has been associated with increased bladder cancer risk in long-term observational studies and should be avoided with active or prior bladder cancer","B":"Rosiglitazone produces a hepatotoxic metabolite that is concentrated and excreted in the bladder, causing direct urothelial mutagenesis over time","C":"Both pioglitazone and rosiglitazone equally increase bladder cancer risk because PPAR-gamma agonism promotes urothelial cell proliferation as a shared class effect","D":"Troglitazone is the only TZD with an FDA bladder cancer warning; neither pioglitazone nor rosiglitazone carry this specific adverse effect concern","E":"Pioglitazone should be avoided only in patients with currently active bladder cancer, since prior cancer in remission does not significantly alter the risk-benefit calculation"},"explanation":"Pioglitazone is the TZD linked to bladder cancer: Katzung (course reading) says bladder tumors were seen in male rats on pioglitazone, a 10-year cohort study and a large pooled analysis found no association, and another population-based study found an association with pioglitazone but not rosiglitazone. The mechanism may involve PPAR-gamma activation in urothelial cells promoting cell proliferation. Pioglitazone is contraindicated in patients with active bladder cancer and should generally be avoided in those with a history of bladder cancer. Rosiglitazone does not carry a bladder cancer warning; instead, rosiglitazone is associated with increased LDL and myocardial infarction risk. Troglitazone was withdrawn for hepatotoxicity, not bladder cancer.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Three TZDs, three different risk profiles. Troglitazone = withdrawn, hepatotoxicity. Rosiglitazone = increased LDL and myocardial infarction risk. Pioglitazone = bladder cancer risk (dose-dependent, long-term use). COMLEX: Pioglitazone can reduce MACE compared to rosiglitazone; useful in PCOS via insulin sensitization; contraindicated in heart failure and bladder cancer.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 5 — TZDs target obesity-driven insulin resistance by activating PPAR-gamma in adipose tissue and liver","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"E":"Pioglitazone is contraindicate"}},{"id":"sdl34_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the pharmacokinetics and mechanism of action of α-glucosidase inhibitors","batch":1,"stem":"A 65-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes starts acarbose and is instructed to take it at the beginning of each meal. Which mechanism explains how this drug lowers postprandial blood glucose?","choices":{"C":"Acarbose competitively inhibits brush-border alpha-glucosidases, delaying digestion of oligosaccharides and reducing postprandial monosaccharide absorption","A":"Acarbose activates GLP-1 receptors on intestinal L-cells, stimulating glucose-dependent insulin secretion before carbohydrates are fully absorbed from the gut","B":"Acarbose inhibits sodium-glucose cotransporter 1 on the apical surface of enterocytes, directly blocking monosaccharide uptake into portal circulation","D":"Acarbose stimulates pancreatic beta cell KATP channel closure via a gut-brain reflex, augmenting glucose-stimulated insulin secretion at mealtime","E":"Acarbose exclusively inhibits pancreatic alpha-amylase in the duodenal lumen, preventing starch hydrolysis while leaving brush border enzymes unaffected"},"explanation":"Alpha-glucosidase inhibitors such as acarbose and miglitol inhibit alpha-glucosidase enzymes (maltase, glucoamylase, sucrase) located on the intestinal brush border. By blocking the final step of carbohydrate digestion, these drugs delay absorption of glucose and other monosaccharides, blunting the postprandial glucose spike. Acarbose also inhibits pancreatic alpha-amylase at higher doses. The drugs must be taken at the start of each meal to be effective. As monotherapy, they do not cause hypoglycemia because they do not stimulate insulin secretion. Acarbose inhibits intestinal alpha-glucosidase and does not activate GLP-1 receptors; GLP-1 receptor agonists do that. Acarbose inhibits intestinal alpha-glucosidases, delaying digestion of starch and disaccharides, rather than blocking monosaccharide uptake through SGLT1. Acarbose acts by inhibiting intestinal alpha-glucosidases and does not cause hypoglycemia as monotherapy, unlike drugs that close beta-cell KATP channels.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Alpha-glucosidase inhibitors = delay carbohydrate digestion at brush border = reduce postprandial glucose. No hypoglycemia as monotherapy. Main ADRs: flatulence, diarrhea (fermentation of undigested carbs). COMLEX: If hypoglycemia occurs on combination therapy, treat with glucose tablets (dextrose) — NOT sucrose or starch, which are blocked.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 1 — Alpha-glucosidase inhibitors reduce postprandial hyperglycemia by slowing intestinal carbohydrate absorption","isHighYield":false,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"Acarbose inhibits intestinal alpha-glucosidase and does not ","B":"Acarbose inhibits intestinal alpha-glucosidases, delaying di","D":"Acarbose acts by inhibiting intestinal alpha-glucosidases an","E":"Acarbose also inhibits pancrea"}},{"id":"sdl34_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the pharmacokinetics and mechanism of action of α-glucosidase inhibitors","batch":1,"stem":"A patient on combination acarbose and glipizide therapy develops symptomatic hypoglycemia with a blood glucose of 55 mg/dL. Which substance should be used to correctly treat this episode?","choices":{"D":"Crackers or starch tablets, which are digested quickly enough by residual pancreatic amylase to raise blood glucose reliably even with acarbose present","A":"Orange juice, because the fructose content is rapidly absorbed via the GLUT5 transporter in the small intestine in a completely enzyme-independent manner","B":"Hard candy containing sucrose, because sucrose dissolves in saliva sufficiently quickly to raise blood glucose through a sublingual absorption pathway","C":"Pure glucose tablets or dextrose gel, because acarbose blocks digestion of sucrose and starches and only monosaccharides are absorbed without enzyme activity","E":"Intramuscular glucagon injection, because oral carbohydrate absorption is so completely blocked by acarbose that no oral glucose source can be reliably absorbed"},"explanation":"Acarbose inhibits sucrase (which breaks down sucrose to glucose and fructose) and alpha-glucosidases (which digest starch and maltose). If hypoglycemia occurs in a patient on acarbose in combination with an insulin secretagogue, the treatment must bypass these blocked enzymes. Only pure glucose (a monosaccharide) does not require digestion and is absorbed directly via SGLT-1 and GLUT2. Sucrose, starch, and complex carbohydrates will all be digested too slowly due to enzyme inhibition. Fructose from orange juice raises blood glucose slowly and unreliably. Glucose tablets or IV dextrose are the treatments of choice, not glucagon (which is reserved for severe unconscious hypoglycemia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: HY clinical pearl — treat hypoglycemia on acarbose with glucose (dextrose), NOT sucrose or starch. Sucrase is inhibited by acarbose so sucrose will not work rapidly enough. COMLEX: Same rule applies to miglitol. Miglitol is systemically absorbed (unlike acarbose) and requires renal dose adjustment; acarbose is minimally absorbed.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 1 — Alpha-glucosidase inhibitors reduce postprandial hyperglycemia by slowing intestinal carbohydrate absorption","isHighYield":false,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"B":"Sucrose, starch, and complex c","D":"Sucrose, starch, and complex c","E":"Glucose tablets or IV dextrose"}},{"id":"sdl34_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the pharmacokinetics and mechanism of action of α-glucosidase inhibitors","batch":1,"stem":"A 60-year-old man on acarbose presents after 4 weeks of therapy with bloating, flatulence, and loose stools that are disrupting his daily life. His physician explains that these symptoms arise directly from the drug's mechanism. Which process best explains his gastrointestinal symptoms?","choices":{"B":"Undigested oligosaccharides pass to the colon where they are fermented by colonic bacteria, generating gas, short-chain fatty acids, and an osmotic load that causes bloating and loose stools","A":"Acarbose stimulates intestinal motility by activating enteric GLP-1 receptors in the submucosal plexus of the small bowel wall","C":"Acarbose directly inhibits enterocyte sodium-potassium ATPase, impairing sodium-coupled fluid absorption and causing osmotic secretory diarrhea","D":"Acarbose induces bile salt malabsorption in the ileum by competing with the apical sodium-bile acid transporter, producing cholestatic diarrhea","E":"Acarbose metabolites are toxic to colonic goblet cells, reducing mucin production and triggering an inflammatory secretory diarrhea"},"explanation":"Because acarbose delays carbohydrate digestion, undigested and partially digested carbohydrates reach the colon in increased amounts. Colonic bacteria ferment these substrates, producing large quantities of gas (hydrogen, methane, carbon dioxide) causing flatulence and bloating, along with short-chain fatty acids and an osmotic load contributing to loose stools. These side effects are dose-related and often improve over time as intestinal flora adapt. The standard approach is to start at 50 mg twice daily just before meals (Katzung Ch 41) and gradually titrate upward over several weeks to minimize GI intolerance. The GI symptoms arise from undigested carbohydrate fermentation in the colon, not from acarbose activating enteric GLP-1 receptors. Acarbose acts on intestinal alpha-glucosidases and does not inhibit the enterocyte Na-K ATPase; its diarrhea is due to fermentation of undigested carbohydrate. The side effects reflect colonic fermentation of carbohydrate reaching the colon, not bile salt malabsorption from transporter competition. The symptoms are explained by bacterial fermentation of undigested carbohydrate, not by drug metabolites toxic to goblet cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GI side effects of acarbose = fermentation of undigested carbs in colon. Dose-dependent; improve over time with titration. COMLEX: Contraindicated in inflammatory bowel disease (acarbose worsens intestinal inflammation), malabsorption syndromes, and hepatic cirrhosis (acarbose can elevate liver enzymes at high doses).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 1 — Alpha-glucosidase inhibitors reduce postprandial hyperglycemia by slowing intestinal carbohydrate absorption","isHighYield":false,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"The GI symptoms arise from undigested carbohydrate fermentat","C":"Acarbose acts on intestinal alpha-glucosidases and does not ","D":"The side effects reflect colonic fermentation of carbohydrat","E":"The symptoms are explained by bacterial fermentation of undi"}},{"id":"sdl34_q16","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Understand the pharmacokinetics and mechanism of action of incretins","batch":1,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes begins exenatide therapy. Her physician explains that the drug mimics an endogenous intestinal hormone. Which of the following correctly describes the primary mechanism by which exenatide lowers blood glucose?","choices":{"E":"Exenatide activates PPAR-gamma in adipocytes and muscle cells, increasing insulin sensitivity and reducing hepatic glucose output over weeks of therapy","A":"Exenatide irreversibly inhibits the DPP-4 enzyme, preventing degradation of endogenous GLP-1 and thereby extending its half-life in the circulation","B":"Exenatide directly antagonizes glucagon receptors on hepatocytes, preventing glucagon-stimulated activation of gluconeogenic enzymes after meals","C":"Exenatide activates beta-cell GLP-1 receptors in a glucose-dependent manner, stimulating insulin secretion only when glucose is above the fasting range","D":"Exenatide inhibits SGLT-2 in the proximal convoluted tubule, reducing renal glucose reabsorption and causing glucosuria after each administered dose"},"explanation":"Exenatide is a synthetic analogue of exendin-4, a peptide from the Gila monster that shares 53% sequence homology with human GLP-1. It activates GLP-1 receptors on pancreatic beta cells to stimulate insulin secretion in a glucose-dependent fashion, meaning it does not trigger insulin secretion when plasma glucose is normal, substantially reducing hypoglycemia risk as monotherapy. Additional mechanisms include suppression of glucagon secretion, delayed gastric emptying (reducing postprandial glucose), and central appetite suppression contributing to weight loss. Exenatide does not inhibit DPP-4 — that is the mechanism of the gliptins. Exenatide suppresses glucagon secretion through GLP-1 receptor effects and does not antagonize glucagon receptors on hepatocytes. SGLT-2 inhibition is the mechanism of the gliflozins, not exenatide. Exenatide is a GLP-1 receptor agonist; PPAR-gamma activation is the thiazolidinedione mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GLP-1 agonist mechanism = GLP-1 receptor activation = glucose-dependent insulin secretion plus glucagon suppression plus delayed gastric emptying. Not DPP-4 inhibition. Weight loss. COMLEX: Exenatide is renally cleared (avoid eGFR below 30). Liraglutide and semaglutide have cardiovascular outcome benefits (LEADER, SUSTAIN-6). Black box = medullary thyroid carcinoma and MEN2 contraindication.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — GLP-1 receptor agonists mimic incretin action to amplify glucose-dependent insulin secretion","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"Exenatide does not inhibit DPP","B":"Exenatide suppresses glucagon secretion through GLP-1 recept","D":"SGLT-2 inhibition is the mechanism of the gliflozins, not ex","E":"Exenatide is a GLP-1 receptor agonist; PPAR-gamma activation"}},{"id":"sdl34_q17","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Understand the pharmacokinetics and mechanism of action of incretins","batch":1,"stem":"Liraglutide and semaglutide have demonstrated significant reductions in major adverse cardiovascular events in large outcomes trials. Beyond glycemic control, which combination of pharmacologic effects best accounts for their cardiovascular benefit?","choices":{"C":"GLP-1 receptor activation reduces body weight and visceral fat, lowers blood pressure and inflammatory markers, and has direct anti-atherosclerotic effects on the vessel wall","A":"Liraglutide and semaglutide increase HDL cholesterol by activating hepatic PPAR-alpha transcription, reducing overall atherogenic lipid burden in patients with metabolic syndrome","B":"Liraglutide and semaglutide prevent coronary thrombosis by inhibiting platelet P2Y12 ADP receptors through a GLP-1 receptor pathway in platelet membranes","D":"Liraglutide and semaglutide directly improve left ventricular ejection fraction by activating myocardial GLP-1 receptors and increasing cardiomyocyte cAMP levels","E":"Liraglutide and semaglutide inhibit hepatic glucose production by activating hepatic AMP-kinase in a manner that also reduces hepatic triglyceride synthesis"},"explanation":"The cardiovascular benefits of liraglutide (LEADER trial) and semaglutide (SUSTAIN-6 trial) arise from multiple mechanisms beyond glucose lowering. GLP-1 receptor activation promotes weight loss, reduces visceral fat, lowers systolic blood pressure, decreases postprandial lipemia, reduces inflammatory cytokines (CRP, IL-6), and has direct anti-atherosclerotic effects on endothelial cells and macrophages. The reduction is driven primarily by fewer non-fatal strokes and non-fatal MIs (atherosclerotic events) rather than heart failure events, distinguishing GLP-1 agonists from SGLT-2 inhibitors, which predominantly reduce heart failure hospitalizations. Liraglutide's outcome benefit came with weight loss and lower systolic blood pressure; PPAR-alpha activity belongs to pioglitazone, not the GLP-1 receptor agonists. GLP-1 receptor agonists are not described as acting on platelet P2Y12 receptors; liraglutide's outcome benefit came with weight loss and lower systolic blood pressure. Liraglutide lowered the composite of cardiovascular death, nonfatal MI or nonfatal stroke alongside weight loss and lower blood pressure; improved ejection fraction via myocardial GLP-1 receptors is not described. Hepatic AMP-kinase activation is the metformin mechanism; liraglutide's outcome benefit came with weight loss and lower systolic blood pressure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Liraglutide and semaglutide reduce MACE (atherosclerotic events — stroke and MI). SGLT-2 inhibitors primarily reduce heart failure. Both are guideline-recommended in T2DM with established CVD. COMLEX: GLP-1 agonists also reduce MACE in patients with established cardiovascular disease — this is now a standard-of-care indication in high-risk T2DM.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — GLP-1 receptor agonists mimic incretin action to amplify glucose-dependent insulin secretion","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"Liraglutide's outcome benefit came with weight loss and lowe","B":"GLP-1 receptor agonists are not described as acting on plate","D":"Liraglutide lowered the composite of cardiovascular death, n","E":"Hepatic AMP-kinase activation is the metformin mechanism; li"}},{"id":"sdl34_q18","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Understand the pharmacokinetics and mechanism of action of incretins","batch":1,"stem":"A 50-year-old man with type 2 diabetes, obesity, and a first-degree relative who was treated for medullary thyroid carcinoma is being considered for GLP-1 receptor agonist therapy for weight management. Which statement correctly identifies the relevant risk?","choices":{"A":"GLP-1 receptor agonists are contraindicated with a personal or family history of medullary thyroid carcinoma or MEN 2, based on rodent C-cell hyperplasia and tumors","B":"GLP-1 receptor agonists are safe in this patient because only a personal diagnosis of medullary thyroid carcinoma — and not a family history — constitutes an FDA contraindication","C":"GLP-1 receptor agonists are contraindicated only when active thyroid nodules have been confirmed by fine-needle aspiration biopsy and cytologic analysis","D":"GLP-1 receptor agonists are contraindicated in all thyroid conditions because they nonspecifically increase TSH secretion through a pituitary GLP-1 receptor pathway","E":"GLP-1 receptor agonists are contraindicated only when combined with DPP-4 inhibitors, since the combination amplifies C-cell GLP-1 receptor stimulation synergistically"},"explanation":"Rodent studies showed that GLP-1 receptor agonists cause dose- and duration-dependent C-cell hyperplasia and medullary thyroid carcinoma, because rat C-cells express higher densities of GLP-1 receptors than human C-cells. The FDA placed a black box warning on all GLP-1 receptor agonists contraindicating their use in patients with a personal or family history of medullary thyroid carcinoma (MTC) or MEN type 2A or 2B. A family history of MEN2 raises concern because MEN2 is autosomal dominant (RET proto-oncogene mutation) and predisposes to MTC. Although human causality has not been established, the precautionary contraindication applies regardless of whether cancer is currently active. The thyroid restriction covers only a personal or family history of medullary thyroid cancer or MEN 2, based on rodent C-cell tumors, not all thyroid conditions. The contraindication applies with a personal or family history of MTC or MEN2 regardless of DPP-4 inhibitor use, based on rodent C-cell hyperplasia and tumors.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GLP-1 agonist black box warning = medullary thyroid carcinoma and MEN2 (personal OR family history). Mechanism = C-cell GLP-1 receptor stimulation (rodents). Also: pancreatitis risk (hold if lipase elevated). COMLEX: Calcitonin is the tumor marker for MTC. RET mutation in MEN2. Family history of MEN2 = contraindication to GLP-1 agonists.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — GLP-1 receptor agonists mimic incretin action to amplify glucose-dependent insulin secretion","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"C":"Although human causality has n","D":"The thyroid restriction covers only a personal or family his","E":"The contraindication applies with a personal or family histo"}},{"id":"sdl34_q19","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Understand the pharmacokinetics and mechanism of action of DPP-4 inhibitors","batch":1,"stem":"Sitagliptin inhibits DPP-4 to prevent endogenous GLP-1 degradation. How does this mechanism differ from that of GLP-1 receptor agonists such as liraglutide in terms of the resulting pharmacologic effect?","choices":{"B":"Sitagliptin raises endogenous GLP-1 only to about physiologic levels rather than supraphysiologic ones, so it lowers glucose more modestly and does not cause weight loss","A":"Sitagliptin directly activates GLP-1 receptors on beta cells, providing an equivalent incretin response to endogenous GLP-1 without requiring ligand competition at the receptor","C":"Sitagliptin produces significant weight loss and reduction in cardiovascular events through the same mechanism as liraglutide, making the two drug classes therapeutically equivalent","D":"Sitagliptin inhibits DPP-4 only within the pancreas, while liraglutide acts at GLP-1 receptors throughout the body including in the brain, gut, and vasculature","E":"Sitagliptin requires hepatic activation to become an active DPP-4 inhibitor, while liraglutide is administered as an already-active drug that directly binds the GLP-1 receptor"},"explanation":"DPP-4 inhibitors such as sitagliptin prevent the rapid enzymatic degradation of endogenous GLP-1 and GIP, whose native half-life is only about 2.5 minutes (Marks Ch 41), so endogenous incretin levels rise but stay in the physiologic range. GLP-1 receptor agonists such as liraglutide produce supraphysiologic GLP-1 receptor stimulation, resulting in greater HbA1c reduction (semaglutide 1.5–1.8% vs. sitagliptin 0.5–1.0%, Katzung Ch 41), significant weight loss, and cardiovascular outcome benefits not seen with DPP-4 inhibitors. Both classes stimulate insulin glucose-dependently and do not cause hypoglycemia as monotherapy. Sitagliptin does not bind GLP-1 receptors; it inhibits DPP-4 to prolong the action of endogenously released GLP-1 and GIP, whereas liraglutide is the receptor agonist. Sitagliptin is given as the active drug: hepatic metabolism is limited, its metabolites have insignificant activity, and it is mainly excreted unchanged in urine.","boardPrep":"Step 1: DPP-4 inhibitors = weight-neutral, modest HbA1c reduction. GLP-1 agonists = weight loss, greater HbA1c reduction, MACE reduction. Key difference = physiologic vs. supraphysiologic GLP-1 receptor stimulation. COMLEX: Sitagliptin is well-tolerated in elderly patients; weight-neutral profile is useful when weight gain from sulfonylureas is a concern.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — DPP-4 inhibitors preserve endogenous GLP-1 to sustain glucose-dependent insulin secretion","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"Sitagliptin does not bind GLP-1 receptors; it inhibits DPP-4","E":"Sitagliptin is given as the active drug: hepatic metabolism "}},{"id":"sdl34_q20","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Understand the pharmacokinetics and mechanism of action of DPP-4 inhibitors","batch":1,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with type 2 diabetes and end-stage renal disease on hemodialysis requires a DPP-4 inhibitor. Which agent is preferred because it relies on biliary and intestinal excretion rather than renal elimination and requires no dose adjustment?","choices":{"D":"Linagliptin, which is excreted mainly via the enterohepatic system in the feces and requires no dose adjustment at any level of renal impairment","A":"Sitagliptin, which is predominantly renally excreted but is approved for dialysis patients with a substantial dose reduction from 100 mg to 25 mg daily","B":"Saxagliptin, which has a large volume of distribution that prevents meaningful drug accumulation even in the complete absence of renal clearance","C":"Alogliptin, which undergoes hepatic glucuronidation to inactive products that are efficiently removed from the circulation during hemodialysis sessions","E":"Vildagliptin, which is hydrolyzed in plasma to inactive metabolites that do not accumulate to toxic levels despite their reliance on renal excretion"},"explanation":"Among the DPP-4 inhibitors, linagliptin is unique in that it is primarily excreted via the bile (Katzung Ch 41). This makes it the only DPP-4 inhibitor that does not require any dose adjustment in renal impairment, including in dialysis patients. Sitagliptin, saxagliptin, alogliptin, and vildagliptin all require dose reductions as kidney function falls (sitagliptin 50 mg at eGFR 30–50 and 25 mg below 30). This pharmacokinetic advantage makes linagliptin the preferred DPP-4 inhibitor in patients with advanced CKD, although all DPP-4 inhibitors are generally well-tolerated in this population.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Linagliptin = bile and fecal excretion = no renal dose adjustment. All other gliptins require dose reduction in CKD. Mnemonic: LINAgliptin = LINes the gut. COMLEX: DPP-4 inhibitors are generally safe in elderly patients with CKD. Saxagliptin increased heart failure hospitalizations in SAVOR-TIMI 53 trial — important for patients with concurrent cardiac disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — DPP-4 inhibitors preserve endogenous GLP-1 to sustain glucose-dependent insulin secretion","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"B":"Sitagliptin, saxagliptin, alog","C":"Sitagliptin, saxagliptin, alog","E":"Sitagliptin, saxagliptin, alog"}},{"id":"sdl34_q21","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Understand the pharmacokinetics and mechanism of action of DPP-4 inhibitors","batch":1,"stem":"A 67-year-old man with type 2 diabetes develops severe joint pain involving the knees and hips 6 months after starting sitagliptin. The pain does not improve with NSAIDs or physical therapy. Which class-specific adverse effect of DPP-4 inhibitors should be considered?","choices":{"A":"Disabling arthralgia is a recognized class effect of DPP-4 inhibitors that can appear months to years after starting and usually resolves when the drug is stopped","B":"Sitagliptin inhibits matrix metalloproteinases in cartilage, causing progressive chondrocyte death and structural osteoarthritis in weight-bearing joints over time","C":"Sitagliptin increases serum urate levels by reducing renal uric acid excretion in the proximal tubule, causing urate crystal deposition and a gout-like arthritis","D":"DPP-4 inhibition promotes accumulation of substance P in synovial tissue, activating joint pain receptors and producing a neurogenic inflammatory arthritis syndrome","E":"Sitagliptin activates synovial T-lymphocytes by inhibiting a DPP-4 homolog involved in T-cell co-stimulation, triggering an autoimmune inflammatory arthritis"},"explanation":"The FDA has warned of severe and potentially disabling arthralgia associated with all DPP-4 inhibitors. This adverse effect appears to be immune-mediated and can develop at any point during therapy, from weeks to years after initiation. The mechanism is incompletely understood but may relate to DPP-4's role in processing neuropeptides and immune regulatory peptides beyond the incretin pathway. Importantly, symptoms resolve after the drug is stopped. Clinicians should consider DPP-4 inhibitor-induced arthralgia in diabetic patients who develop unexplained joint pain, as this can be mistaken for osteoarthritis, gout, or inflammatory arthropathy. The FDA warning is of DPP-4 inhibitor joint pain that resolves after the drug is stopped, not progressive structural osteoarthritis from cartilage metalloproteinase inhibition. DPP-4 inhibitor arthralgia is not attributed to raised serum urate or gout; it is a class effect that resolves when the drug is stopped.","boardPrep":"Step 1: DPP-4 inhibitor class adverse effects: severe arthralgia (FDA warning, resolves with discontinuation), nasopharyngitis, urticaria, angioedema, rare pancreatitis. COMLEX: Unexplained disabling joint pain in a patient on a gliptin = consider stopping the drug before extensive rheumatologic workup. Arthralgia resolves after discontinuation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — DPP-4 inhibitors preserve endogenous GLP-1 to sustain glucose-dependent insulin secretion","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"The FDA warning is of DPP-4 inhibitor joint pain that resolv","C":"DPP-4 inhibitor arthralgia is not attributed to raised serum"}},{"id":"sdl34_q22","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Understand the pharmacokinetics and mechanism of action of SGLT-2 inhibitors","batch":1,"stem":"A 60-year-old man with type 2 diabetes is started on empagliflozin. He asks how this medication lowers his blood glucose. Which mechanism best explains empagliflozin's primary glucose-lowering effect?","choices":{"D":"Empagliflozin inhibits SGLT-2 in the proximal tubule, reducing renal glucose reabsorption and causing glucosuria regardless of circulating insulin","A":"Empagliflozin activates hepatic AMP-activated protein kinase to suppress gluconeogenesis and reduce fasting hepatic glucose output after each dose","B":"Empagliflozin inhibits the pancreatic KATP channel on beta cells, augmenting glucose-dependent insulin secretion in a mealtime-synchronized fashion","C":"Empagliflozin binds PPAR-gamma in adipocytes and skeletal muscle cells, increasing insulin receptor expression and peripheral glucose uptake over weeks","E":"Empagliflozin inhibits intestinal alpha-glucosidase enzymes to delay carbohydrate digestion and reduce the postprandial glucose load at each meal"},"explanation":"SGLT-2 is located in the S1 segment of the proximal convoluted tubule and is responsible for reabsorbing approximately 90% of the glucose filtered at the glomerulus. SGLT-2 inhibitors block this transporter, causing glucosuria even at normal plasma glucose levels. This lowers blood glucose in an insulin-independent manner and also produces osmotic diuresis (lowering blood pressure) and mild natriuresis. Katzung notes they lower the glycosuria threshold from about 180 to about 40 mg/dL and lower HbA1c by 0.5–1%. The mechanism is entirely independent of beta cell function or insulin secretion. Empagliflozin acts by inhibiting SGLT-2 in the proximal tubule, not by activating hepatic AMPK; that is the metformin mechanism. Empagliflozin lowers glucose by inhibiting renal SGLT2 to cause glycosuria, not through beta-cell KATP channels. PPAR-gamma activation is the thiazolidinedione mechanism, not that of empagliflozin. Alpha-glucosidase inhibition is the acarbose mechanism; empagliflozin works on renal SGLT-2.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SGLT-2 = proximal tubule, handles 90% of filtered glucose reabsorption. SGLT-2 inhibitors = glucosuria, osmotic diuresis, BP reduction, weight loss. ADRs: UTI, genital mycotic infections, euglycemic DKA, Fournier gangrene, canagliflozin = lower limb amputation risk. COMLEX: Osmotic diuresis reduces preload and afterload — mechanism of benefit in heart failure.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 6 — SGLT2 inhibitors reduce renal glucose reabsorption and confer renal and cardiovascular protection in type 2 DM","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"Empagliflozin acts by inhibiting SGLT-2 in the proximal tubu","B":"Empagliflozin lowers glucose by inhibiting renal SGLT2 to ca","C":"PPAR-gamma activation is the thiazolidinedione mechanism, no","E":"Alpha-glucosidase inhibition is the acarbose mechanism; empa"}},{"id":"sdl34_q23","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Understand the pharmacokinetics and mechanism of action of SGLT-2 inhibitors","batch":1,"stem":"A 55-year-old man with type 2 diabetes on dapagliflozin presents with a 3-day history of severe perineal pain, fever, and erythema of the scrotum. Examination reveals necrotic tissue involving the perineum and genitalia. Which SGLT-2 inhibitor-associated adverse effect best explains this presentation?","choices":{"C":"Bacterial prostatitis caused by ascending urinary tract infection from the high-glucose urinary environment created by ongoing SGLT-2 inhibition","A":"Peritoneal abscess formation due to SGLT-2-mediated mucosal immune suppression allowing translocation of enteric bacteria into the peritoneal cavity","B":"Invasive perineal candidiasis progressing from superficial genital mycotic infection caused by systemic immunosuppression induced by dapagliflozin","D":"Fournier gangrene, a necrotizing fasciitis of the perineum and genitalia associated with the glucosuric environment created by SGLT-2 inhibitor use","E":"Scrotal hematoma with secondary bacterial superinfection caused by SGLT-2 inhibitor-mediated platelet dysfunction and impaired local wound healing"},"explanation":"Fournier gangrene is a rare but life-threatening polymicrobial necrotizing fasciitis of the external genitalia, perineum, and perianal area. The FDA has warned that SGLT-2 inhibitors increase the risk of Fournier gangrene, likely because glucosuria creates a high-glucose, low-pH environment in the perineal region that promotes rapid growth of mixed aerobic and anaerobic bacteria. Cases have been reported in both men and women. Management requires urgent surgical debridement, broad-spectrum antibiotics, and immediate discontinuation of the SGLT-2 inhibitor. The condition carries significant morbidity and mortality. Necrotic perineal and genital tissue fits Fournier gangrene, the necrotizing fasciitis of the perineum reported with SGLT2 inhibitors, not a peritoneal abscess. SGLT2 inhibitors do cause genital mycotic infections, but necrotic perineal tissue with fever is Fournier gangrene, the reported necrotizing fasciitis of the perineum. Prostatitis would not cause necrotic tissue of the perineum and genitalia; the FDA-warned SGLT-2 inhibitor complication with this picture is Fournier gangrene. Platelet dysfunction is not a described SGLT2 inhibitor effect; the reported perineal complication is necrotizing fasciitis (Fournier gangrene).","boardPrep":"Step 1: SGLT-2 inhibitor ADRs: UTI, genital mycotic infections (most common), euglycemic DKA, Fournier gangrene (FDA warning), lower limb amputations (canagliflozin specifically). COMLEX: Glucosuria creates yeast- and bacteria-favorable environment in perineum. Counsel patients on genital hygiene. Fournier gangrene = surgical emergency requiring debridement.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 6 — SGLT2 inhibitors reduce renal glucose reabsorption and confer renal and cardiovascular protection in type 2 DM","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"Necrotic perineal and genital tissue fits Fournier gangrene,","B":"SGLT2 inhibitors do cause genital mycotic infections, but ne","C":"Prostatitis would not cause necrotic tissue of the perineum ","E":"Platelet dysfunction is not a described SGLT2 inhibitor effe"}},{"id":"sdl34_q24","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Understand the pharmacokinetics and mechanism of action of SGLT-2 inhibitors","batch":1,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with type 2 diabetes, diabetic nephropathy, and an eGFR of 28 mL/min is followed by nephrology. His cardiologist recommends continuing his SGLT-2 inhibitor despite limited glycemic benefit at this GFR. What is the primary rationale for maintaining this therapy?","choices":{"A":"SGLT-2 inhibitors slow diabetic nephropathy by reducing intraglomerular hyperfiltration through tubuloglomerular feedback, independent of glucose lowering","B":"SGLT-2 inhibitors undergo renal activation in the proximal tubule to generate an active anti-fibrotic metabolite with direct mesangial cell protective effects","C":"SGLT-2 inhibitors provide glycemic benefit in severe renal impairment by compensatorily upregulating SGLT-1 in the intestine to increase glucose excretion","D":"SGLT-2 inhibitors lower urinary uric acid excretion in the collecting duct, reducing tubular urate crystal deposition and slowing tubular injury in diabetic nephropathy","E":"SGLT-2 inhibitors prevent uremic complications by reducing the nitrogen load from protein catabolism that results from uncontrolled gluconeogenesis"},"explanation":"SGLT-2 inhibitors have demonstrated robust renoprotective effects in the CREDENCE (canagliflozin) and DAPA-CKD (dapagliflozin) trials that are independent of and exceed their glucose-lowering effects. The primary mechanism is reduction of glomerular hyperfiltration: by blocking SGLT-2, sodium delivery to the macula densa increases, activating tubuloglomerular feedback and causing afferent arteriolar constriction, which reduces intraglomerular pressure. Costanzo (renal physiology) gives this as a clinical application of tubuloglomerular feedback: SGLT2 inhibitors increase Na+ delivery to the macula densa, constricting nearby afferent arterioles and offsetting diabetic hyperfiltration. This reduces albuminuria and slows eGFR decline. Katzung does not recommend them for glycemic control below an eGFR of about 45 mL/min, because glycemic benefit becomes small (insufficient filtered glucose), but the renal and cardiovascular protective effects persist. Current guidelines endorse SGLT-2 inhibitors in CKD with albuminuria regardless of glycemic status. SGLT2 glycemic efficacy falls in chronic kidney disease, and dapagliflozin is kept at low eGFR for its renal benefits, not for glucose excretion through intestinal SGLT1.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SGLT-2 inhibitors = renoprotection via tubuloglomerular feedback and reduced glomerular hyperfiltration. CREDENCE and DAPA-CKD trials showed benefit below the glycemic threshold. COMLEX: SGLT-2 inhibitors plus ACE inhibitors provide complementary renal protection in diabetic nephropathy. Glycemic use is not recommended below an eGFR of 45 (contraindicated below 30 for that purpose); dapagliflozin renal benefit was seen down to an eGFR of 25.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 6 — SGLT2 inhibitors reduce renal glucose reabsorption and confer renal and cardiovascular protection in type 2 DM","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"C":"SGLT2 glycemic efficacy falls in chronic kidney disease, and"}},{"id":"sdl34_q25","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the pharmacokinetics and mechanism of action of sulfonylureas","batch":2,"stem":"A 23-year-old man has had type 1 diabetes for 9 years, and his serum C-peptide is undetectable. Tired of injections, he asks whether glipizide tablets could replace his insulin. Which of the following best explains his physician's answer?","choices":{"D":"Glipizide could not replace insulin, because the kidneys clear it unchanged within minutes, so its effect never lasts between meals","A":"Glipizide could replace insulin, because it lowers glucose mainly by activating AMP-kinase and suppressing hepatic gluconeogenesis","B":"Glipizide could replace insulin, because it binds insulin receptors on muscle and fat directly and reproduces the action of insulin","C":"Glipizide could not replace insulin, because gastric acid destroys it, so it must be injected like insulin to have any effect at all","E":"Glipizide could not replace insulin, because it closes beta-cell potassium channels to release the stored insulin he no longer makes"},"explanation":"Sulfonylureas are insulin secretagogues. They bind the high-affinity sulfonylurea receptor associated with the ATP-sensitive potassium channel of the beta cell; this inhibits potassium efflux and depolarizes the cell, voltage-gated calcium channels open, and calcium influx releases preformed insulin. The drug therefore needs functioning beta cells. An undetectable C-peptide, which is secreted with endogenous insulin, shows that he has essentially none, so type 1 diabetes is treated with insulin replacement. Activation of AMP-activated protein kinase with reduced hepatic gluconeogenesis is the action of metformin, not of a sulfonylurea, and sulfonylureas do not act on insulin receptors. Glipizide is given by mouth, and at least 90% of it is metabolized in the liver to inactive products, with a half-life of 2 to 4 hours; it is not destroyed in the stomach and is not cleared unchanged within minutes by the kidney.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sulfonylureas and meglitinides close the beta-cell K-ATP channel and release stored insulin, so they need functioning beta cells and have no role in type 1 diabetes; their most common adverse effects are hypoglycemia and weight gain. COMLEX: Type 1 DM requires insulin replacement; oral secretagogues are used in type 2 DM, where beta cells still secrete insulin.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 1 — Cellular mechanism of glucose-stimulated insulin secretion via KATP channel and calcium influx in beta cells","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"B":"Activation of AMP-activated pr","C":"Glipizide is given by mouth, a"}},{"id":"sdl34_q26","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the pharmacokinetics and mechanism of action of sulfonylureas","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old man with type 2 diabetes presents for routine follow-up. He has been taking chlorpropamide for 12 years. His labs show serum sodium 128 mEq/L, urine osmolality 520 mOsm/kg, and urine sodium 45 mEq/L. He is asymptomatic and on no other medications. Which mechanism best explains his laboratory findings?","choices":{"E":"Chlorpropamide potentiates the action of ADH on the renal collecting duct, increasing aquaporin-mediated water reabsorption that dilutes serum sodium","A":"Chlorpropamide directly inhibits renal prostaglandin synthesis in the loop of Henle, reducing the prostaglandin-mediated diluting capacity and causing water retention","B":"Chlorpropamide causes nephrogenic diabetes insipidus as a recognized class effect shared by all sulfonylureas, but the patient is compensating with excessive water intake","C":"Chlorpropamide stimulates adrenal aldosterone release through an ACTH-independent mechanism, causing preferential sodium wasting into the urine","D":"Chlorpropamide directly activates tubular Na-K-ATPase in the collecting duct, redistributing sodium from the bloodstream into the urinary filtrate"},"explanation":"Chlorpropamide potentiates the effect of antidiuretic hormone (ADH/vasopressin) on the renal collecting duct, increasing aquaporin-2 insertion and promoting free water reabsorption beyond what is physiologically appropriate. The 2014 European hyponatraemia guideline (ESE, ESICM and ERBP) lists chlorpropamide among the drugs that cause SIAD and notes that such drugs can increase vasopressin release or its effects in the collecting duct. This leads to the syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone secretion (SIADH): dilutional hyponatremia, concentrated urine (high urine osmolality), and elevated urine sodium in the context of low serum sodium. Chlorpropamide is the sulfonylurea classically linked to this effect. The laboratory pattern shown (euvolemic hyponatremia with concentrated urine and high urine sodium) is the classic SIADH profile. Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus produces large volumes of dilute urine (osmolality usually <300 mOsm/kg), not hyponatremia with a urine osmolality of 520. Aldosterone increases sodium reabsorption by principal cells, so it would retain rather than waste urinary sodium. The collecting-duct Na+-K+ ATPase sits in the basolateral membrane and extrudes reabsorbed sodium from the cell toward blood, not into the filtrate.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Chlorpropamide causes SIADH by potentiating ADH on the collecting duct. Classic SIADH labs: low serum sodium, high urine osmolality, high urine sodium. Chlorpropamide is the sulfonylurea classically linked to SIADH. COMLEX: SIADH versus diabetes insipidus differential — know the urine osmolality direction for each. Chlorpropamide SIADH is a named pharmacologic cause.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 1 — Cellular mechanism of glucose-stimulated insulin secretion via KATP channel and calcium influx in beta cells","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"B":"Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus produces large volumes of dil","C":"Aldosterone increases sodium reabsorption by principal cells","D":"The collecting-duct Na+-K+ ATPase sits in the basolateral me"}},{"id":"sdl34_q27","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the pharmacokinetics and mechanism of action of sulfonylureas","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with type 2 diabetes is controlled on glipizide 10 mg daily with an HbA1c of 7.1%. He is started on fluconazole for an oral fungal infection. Two days later, he presents with diaphoresis, tremor, and a blood glucose of 46 mg/dL. Which pharmacokinetic interaction best explains this event?","choices":{"C":"Fluconazole inhibits CYP2C9-mediated hydroxylation of glipizide, raising glipizide levels and prolonging its insulin-stimulating effect","A":"Fluconazole activates UDP-glucuronosyltransferase, increasing glipizide conjugation to an active glucuronide that accumulates and stimulates insulin secretion","B":"Fluconazole displaces glipizide from KATP binding sites on the SUR1 subunit, noncompetitively augmenting the insulin-releasing effect of the drug","D":"Fluconazole reduces intestinal glucose absorption by inhibiting both SGLT-1 and alpha-glucosidase enzymes in the brush border membrane","E":"Fluconazole increases glipizide binding to plasma albumin through a hydrophobic interaction, reducing its hepatic first-pass clearance on subsequent doses"},"explanation":"Glipizide is primarily metabolized by CYP2C9 via hepatic hydroxylation to inactive metabolites. Fluconazole is a potent inhibitor of CYP2C9, which substantially reduces glipizide clearance, leading to accumulation of the parent drug. The resulting elevated glipizide plasma concentration causes excessive KATP channel blockade and sustained insulin secretion, producing symptomatic hypoglycemia. This is a classic and clinically important drug-drug interaction. Other CYP2C9 inhibitors that can potentiate sulfonylurea effects include amiodarone, certain antibiotics (fluconazole, miconazole), and some NSAIDs. Oral azole antifungals inhibit sulfonylurea metabolism, and glipizide is metabolized in the liver to inactive products, so no active glucuronide accumulates. Oral azole antifungals prolong sulfonylurea hypoglycemia by inhibiting their metabolism, not by displacing the drug from SUR1. The hypoglycemia comes from azole inhibition of sulfonylurea metabolism, not from fluconazole blocking SGLT1 or alpha-glucosidase. Azoles prolong sulfonylurea action by inhibiting their hepatic metabolism, not by increasing glipizide albumin binding.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Glipizide is metabolized by CYP2C9. Fluconazole inhibits CYP2C9. Result: glipizide accumulation and hypoglycemia. Classic drug-drug interaction. COMLEX: Sulfonylurea potentiators via CYP inhibition include azole antifungals, clarithromycin, amiodarone. Always review for interactions when starting antifungals in patients on sulfonylureas.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 1 — Cellular mechanism of glucose-stimulated insulin secretion via KATP channel and calcium influx in beta cells","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"Oral azole antifungals inhibit sulfonylurea metabolism, and ","B":"Oral azole antifungals prolong sulfonylurea hypoglycemia by ","D":"The hypoglycemia comes from azole inhibition of sulfonylurea","E":"Azoles prolong sulfonylurea action by inhibiting their hepat"}},{"id":"sdl34_q28","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the pharmacokinetics and mechanism of action of meglitinide analogs","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old man with type 2 diabetes had a documented severe allergic reaction to a sulfonylurea. His fasting glucose is controlled on basal insulin, but his postprandial glucose remains high, and his physician plans to add repaglinide before meals. Which property of repaglinide makes it a reasonable choice for him?","choices":{"A":"Its structure contains no sulfur, so it may be used in patients with a severe sulfur or sulfonylurea allergy","B":"It closes beta-cell KATP channels through a binding site that has nothing in common with the sulfonylurea site","C":"It lowers glucose without stimulating insulin release, so it carries no risk of hypoglycemia when used","D":"Its 24-hour duration of action allows once-daily dosing that does not depend on the timing of meals","E":"It is excreted unchanged by the kidneys and so avoids the hepatic metabolism that sulfonylureas need"},"explanation":"Repaglinide has no sulfur in its structure, so it may be used in patients with type 2 diabetes who have a severe sulfur or sulfonylurea allergy. Meglitinides regulate potassium efflux through the beta-cell KATP channel and overlap with the sulfonylureas at their molecular site: they share two binding sites with the sulfonylureas and have one unique site. Repaglinide is an insulin secretagogue, so hypoglycemia is a risk if the meal is delayed or skipped or contains too little carbohydrate. It acts quickly, with peak effect in about 1 hour and a duration of 4 to 7 hours, so it is taken just before each meal to control postprandial glucose. It is cleared by hepatic CYP3A4 with a plasma half-life of about 1 hour, which is why it can be used in renal impairment.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Meglitinides (repaglinide) and the D-phenylalanine derivative nateglinide close beta-cell KATP channels like sulfonylureas but with fast onset and short duration; they are taken just before meals to blunt postprandial glucose. Repaglinide contains no sulfur. COMLEX: Hypoglycemia is the main adverse effect, especially when a meal is delayed or skipped after the dose.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 1 — Cellular mechanism of glucose-stimulated insulin secretion via KATP-SUR1 blockade — meglitinides use distinct binding site on SUR1 subunit","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"D":"It acts quickly, with peak eff","E":"It is cleared by hepatic CYP3A"}},{"id":"sdl34_q29","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the pharmacokinetics and mechanism of action of meglitinide analogs","batch":2,"stem":"A 59-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes is on repaglinide 1 mg before each meal. She is later prescribed gemfibrozil for hypertriglyceridemia. One week after starting gemfibrozil, her postprandial glucose drops to 48 mg/dL. Which drug interaction best explains this adverse outcome?","choices":{"C":"Gemfibrozil displaces repaglinide from albumin binding sites, increasing the free drug fraction in plasma and augmenting insulin-stimulating activity at beta cells","A":"Gemfibrozil inhibits intestinal P-glycoprotein, reducing repaglinide first-pass elimination from enterocytes and increasing oral bioavailability substantially","B":"Gemfibrozil inhibits aldehyde dehydrogenase, potentiating the autonomic adrenergic response to hypoglycemia by causing acetaldehyde accumulation","D":"Gemfibrozil inhibits CYP2C8 and the hepatic uptake transporter OATP1B1, dramatically increasing repaglinide plasma exposure and prolonging its hypoglycemic effect","E":"Gemfibrozil inhibits gastric motility by antagonizing bile acid receptors in the stomach, prolonging repaglinide absorption and delaying its peak effect"},"explanation":"Gemfibrozil is a potent inhibitor of both CYP2C8 (the primary enzyme responsible for repaglinide oxidative metabolism) and OATP1B1 (a hepatic uptake transporter that helps clear repaglinide from portal blood). Together, these inhibitory effects raise repaglinide exposure many-fold, dramatically prolonging and intensifying its insulin-stimulating effect. This interaction has caused severe, prolonged hypoglycemia and is considered a contraindicated combination. When a fibrate is needed in a patient on repaglinide, fenofibrate (a weaker CYP2C8 inhibitor) is substantially safer. Gemfibrozil raises repaglinide exposure by inhibiting CYP2C8 and OATP1B1, not by inhibiting P-glycoprotein. Gemfibrozil is a CYP2C8 inhibitor and repaglinide a CYP2C8 substrate; the disulfiram-like reaction belongs to chlorpropamide with alcohol, not gemfibrozil. The interaction is gemfibrozil's inhibition of CYP2C8 and OATP1B1, which raises repaglinide exposure, not displacement from albumin. Gemfibrozil does not antagonize bile acid receptors to slow absorption; it inhibits CYP2C8 and OATP1B1.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Gemfibrozil plus repaglinide = major contraindicated drug interaction. Gemfibrozil inhibits CYP2C8 and OATP1B1, causing a many-fold increase in repaglinide exposure. COMLEX: When a fibrate is needed with a meglitinide, use fenofibrate instead of gemfibrozil. Drug interaction testing is a high-yield COMLEX pharmacology category.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 1 — Cellular mechanism of glucose-stimulated insulin secretion via KATP-SUR1 blockade — meglitinides use distinct binding site on SUR1 subunit","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"Gemfibrozil raises repaglinide exposure by inhibiting CYP2C8","B":"Gemfibrozil is a CYP2C8 inhibitor and repaglinide a CYP2C8 s","C":"The interaction is gemfibrozil's inhibition of CYP2C8 and OA","E":"Gemfibrozil does not antagonize bile acid receptors to slow "}},{"id":"sdl34_q30","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the pharmacokinetics and mechanism of action of meglitinide analogs","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man with type 2 diabetes takes repaglinide 1 mg three times daily. On workdays he often misses lunch but still takes his midday tablet, and on those afternoons he becomes shaky and sweaty, with a fingerstick glucose of 54 mg/dL. Which instruction would best prevent these episodes?","choices":{"D":"Take each dose just before a meal and omit it when that meal is skipped, since each dose drives insulin release for hours","A":"Take all three daily doses together with breakfast, so that their action is spread evenly across the whole working day","B":"Take each dose about 2 hours after eating, so that the insulin it releases arrives when glucose absorption is at its peak","C":"Move the midday dose on workdays to bedtime, so that it covers the long overnight fast instead of the missed lunch period","E":"Switch to glyburide once each morning, because a longer-acting secretagogue gives steadier insulin levels and fewer lows"},"explanation":"Repaglinide has a fast onset, with peak concentration and peak effect about 1 hour after ingestion and a duration of action of 4 to 7 hours. Because of its rapid onset it is used to control postprandial glucose excursions and is taken just before each meal, and Katzung warns that hypoglycemia is a risk if the meal is delayed or skipped or contains too little carbohydrate. Each tablet stimulates insulin release whether or not food arrives, so the dose should go with the meal and be left out when the meal is skipped. Taking the three doses together at breakfast would give one large insulin surge lasting 4 to 7 hours, risking morning hypoglycemia and leaving later meals uncovered. Taking it 2 hours after a meal mismatches its rapid onset with meal absorption. A bedtime dose would release insulin during the overnight fast and invite nocturnal hypoglycemia. Glyburide has metabolites with hypoglycemic activity and a biologic effect that persists 24 hours after one morning dose; it carries a higher hypoglycemia risk, especially in older patients.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Meglitinides (repaglinide, nateglinide) bind the beta-cell K-ATP channel at a site that overlaps the sulfonylurea site, act quickly and briefly, and are taken just before meals; skipping the meal but taking the dose causes hypoglycemia. COMLEX: Counsel 'one meal, one dose'; repaglinide can be used in renal impairment and in the elderly, and it contains no sulfur.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 1 — Cellular mechanism of glucose-stimulated insulin secretion via KATP-SUR1 blockade — meglitinides use distinct binding site on SUR1 subunit","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"B":"Taking it 2 hours after a meal","E":"Glyburide has metabolites with"}},{"id":"sdl34_q31","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pharmacokinetics and mechanism of action of biguanides","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes takes metformin 1000 mg twice daily as her only glucose-lowering drug. She plans a 24-hour religious fast and asks whether her medication puts her at high risk of hypoglycemia. Her physician tells her that the risk is low. Which of the following best explains this answer?","choices":{"A":"Metformin lowers glucose mainly by curbing hepatic glucose output and does not make the beta cells secrete insulin","B":"Metformin stimulates glucagon release from alpha cells during a fast, and the glucagon keeps her glucose in range","C":"Metformin is metabolized by the liver within an hour of each dose, so that no active drug remains during the fast","D":"Metformin acts by delaying carbohydrate digestion in the gut, so that it has no effect while no food is being eaten","E":"Metformin releases insulin exclusively when glucose is above about 100 mg/dL, so secretion stops as glucose falls"},"explanation":"Metformin is not an insulin secretagogue. Katzung places it with the drugs that lower glucose through their actions on liver, muscle and adipose tissue: it increases hepatic AMP-activated protein kinase activity, which reduces hepatic gluconeogenesis. First Aid describes the target as mitochondrial glycerol-3-phosphate dehydrogenase, with inhibition of hepatic gluconeogenesis and of the action of glucagon; both sources agree that it suppresses hepatic glucose output rather than raising insulin. Because insulin secretion is not driven by the drug, it falls normally during a fast, and Katzung lists sulfonylureas and insulin, not metformin, as the agents with increased hypoglycemia risk. Metformin opposes the action of glucagon rather than stimulating its release. It is not metabolized; it is excreted unchanged by the kidneys with a half-life of 1.5 to 3 hours. Delaying carbohydrate digestion in the gut is the action of the alpha-glucosidase inhibitors. Glucose-dependent insulin release is the action of GLP-1 receptor agonists and DPP-4 inhibitors.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Metformin monotherapy rarely causes hypoglycemia because it does not stimulate insulin secretion; hypoglycemia appears when it is combined with a sulfonylurea, a meglitinide or insulin. COMLEX: Metformin is started at diagnosis of type 2 DM; Katzung calls it weight neutral, while First Aid lists weight loss.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 4 — Pathophysiology of type 2 diabetes mellitus, including hepatic insulin resistance and gluconeogenesis dysregulation","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"C":"It is not metabolized; it is e","E":"Glucose-dependent insulin rele"}},{"id":"sdl34_q32","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pharmacokinetics and mechanism of action of biguanides","batch":2,"stem":"A 49-year-old woman with newly diagnosed type 2 diabetes starts metformin 500 mg twice daily, taking it on an empty stomach. Five days later she reports nausea, abdominal discomfort and loose stools. She is not vomiting, her vital signs are normal, her eGFR is 88 mL/min/1.73 m2, and her serum lactate is normal. Which is the most appropriate advice?","choices":{"E":"Take it with meals or change to an extended-release form, since these dose-related effects are often transient","A":"Stop metformin permanently, because early gastrointestinal upset is the first warning sign of lactic acidosis","B":"Begin vitamin B12 injections, because early diarrhea reflects calcium-dependent ileal B12 malabsorption","C":"Hold metformin until kidney function is retested, because these symptoms mean the drug is accumulating","D":"Switch to glipizide, because the gastrointestinal effects of metformin persist in most of the patients who get them"},"explanation":"Gastrointestinal effects (anorexia, nausea, vomiting, abdominal discomfort and diarrhea) are the most common adverse effects of metformin, occurring in up to 20% of patients. They are dose-related, tend to occur at the start of therapy and are often transient; taking metformin with food or using an extended-release formulation can ameliorate them, and only 3% to 5% of patients have to stop because of persistent diarrhea. Treatment is usually started at 500 mg daily with a meal and increased gradually. Lactic acidosis is rare and occurs mainly with tissue hypoxia or renal failure; her lactate and kidney function are normal. Vitamin B12 deficiency from impaired calcium-dependent ileal absorption develops after many years of use, not within days. Her normal eGFR gives no reason to suspect accumulation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Metformin lowers hepatic gluconeogenesis through AMPK; adverse effects are GI upset (most common), long-term B12 deficiency and rare lactic acidosis; it is contraindicated when eGFR is below 30 mL/min/1.73 m2. COMLEX: Start low (500 mg daily with a meal) and titrate; an extended-release formulation improves gastrointestinal tolerance.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 4 — Pathophysiology of type 2 diabetes mellitus, including hepatic insulin resistance and gluconeogenesis dysregulation","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"C":"Her normal eGFR gives no reaso","D":"They are dose-related, tend to"}},{"id":"sdl34_q33","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pharmacokinetics and mechanism of action of biguanides","batch":2,"stem":"A clinical pharmacology student builds a table comparing the pharmacokinetics of oral glucose-lowering drugs. Which of the following entries correctly describes metformin?","choices":{"B":"Not bound to plasma proteins and not metabolized, excreted unchanged by the kidneys, with a plasma half-life of about 1.5 to 3 hours","A":"Metabolized in the liver to products with some hypoglycemic activity, with a biologic effect lasting about 24 hours after one dose","C":"Converted by hepatic CYP2C8 and CYP3A4 to active metabolites, with a full glucose-lowering effect that takes several weeks to appear","D":"Very little absorbed from the gut because of its tetrasaccharide-like structure, so that it acts within the intestinal lumen itself","E":"Cleared by hepatic CYP3A4 with a plasma half-life of about 1 hour, which suits dosing just before each meal to cover that meal"},"explanation":"Metformin has a half-life of 1.5 to 3 hours, is not bound to plasma proteins, is not metabolized, and is excreted by the kidneys as the active compound. It is also a substrate for organic cation transporter 1, which is abundant in hepatocytes and the gut. Because the kidney is its only route of elimination, it accumulates in renal insufficiency; Katzung allows safe use at an eGFR of 60 to 45, cautious use at 45 to 30, and contraindicates it below 30 mL/min per 1.73 m2. The other entries describe other drugs. Glyburide is metabolized to products with hypoglycemic activity, and its biologic effect persists 24 hours after a single morning dose despite a short circulating half-life. Pioglitazone is metabolized by CYP2C8 and CYP3A4 to active metabolites, and First Aid notes a delayed onset of several weeks. Acarbose has the structure of a tetrasaccharide and very little is absorbed. Repaglinide is cleared by hepatic CYP3A4 with a half-life of about 1 hour and is taken just before meals.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Metformin is not protein bound, is not metabolized and is excreted unchanged in the urine, so renal function, not hepatic CYP activity, sets its dose; check renal function at least yearly. COMLEX: Lower doses are used in older adults with limited renal reserve and when the eGFR is 30 to 45 mL/min per 1.73 m2.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 4 — Pathophysiology of type 2 diabetes mellitus, including hepatic insulin resistance and gluconeogenesis dysregulation","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B"},{"id":"sdl34_q34","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the pharmacokinetics and mechanism of action of thiazolidinediones","batch":2,"stem":"A 32-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes and polycystic ovary syndrome presents with oligomenorrhea, acne, and difficulty conceiving. Her BMI is 34 kg/m2. She is started on pioglitazone. After 8 weeks, she reports improvement in her menstrual regularity and acne. Laboratory studies confirm improved insulin sensitivity. Which mechanism best explains pioglitazone's beneficial effects in this patient?","choices":{"A":"PPAR-gamma activation in adipose tissue improves insulin sensitivity and lowers hyperinsulinemia, and lower insulin reduces LH-driven ovarian androgen production","B":"PPAR-gamma activation in ovarian granulosa cells directly inhibits aromatase, reducing testosterone-to-estradiol conversion and normalizing the androgen-to-estrogen ratio","C":"PPAR-gamma activation in hypothalamic neurons normalizes pulsatile GnRH release, restoring the LH-to-FSH ratio and resuming regular folliculogenesis","D":"PPAR-gamma activation in adrenal cortex cells directly inhibits 17-alpha-hydroxylase, reducing adrenal androgen production and serum free testosterone","E":"PPAR-gamma activation in hepatocytes upregulates sex hormone-binding globulin gene expression, increasing SHBG and reducing free androgen bioavailability"},"explanation":"In polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS), insulin resistance and hyperinsulinemia are major drivers of excessive ovarian androgen production. First Aid traces PCOS to hyperinsulinemia or insulin resistance that alters hypothalamic feedback, raising the LH:FSH ratio and androgen production. By activating PPAR-gamma in adipose tissue, pioglitazone improves insulin sensitivity and reduces circulating insulin levels. This indirectly reduces LH-driven androgen production in ovarian theca cells, leading to lower free testosterone, improved menstrual regularity, and reduced acne. Pioglitazone is one of the insulin-sensitizing agents with demonstrated benefit in PCOS, alongside metformin.","boardPrep":"Step 1: TZDs improve PCOS by reducing hyperinsulinemia through PPAR-gamma-mediated insulin sensitization, which lowers LH-driven ovarian androgen production. COMLEX: PCOS is a named clinical presentation in COMLEX CP3. Insulin sensitizers (metformin and TZDs) are used when hormonal imbalance is driven by insulin resistance. TZDs are category C in pregnancy — counsel appropriately.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 5 — Role of obesity and insulin resistance in type 2 diabetes pathophysiology — TZDs directly target adipose-driven insulin resistance via PPAR-gamma","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A"},{"id":"sdl34_q35","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the pharmacokinetics and mechanism of action of thiazolidinediones","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with type 2 diabetes on rosiglitazone presents for follow-up. Labs show LDL 152 mg/dL, HDL 38 mg/dL, and TG 205 mg/dL. His physician compares the lipid effects of rosiglitazone and pioglitazone. Which statement correctly contrasts the lipid profiles associated with these two thiazolidinediones?","choices":{"E":"Rosiglitazone raises LDL more than pioglitazone, while pioglitazone tends to lower triglycerides and raise HDL, giving it a better lipid profile","A":"Rosiglitazone and pioglitazone both substantially and equally increase LDL cholesterol as an inherent class effect of PPAR-gamma agonism in all tissues","B":"Rosiglitazone decreases LDL cholesterol more than pioglitazone because it has stronger hepatic PPAR-gamma activity that upregulates LDL receptor expression","C":"Pioglitazone increases VLDL production more than rosiglitazone, leading to higher plasma triglyceride levels and a worse dyslipidemia profile on pioglitazone","D":"Pioglitazone significantly raises LDL cholesterol through hepatic PPAR-gamma-mediated upregulation of apolipoprotein B synthesis in hepatocytes"},"explanation":"The two clinically available TZDs have meaningfully different lipid effects despite sharing the PPAR-gamma agonist mechanism. Rosiglitazone tends to increase LDL cholesterol substantially, which contributed to concerns about its cardiovascular safety (RECORD trial). Pioglitazone, in contrast, tends to lower triglycerides and raise HDL cholesterol, without affecting total cholesterol and LDL cholesterol (Katzung), giving it a comparatively more favorable lipid profile; Katzung says rosiglitazone raises total, HDL and LDL cholesterol without a significant effect on triglycerides. The differential PPAR-alpha co-activation properties of pioglitazone versus rosiglitazone may partly explain these lipid differences.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Rosiglitazone = raises LDL more (worse lipid profile, increased MI risk). Pioglitazone = lowers TG, raises HDL (better lipid profile). This distinction explains their different cardiovascular outcome profiles. COMLEX: Pioglitazone is generally preferred over rosiglitazone when a TZD is required. Rosiglitazone is still available but with restricted prescribing in some countries.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 5 — Role of obesity and insulin resistance in type 2 diabetes pathophysiology — TZDs directly target adipose-driven insulin resistance via PPAR-gamma","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E"},{"id":"sdl34_q36","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the pharmacokinetics and mechanism of action of thiazolidinediones","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes and a myocardial infarction 18 months ago is evaluated for glycemic management. Her cardiologist is concerned about adding a thiazolidinedione to her regimen. Which specific TZD-associated cardiovascular concern most directly influences prescribing in a patient with prior MI?","choices":{"D":"Rosiglitazone has been associated with increased risk of myocardial infarction in a meta-analysis and cardiovascular outcomes data, making it the TZD of greater concern in patients with established coronary disease","A":"Pioglitazone causes direct myocardial toxicity through PPAR-gamma-mediated promotion of cardiac fibrosis in regions of ischemia-reperfusion injury","B":"Both thiazolidinediones cause a dose-dependent increase in ventricular arrhythmias in patients with prior MI through ion channel effects in ischemic myocardium","C":"Pioglitazone increases platelet thromboxane production, raising the risk of recurrent coronary thrombosis in patients with prior myocardial infarction","E":"All thiazolidinediones are equally contraindicated after myocardial infarction because of their class-wide effect on increasing left ventricular wall stress"},"explanation":"Rosiglitazone became highly controversial after a meta-analysis of randomized trials suggested a significant increase in myocardial infarction risk. Subsequent cardiovascular outcomes data from the RECORD trial showed a trend toward increased MI but did not achieve significance. Rosiglitazone was suspended in Europe and severely restricted in the United States; the US later lifted the restrictions (Katzung Ch 41). In contrast, Katzung says that in nondiabetic insulin-resistant patients with a recent ischemic stroke or TIA, pioglitazone reduced the risk of subsequent stroke or myocardial infarction. Therefore, in a patient with prior MI, pioglitazone is generally preferred if a TZD is warranted, and rosiglitazone requires additional caution.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Rosiglitazone = increased MI risk (meta-analysis signal, FDA restrictions). Pioglitazone = neutral to favorable cardiovascular profile (reduced subsequent stroke or MI after stroke or TIA in Katzung). Troglitazone = withdrawn (hepatotoxicity). COMLEX: TZD class ADRs: edema, weight gain, heart failure, osteoporosis, bladder cancer (pioglitazone). MI risk (rosiglitazone). Know which is which.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 5 — Role of obesity and insulin resistance in type 2 diabetes pathophysiology — TZDs directly target adipose-driven insulin resistance via PPAR-gamma","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D"},{"id":"sdl34_q37","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the pharmacokinetics and mechanism of action of α-glucosidase inhibitors","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes and stage 4 chronic kidney disease has postprandial hyperglycemia. Her physician considers an alpha-glucosidase inhibitor and compares acarbose with miglitol. Which statement about the pharmacokinetics of these two drugs is correct?","choices":{"E":"Miglitol resembles glucose, is absorbed and is cleared by the kidney, whereas very little acarbose is absorbed","A":"Both are absorbed intact and inactivated by hepatic CYP3A4, so neither needs adjustment for kidney function","B":"Acarbose is a prodrug that colonic bacteria activate, whereas miglitol acts directly on brush-border enzymes","C":"Miglitol strongly inhibits lactase and trehalase, so it causes more diarrhea than acarbose in chronic kidney disease","D":"Both stimulate insulin secretion after meals, so accumulation of either drug raises the risk of hypoglycemia"},"explanation":"Acarbose has the molecular mass and structure of a tetrasaccharide, and very little of it is absorbed; it acts in the intestinal lumen. Miglitol is structurally similar to glucose and is absorbed; it is not metabolized, is cleared by the kidney and should not be used in renal failure. Both drugs competitively inhibit the intestinal alpha-glucosidases (they are potent inhibitors of glucoamylase, alpha-amylase and sucrase, have less effect on isomaltase and hardly any on trehalase and lactase), delaying the digestion and absorption of starch and disaccharides. Neither drug is a prodrug, neither depends on hepatic CYP3A4, and neither stimulates insulin secretion, so hypoglycemia is not a problem with monotherapy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Alpha-glucosidase inhibitors (acarbose, miglitol) competitively inhibit brush-border alpha-glucosidases and lower postprandial glucose by 30% to 50%; acarbose is barely absorbed, while miglitol is absorbed and renally cleared. COMLEX: Avoid miglitol in renal failure; flatulence and diarrhea usually lessen with continued use as alpha-glucosidase expression is induced in the jejunum and ileum.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 1 — Definition and classification of diabetes mellitus, including postprandial glucose dysregulation targeted by alpha-glucosidase inhibitors","isHighYield":false,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"B":"Neither drug is a prodrug, nei"}},{"id":"sdl34_q38","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the pharmacokinetics and mechanism of action of α-glucosidase inhibitors","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old man with type 2 diabetes has had his acarbose increased over several months to 200 mg three times daily. Routine laboratory studies now show an alanine aminotransferase three times the upper limit of normal. He drinks no alcohol, viral hepatitis serologies are negative, and he has started no other drugs. Which is the best interpretation of this finding?","choices":{"A":"A dose-related rise in aminotransferases reported with acarbose above 300 mg/day that resolves when it is stopped","B":"Lactic acidosis from impaired hepatic lactate clearance, the mechanism by which acarbose injures hepatocytes","C":"Saturation of extensive hepatic CYP3A4 metabolism of absorbed acarbose, causing irreversible liver necrosis","D":"Hepatic glycogen overload from the extra glucose that reaches the portal vein when acarbose is taken with meals","E":"Steatohepatitis from colonic fermentation products reaching the liver, which progresses after acarbose is stopped"},"explanation":"An increase in hepatic aminotransferases has been noted in clinical trials with acarbose, especially at doses above 300 mg/day, and the abnormalities resolve when the drug is stopped. His current dose is 600 mg/day, above the usual maintenance target of 100 mg three times daily. Very little acarbose is absorbed, so hepatic CYP3A4 metabolism of the drug does not explain the finding. Acarbose delays carbohydrate digestion and absorption, so it reduces rather than increases the glucose reaching the portal vein after meals. Lactic acidosis is the rare serious adverse effect of metformin, not of acarbose. Colonic fermentation of undigested carbohydrate produces flatulence, diarrhea and abdominal pain, not liver disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Acarbose adverse effects: flatulence, diarrhea and abdominal pain from colonic fermentation of undigested carbohydrate; reversible aminotransferase elevation at doses above 300 mg/day; no hypoglycemia as monotherapy. COMLEX: Acarbose is started at 50 mg twice daily just before meals and titrated to 100 mg three times daily; lower the dose or stop the drug if aminotransferases rise.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 1 — Definition and classification of diabetes mellitus, including postprandial glucose dysregulation targeted by alpha-glucosidase inhibitors","isHighYield":false,"correct":"A"},{"id":"sdl34_q39","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the pharmacokinetics and mechanism of action of α-glucosidase inhibitors","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes and Crohn disease currently in remission is evaluated for additional antidiabetic therapy. Her physician considers adding acarbose to target postprandial glucose elevations. Which aspect of her history most strongly argues against this choice?","choices":{"B":"Intestinal disorders such as inflammatory bowel disease argue against alpha-glucosidase inhibitors, which send undigested carbohydrate to the colon, where fermentation causes gas, diarrhea, and pain","A":"Crohn disease causes chronic protein malabsorption that reduces acarbose binding to intestinal albumin-like proteins, increasing free drug toxicity in the intestinal wall","C":"Crohn disease elevates TNF-alpha levels that directly inactivate acarbose in the intestinal lumen before it can bind to brush border enzyme active sites","D":"Patients with Crohn disease have reduced intestinal alpha-glucosidase expression at baseline, making acarbose pharmacologically ineffective in this population","E":"Crohn disease-associated malabsorption reduces acarbose binding to plasma albumin, increasing systemic drug levels and causing hepatotoxicity at standard doses"},"explanation":"Katzung's (course reading) drug summary table says alpha-glucosidase inhibitors cannot be used in patients with impaired renal or hepatic function or intestinal disorders, and Crohn disease is an intestinal disorder even in remission. Katzung explains that the prominent adverse effects, flatulence, diarrhea and abdominal pain, result from undigested carbohydrate reaching the colon, where it is fermented into short-chain fatty acids and gas. Katzung does not describe a flare mechanism, so the point is that these predictable gastrointestinal effects fall on an already diseased bowel. Acarbose is very little absorbed, so the plasma albumin and hepatotoxicity choices do not apply, and Katzung notes that raised aminotransferases with acarbose occur mainly at doses above 300 mg/d.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Alpha-glucosidase inhibitor contraindications: IBD (Crohn, UC), malabsorption syndromes, intestinal obstruction, cirrhosis. GI side effects from colonic fermentation would be amplified and harmful in IBD. COMLEX: Know the mechanism of the contraindication — increased colonic fermentation worsens intestinal inflammation. Test this by asking why a patient with Crohn disease should not receive acarbose.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 1 — Definition and classification of diabetes mellitus, including postprandial glucose dysregulation targeted by alpha-glucosidase inhibitors","isHighYield":false,"correct":"B"},{"id":"sdl34_q40","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Understand the pharmacokinetics and mechanism of action of incretins","batch":2,"stem":"A 54-year-old man with type 2 diabetes and a BMI of 38 kg/m2 is started on semaglutide weekly injection. After 4 months, his HbA1c falls from 9.1% to 6.9% and he has lost 11 kg. He subsequently presents with nausea, epigastric pain radiating to the back, and a lipase of 680 U/L. Which adverse effect of GLP-1 receptor agonists is most concerning given this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Cholecystitis resulting from GLP-1-mediated inhibition of gallbladder contraction, causing bile stasis and gallstone formation that obstructs the cystic duct","B":"Acute pancreatitis, which has been reported with GLP-1 receptor agonists and requires stopping the drug and further evaluation when lipase is elevated","C":"Exocrine pancreatic insufficiency resulting from direct GLP-1 receptor agonist-mediated injury to acinar cells over months of treatment","D":"Gastroparesis developing from prolonged gastric emptying delay caused by GLP-1 receptor-mediated inhibition of the migrating motor complex","E":"Mesenteric ischemia caused by the vasoconstriction associated with GLP-1 receptor-mediated sympathetic activation in mesenteric arterioles"},"explanation":"GLP-1 receptor agonists have been associated with acute pancreatitis in postmarketing reports and in some clinical trial data, though causality is debated because type 2 diabetes itself is a risk factor for pancreatitis. The FDA includes a pancreatitis warning in the labeling of all GLP-1 receptor agonists. Clinicians should stop the drug and investigate further if acute pancreatitis is suspected. Elevated lipase alone is insufficient — clinical presentation of pancreatitis (epigastric pain radiating to the back, nausea) plus lipase above 3 times normal meets standard diagnostic criteria. Gallbladder disease (cholecystitis, cholelithiasis) is also a recognized adverse effect of GLP-1 agonists, particularly with rapid weight loss, but would not explain the significantly elevated lipase and radiating back pain. The GLP-1 agonist warning is acute pancreatitis, presenting as persistent severe abdominal pain, not exocrine insufficiency from acinar injury. Delayed gastric emptying does not explain epigastric pain radiating to the back with elevated lipase; the picture is pancreatitis. Epigastric pain radiating to the back with elevated lipase indicates pancreatitis, not mesenteric ischemia from sympathetic vasoconstriction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GLP-1 agonist = pancreatitis risk (hold if suspected). Also gallbladder disease risk (bile stasis from reduced gallbladder motility with weight loss). Black box = medullary thyroid carcinoma and MEN2. COMLEX: Stop GLP-1 agonist if pancreatitis is suspected. Do not restart after confirmed pancreatitis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 3 — Factors that increase and decrease insulin secretion, including incretin hormones GLP-1 and GIP from intestinal L and K cells","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"Gallbladder disease (cholecyst","C":"The GLP-1 agonist warning is acute pancreatitis, presenting ","D":"Delayed gastric emptying does not explain epigastric pain ra","E":"Epigastric pain radiating to the back with elevated lipase i"}},{"id":"sdl34_q41","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Understand the pharmacokinetics and mechanism of action of incretins","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes and CKD (eGFR 22 mL/min) is on exenatide twice daily. Her nephrologist asks whether the drug requires modification given her renal function. Which pharmacokinetic property of exenatide specifically limits its use in advanced kidney disease?","choices":{"D":"Exenatide is cleared mainly by glomerular filtration followed by proteolysis, so it is not recommended when eGFR falls below 30 mL/min","A":"Exenatide is metabolized by hepatic CYP3A4 enzymes and requires dose reduction in severe renal failure due to altered protein binding","B":"Exenatide undergoes substantial plasma protein binding that is dysregulated by the uremia of advanced CKD, increasing free drug toxicity","C":"Exenatide activates renal GLP-1 receptors in a manner that impairs tubular secretory function in patients with advanced diabetic nephropathy","E":"Exenatide binds to the P-glycoprotein transporter in the proximal tubule, and reduced P-gp activity in CKD significantly prolongs its plasma half-life"},"explanation":"Exenatide (both the twice-daily and the weekly extended-release formulation) is primarily eliminated by the kidneys. The drug is filtered at the glomerulus, reabsorbed in the proximal tubule, and degraded by luminal proteases. As renal function declines, exenatide clearance is substantially reduced. Twice-daily exenatide is not recommended when eGFR is below 30 mL/min, and the weekly formulation has similar restrictions. In contrast, liraglutide and semaglutide are primarily metabolized by proteolytic degradation throughout the body (not renally cleared) and can generally be used with appropriate caution across a broader range of renal function, making them preferred GLP-1 agonists in patients with advanced CKD. Exenatide undergoes glomerular filtration and is not approved when eGFR is below 30, rather than being cleared by hepatic CYP3A4. Exenatide's limit in CKD is its clearance by glomerular filtration, not dysregulated plasma protein binding.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Exenatide = renally cleared = avoid when eGFR below 30. Liraglutide and semaglutide = proteolytic degradation = safer in CKD. This pharmacokinetic distinction is a high-yield exam topic. COMLEX: In CKD patients needing a GLP-1 agonist, choose liraglutide or semaglutide over exenatide.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 3 — Factors that increase and decrease insulin secretion, including incretin hormones GLP-1 and GIP from intestinal L and K cells","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"Exenatide undergoes glomerular filtration and is not approve","B":"Exenatide's limit in CKD is its clearance by glomerular filt"}},{"id":"sdl34_q42","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Understand the pharmacokinetics and mechanism of action of incretins","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes is starting once-weekly subcutaneous semaglutide. She asks why her drug lasts a week when the body's own GLP-1 cannot be used as a medicine. Which structural feature best explains the long duration of action of semaglutide?","choices":{"B":"Fusion of two GLP-1 analog molecules to an IgG4 Fc fragment that slows clearance and resists DPP-4 attack","A":"An amino acid substitution at position 8 that resists DPP-4 and a C-18 fatty diacid chain that binds albumin","C":"Derivation from the exendin-4 peptide of Gila monster venom with a glycine substitution that slows breakdown","D":"Complexing with protamine and zinc, forming a subcutaneous crystal depot that dissolves over about a week","E":"A disulfide bridge between positions 1 and 7 that prevents renal filtration of the intact peptide molecule"},"explanation":"Native GLP-1 is rapidly degraded by dipeptidyl peptidase 4 and other enzymes and is cleared by the kidney, so it cannot be used therapeutically. Semaglutide has an alpha-aminoisobutyric acid substitution at position 8 that makes it resistant to DPP-4 degradation and a C-18 fatty diacid chain attached to lysine 26 that binds albumin, which accounts for its half-life of about 1 week. An IgG4 Fc fusion of two GLP-1 analogs describes dulaglutide (half-life about 5 days). An exendin-4 backbone with a glycine substitution describes exenatide, which is injected twice daily or given as a weekly extended-release preparation. Protamine is used to prolong certain insulin preparations, not GLP-1 receptor agonists. A disulfide bond between positions 1 and 7 is a feature of calcitonin, not of semaglutide.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GLP-1 receptor agonists (exenatide, liraglutide, dulaglutide, semaglutide) are DPP-4-resistant analogs; a fatty acid chain that binds albumin prolongs half-life (liraglutide C16, about 12 hours, once daily; semaglutide C18, about 1 week, once weekly). COMLEX: Oral semaglutide, formulated with the absorption enhancer SNAC, has 0.4% to 1% bioavailability and must be taken fasting with a glass of water, 30 minutes before food, drink or other medicines.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 3 — Factors that increase and decrease insulin secretion, including incretin hormones GLP-1 and GIP from intestinal L and K cells","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"D":"Protamine is used to prolong c","E":"A disulfide bond between posit"}},{"id":"sdl34_q43","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Understand the pharmacokinetics and mechanism of action of DPP-4 inhibitors","batch":2,"stem":"A 65-year-old man with type 2 diabetes develops sudden facial swelling, lip edema, and tongue swelling two weeks after starting sitagliptin. He has been on lisinopril for 5 years without prior events. He presents to the emergency department with partial upper airway obstruction. Which mechanism best explains this presentation?","choices":{"D":"DPP-4 helps degrade bradykinin and substance P, so sitagliptin-mediated DPP-4 inhibition lets these vasoactive peptides accumulate on top of the bradykinin rise already caused by lisinopril","A":"Sitagliptin activates mast cell GLP-1 receptors in the face and neck, releasing histamine and triggering an IgE-mediated immediate hypersensitivity reaction","B":"Sitagliptin cross-reacts chemically with the sulfonamide group present in lisinopril, causing immune complex deposition in submucosal blood vessels","C":"DPP-4 inhibition activates complement factor C1q on laryngeal endothelium, triggering acquired C1-esterase inhibitor deficiency and hereditary-pattern angioedema","E":"Sitagliptin upregulates histidine decarboxylase in submucosal mast cells, increasing de novo histamine synthesis and causing non-IgE-mediated angioedema"},"explanation":"DPP-4 cleaves a diverse range of substrates beyond GLP-1 and GIP, including bradykinin and substance P. ACE inhibitors such as lisinopril prevent the conversion of angiotensin I to angiotensin II and also prevent the breakdown of bradykinin by ACE (kininase II). This causes bradykinin accumulation, which is the mechanism of ACE inhibitor-induced angioedema. When sitagliptin is added, DPP-4 inhibition removes a backup route for breaking down substance P and bradykinin, adding to the ACE inhibitor effect; Brown et al. (Hypertension 2009) found that vildagliptin raised angioedema risk in patients taking an ACE inhibitor (odds ratio about 4.6) but not overall, and noted that DPP-4 inactivates substance P during ACE inhibition. The combination substantially increases the risk of bradykinin-mediated angioedema. This is a recognized drug interaction, and DPP-4 inhibitors should be used cautiously with ACE inhibitors. Sitagliptin does not activate GLP-1 receptors itself; it inhibits DPP-4, and ACE inhibitor angioedema is attributed to bradykinin rather than an IgE mast-cell reaction. Lisinopril's angioedema risk comes from blocked bradykinin degradation, and ACE inhibitors are not on First Aid's sulfa drug list. C1 esterase inhibitor deficiency is the cause of hereditary angioedema, whereas ACE inhibitor angioedema comes from blocked bradykinin degradation. ACE inhibitor angioedema is attributed to bradykinin, not to increased histamine synthesis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: DPP-4 inhibitors slow degradation of substance P and bradykinin (DPP-4 is a backup enzyme for these peptides when ACE is blocked). ACE inhibitors also increase bradykinin. Combination = additive bradykinin accumulation = increased angioedema risk. COMLEX: Angioedema on DPP-4 inhibitor plus ACE inhibitor = stop both and manage airway. High-yield drug interaction category.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 3 — Factors that increase and decrease insulin secretion, including GLP-1 preservation by DPP-4 inhibitors acting on the same degradative pathway","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"Sitagliptin does not activate GLP-1 receptors itself; it inh","B":"Lisinopril's angioedema risk comes from blocked bradykinin d","C":"C1 esterase inhibitor deficiency is the cause of hereditary ","E":"ACE inhibitor angioedema is attributed to bradykinin, not to"}},{"id":"sdl34_q44","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Understand the pharmacokinetics and mechanism of action of DPP-4 inhibitors","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes and stable heart failure with reduced ejection fraction (EF 35%) is admitted for acute decompensated heart failure. Her medication list includes saxagliptin 5 mg daily. Her cardiologist reviews outcomes data. Which finding from clinical trials should most concern the cardiologist regarding saxagliptin in this patient?","choices":{"B":"Saxagliptin significantly increased hospitalization for heart failure in the SAVOR-TIMI 53 trial, a finding not consistently seen with other DPP-4 inhibitors","A":"Saxagliptin was associated with a significant increase in the rate of myocardial infarction compared to placebo in the SAVOR-TIMI 53 trial primary outcome analysis","C":"All DPP-4 inhibitors were associated with a significantly increased cardiovascular mortality rate in patients with pre-existing cardiac dysfunction in outcomes trials","D":"Saxagliptin was found to inhibit cardiac PPAR-alpha in myocardial cells, reducing fatty acid oxidation and impairing the energetics of failing cardiomyocytes","E":"Saxagliptin was associated with significantly increased rates of atrial fibrillation through direct inhibition of sinoatrial node DPP-4 membrane proteins"},"explanation":"The SAVOR-TIMI 53 trial was a large cardiovascular outcomes trial that evaluated saxagliptin versus placebo in patients with type 2 diabetes and high cardiovascular risk. While saxagliptin was non-inferior to placebo for the primary MACE endpoint (no increased MI or stroke), the trial found a significant 27% increase in the rate of hospitalization for heart failure with saxagliptin. Katzung (course reading) says saxagliptin may increase the risk of heart failure (hazard ratio 1.27 in a postmarketing study) and that alogliptin was associated with a slightly increased rate of heart failure; the mechanism is not known. This finding was not consistently replicated with other DPP-4 inhibitors (sitagliptin in TECOS was neutral for heart failure hospitalizations), suggesting it may be a saxagliptin-specific or class-variable effect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Saxagliptin = increased heart failure hospitalizations (SAVOR-TIMI 53, 27% increase). TECOS trial (sitagliptin) = neutral for heart failure. Alogliptin in EXAMINE was neutral. COMLEX: In a patient with pre-existing heart failure, saxagliptin should be avoided. Sitagliptin is the safer DPP-4 inhibitor choice. Linagliptin is preferred in CKD.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 3 — Factors that increase and decrease insulin secretion, including GLP-1 preservation by DPP-4 inhibitors acting on the same degradative pathway","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"While saxagliptin was non-infe","C":"This finding was not consisten"}},{"id":"sdl34_q45","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Understand the pharmacokinetics and mechanism of action of DPP-4 inhibitors","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old man with type 2 diabetes on linagliptin presents with a 10-day history of sore throat, nasal congestion, and mild cough. Blood glucose is well-controlled and there are no signs of pneumonia. His physician explains that this is a known adverse effect associated with his medication class. Which adverse effect best accounts for his symptoms?","choices":{"C":"Linagliptin suppresses cytotoxic T lymphocyte activity by inhibiting the T-cell surface DPP-4, reducing the adaptive immune response against rhinovirus infection","A":"Linagliptin inhibits DPP-4 in intestinal mucosa, impairing the mucosal immune barrier and allowing viral entry from the GI tract to the upper respiratory system","B":"Nasopharyngitis and upper respiratory tract infections are a recognized class adverse effect of DPP-4 inhibitors, observed consistently across clinical trials","D":"Linagliptin enterohepatic circulation concentrates the drug in mucosal secretions of the nasopharynx, causing local tissue inflammation mimicking rhinovirus infection","E":"Linagliptin cross-inhibits fibroblast activation protein and DPP-8 and DPP-9 at therapeutic concentrations, disrupting mucosal barrier immunity in the nasopharynx"},"explanation":"Nasopharyngitis (common cold-like symptoms) and upper respiratory tract infections are among the most commonly reported adverse effects in DPP-4 inhibitor clinical trials across all agents in the class. Katzung (course reading) calls a predisposition to nasopharyngitis or upper respiratory tract infection the main adverse effect of DPP-4 inhibitors and does not give a mechanism. This effect is generally mild and does not require drug discontinuation unless there is a serious infection. It is clinically important to recognize this as a drug class effect rather than attributing the symptoms to a separate illness.","boardPrep":"Step 1: DPP-4 inhibitor adverse effects: nasopharyngitis (most common), upper respiratory tract infections, urticaria, angioedema, severe arthralgia, rare pancreatitis. COMLEX: DPP-4 (CD26) is expressed on immune cells including T cells — inhibition may affect T-cell co-stimulation and mucosal immunity. Recognize the class association with nasopharyngitis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 3 — Factors that increase and decrease insulin secretion, including GLP-1 preservation by DPP-4 inhibitors acting on the same degradative pathway","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B"},{"id":"sdl34_q46","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Understand the pharmacokinetics and mechanism of action of SGLT-2 inhibitors","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes and obesity is started on empagliflozin. Six weeks later, she presents with nausea, vomiting, and epigastric pain. Blood glucose is 162 mg/dL. Labs show pH 7.19, bicarbonate 10 mEq/L, anion gap 22 mEq/L, and elevated serum and urine ketones. Which complication of SGLT-2 inhibitor therapy best explains this presentation?","choices":{"E":"Euglycemic diabetic ketoacidosis, in which volume depletion and relative insulin deficiency shift fuel metabolism toward ketogenesis despite near-normal glucose","A":"Hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state caused by excessive urinary glucose loss triggering maximal hepatic gluconeogenesis and free water depletion in the absence of ketosis","B":"Acute pancreatitis triggered by SGLT-2-mediated increased pancreatic lipase substrate delivery through an indirect mechanism involving ketone body accumulation","C":"Hyperkalemic metabolic acidosis caused by SGLT-2-mediated inhibition of collecting duct hydrogen ion secretion, producing a distal renal tubular acidosis pattern","D":"Type B lactic acidosis caused by SGLT-2 inhibitor-mediated inhibition of mitochondrial complex I in hepatocytes, identical to the metformin lactic acidosis mechanism"},"explanation":"Euglycemic diabetic ketoacidosis (euDKA) is a recognized adverse effect of SGLT-2 inhibitors. It presents with the biochemical features of DKA (elevated ketones, metabolic acidosis, high anion gap) but without the marked hyperglycemia expected in classic DKA. SGLT-2 inhibitors cause continuous urinary glucose loss, which reduces glucose availability and creates relative insulin insufficiency. Simultaneously, they suppress insulin secretion (via sodium-glucose cotransport changes) and increase glucagon release. The resulting shift in fuel metabolism increases fatty acid oxidation and ketogenesis. Triggers include fasting, low-carbohydrate diets, illness, exercise, or alcohol use. SGLT-2 inhibitors should be held before surgery and during prolonged fasting. The patient has ketoacidosis with a high anion gap and glucose of only 162, not hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state without ketosis. Ketones with a high anion gap acidosis and near-normal glucose on an SGLT2 inhibitor is euglycemic ketoacidosis, not pancreatitis. Distal RTA is a failure of alpha-intercalated cell H+ secretion, whereas ketones with a high anion gap on an SGLT2 inhibitor indicate euglycemic ketoacidosis. SGLT2 inhibitors are not described as inhibiting mitochondrial complex I; elevated ketones with near-normal glucose identify euglycemic ketoacidosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SGLT-2 inhibitor = euglycemic DKA risk (glucose can be near-normal despite DKA). HY distinguishing feature from classic DKA. Mechanism = reduced glucose availability plus increased glucagon plus ketogenesis. COMLEX: Hold SGLT-2 inhibitors before elective surgery and during prolonged fasting. Euglycemic DKA is a diagnostic trap — screen with urine/serum ketones even when glucose is borderline.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 6 — Pathophysiology of diabetes mellitus complications, including the metabolic consequences of insulin deficiency and renal glucose handling","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"A":"The patient has ketoacidosis with a high anion gap and gluco","B":"Ketones with a high anion gap acidosis and near-normal gluco","C":"Distal RTA is a failure of alpha-intercalated cell H+ secret","D":"SGLT2 inhibitors are not described as inhibiting mitochondri"}},{"id":"sdl34_q47","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Understand the pharmacokinetics and mechanism of action of SGLT-2 inhibitors","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with type 2 diabetes and peripheral artery disease with prior amputation of the right second toe is evaluated for intensification of glycemic therapy. His cardiologist is considering adding canagliflozin. Which adverse effect associated specifically with canagliflozin is most relevant to this patient's vascular history?","choices":{"B":"Canagliflozin has been linked to increased lower extremity amputation risk, especially with peripheral artery disease or prior amputation","A":"Canagliflozin causes peripheral sensorimotor neuropathy by inhibiting SGLT-2 expressed in Schwann cells of the peripheral nervous system","C":"Canagliflozin causes critical limb ischemia by activating alpha-adrenergic receptors in peripheral arterioles, causing distal vasoconstriction","D":"Canagliflozin promotes progressive limb ischemia through SGLT-2-mediated inhibition of endothelial nitric oxide synthase in lower limb vasculature","E":"Canagliflozin reduces lower limb perfusion by promoting thrombocytosis and platelet aggregation through a SGLT-2-independent osmolality mechanism"},"explanation":"The CANVAS trial of canagliflozin found an increased risk of lower extremity amputation compared to placebo, especially of the toes (Katzung Ch 41). The risk was highest in patients with prior amputation or peripheral artery disease. Katzung says one multinational study of canagliflozin showed this increase and that it has not been observed in other studies of this drug or with the other SGLT-2 inhibitors; the mechanism is not known. CANVAS led to a boxed warning that the FDA later removed, but the label still advises caution in patients with peripheral artery disease or prior amputation. The other SGLT-2 inhibitors (empagliflozin, dapagliflozin) did not show a clear amputation signal in their trials. Canagliflozin should be used cautiously, if at all, in patients with vascular compromise.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Canagliflozin = increased lower limb amputation risk (CANVAS trial; boxed warning later removed). Specific to canagliflozin among SGLT-2 inhibitors. Risk highest in PAD or prior amputation. COMLEX: In a patient with PAD or prior amputation, canagliflozin is the SGLT-2 inhibitor to avoid. Choose empagliflozin or dapagliflozin instead if SGLT-2 inhibitor benefits are desired.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 6 — Pathophysiology of diabetes mellitus complications, including the metabolic consequences of insulin deficiency and renal glucose handling","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B"},{"id":"sdl34_q48","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Understand the pharmacokinetics and mechanism of action of SGLT-2 inhibitors","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man with heart failure with reduced ejection fraction (EF 28%) and type 2 diabetes is on maximally tolerated doses of an ACE inhibitor, beta-blocker, and mineralocorticoid antagonist. His cardiologist adds dapagliflozin. The patient asks why a diabetes drug is being used for his heart failure. Which explanation best describes dapagliflozin's benefit in this setting?","choices":{"A":"SGLT-2 inhibitors reduce hospitalization for heart failure and cardiovascular death in patients with HFrEF through hemodynamic and metabolic effects including osmotic diuresis, reduced preload and afterload, and potential direct myocardial benefits","B":"SGLT-2 inhibitors improve cardiac output in HFrEF by inhibiting the sodium-calcium exchanger in cardiomyocytes, increasing intracellular calcium and enhancing contractility","C":"SGLT-2 inhibitors reduce myocardial afterload exclusively by blocking renal sodium reabsorption and lowering blood pressure through a mechanism identical to thiazide diuretics","D":"SGLT-2 inhibitors are used in HFrEF only because they prevent furosemide-induced hyperglycemia by reducing urinary glucose excretion during diuresis","E":"SGLT-2 inhibitors improve HFrEF survival by activating myocardial PPAR-delta receptors, which enhance fatty acid oxidation and restore mitochondrial efficiency"},"explanation":"Dapagliflozin and empagliflozin have demonstrated significant reductions in hospitalization for heart failure and cardiovascular death in patients with HFrEF in the DAPA-HF and EMPEROR-Reduced trials, respectively, regardless of diabetes status. The cardiovascular benefits in heart failure are multifactorial: osmotic diuresis reduces preload, mild natriuresis reduces afterload, hematocrit increase improves oxygen-carrying capacity, and potential direct myocardial effects (shifting fuel substrate toward ketone oxidation, inhibiting NHE-1 sodium-hydrogen exchanger in cardiomyocytes) may improve myocardial energetics. SGLT-2 inhibitors are now guideline-recommended for HFrEF regardless of diabetic status, representing a major expansion of their indications beyond diabetes management. Katzung links SGLT2 cardiovascular benefit to weight loss, lower blood pressure and diuresis, not to sodium-calcium exchanger inhibition boosting contractility. Katzung says the mechanisms of benefit remain unclear, with weight loss, lower blood pressure and diuresis possibly contributing, so it is not an exclusively thiazide-like effect. Dapagliflozin reduced worsening heart failure or cardiovascular death equally in patients with and without diabetes, and SGLT2 inhibitors increase rather than reduce urinary glucose. PPAR-delta activation is not described; Katzung links SGLT2 benefit to weight loss, lower blood pressure and diuresis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SGLT-2 inhibitors reduce HFrEF hospitalizations and CV death (DAPA-HF, EMPEROR-Reduced). Benefits are independent of glucose lowering. Mechanism = preload and afterload reduction, potentially NHE-1 inhibition in cardiomyocytes. COMLEX: SGLT-2 inhibitors are now guideline-directed medical therapy for HFrEF even in non-diabetic patients. Empagliflozin and dapagliflozin approved for HFrEF.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 6 — Pathophysiology of diabetes mellitus complications, including the metabolic consequences of insulin deficiency and renal glucose handling","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"Katzung links SGLT2 cardiovascular benefit to weight loss, l","C":"Katzung says the mechanisms of benefit remain unclear, with ","D":"Dapagliflozin reduced worsening heart failure or cardiovascu","E":"PPAR-delta activation is not described; Katzung links SGLT2 "}}],"36":[{"id":"sdl36_n01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand how calcium and phosphate are distributed and regulated in extracellular fluid and plasma.","batch":1,"stem":"Total plasma calcium is normally about 9.4 mg/dL and circulates in three forms. Which fraction cannot diffuse across the capillary membrane and is therefore not filtered at the glomerulus?","choices":{"A":"The protein-bound fraction, roughly 40% of the total, carried mainly on albumin","B":"The ionized fraction, roughly 50% of the total, which is the physiologically active form","C":"The anion-complexed fraction, roughly 10% of the total, bound to citrate and phosphate","D":"The exchangeable bone fraction, roughly 1% of bone calcium, held as amorphous salts","E":"The intracellular fraction, roughly 1% of body calcium, stored within cell organelles"},"explanation":"About 41% of plasma calcium is bound to plasma proteins, chiefly albumin. This protein-bound calcium is nondiffusible through the capillary membrane and is not filtered by the glomerular capillaries. The remaining calcium is diffusible: about 50% is ionized and about 9% is complexed with anions such as citrate and phosphate, and both of these forms are filtered. The ionized fraction is the biologically active, tightly regulated form, but it is freely filtered rather than retained. Exchangeable bone calcium and intracellular calcium are not plasma fractions at all: the exchangeable pool is a small, rapidly mobilized store of amorphous salts in bone, and the cells hold about 1% of body calcium.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Plasma Ca2+ is about half ionized (active), about 40% albumin-bound, and the rest complexed with anions; only ionized Ca2+ is sensed by the parathyroid glands. COMLEX: Because albumin-bound calcium is not filtered, urinary calcium reflects the filtered ionized and complexed fractions that escape tubular reabsorption.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 3 — Parathyroid chief cells respond to ionized calcium, the diffusible fraction, rather than to total calcium","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"The ionized fraction is the bi"}},{"id":"sdl36_n02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand how calcium and phosphate are distributed and regulated in extracellular fluid and plasma.","batch":1,"stem":"A volunteer receives a rapid intravenous infusion of a soluble calcium salt, and plasma ionized calcium rises sharply. Within 30 to 60 minutes it returns to normal. Which mechanism accounts for most of this rapid correction?","choices":{"A":"Formation of new osteoblasts that deposit the extra calcium as hydroxyapatite crystals","B":"Induction of calbindin in the renal tubules by 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol within the hour","C":"Uptake into exchangeable amorphous calcium salts in bone that equilibrate with the ECF","D":"Precipitation of calcium phosphate in the arterial walls and other normal soft tissues","E":"Active secretion of the excess calcium into the intestinal lumen by the small bowel mucosa"},"explanation":"Bone contains a type of exchangeable calcium, about 0.4% to 1% of total bone calcium, deposited as readily mobilizable salts such as CaHPO4 and other amorphous salts. This pool is always in equilibrium with calcium in the extracellular fluid, so after an intravenous calcium load (or after calcium removal) the ionized calcium returns to normal within 30 to 60 minutes. It is a rapid buffer against transient excess or deficit. New osteoblast formation and conversion of salts into hydroxyapatite crystals take days to months, far too slow for this time course. Calbindin is an intestinal calcium-binding protein induced by 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol over about 2 days. Calcium phosphate does not normally precipitate in soft tissues because inhibitors such as pyrophosphate prevent it. Calcium entering the gut in digestive juices and sloughed cells is a small, steady daily flux, not a rapid correction. The 30–60 minute correction comes from exchangeable bone calcium; calcium enters the gut only at about 200 mg/day in digestive juices and sloughed mucosal cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: About 99% of body calcium is in bone; the exchangeable bone pool buffers minute-to-minute changes, while PTH and vitamin D provide slower, sustained control. COMLEX: Intravenous calcium relieves hypocalcemic tetany immediately, but the dose redistributes into exchangeable pools, so the cause must still be treated.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 2 — PTH supplies the slower, sustained correction of ECF calcium through bone, kidney, and intestine","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"E":"The 30–60 minute correction comes from exchangeable bone cal"}},{"id":"sdl36_n13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand how calcium and phosphate are distributed and regulated in extracellular fluid and plasma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 22-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department during a panic attack. She is breathing rapidly and reports tingling around her mouth and in her fingertips, and her hands show carpopedal spasm. Arterial pH is 7.56 and PaCO2 is 24 mm Hg. Total serum calcium is 9.5 mg/dL and albumin is 4.2 g/dL. Which of the following best explains her symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Alkalemia releases calcium from albumin, so ionized calcium rises and nerves become refractory","B":"Alkalemia frees binding sites on albumin, so more calcium binds and ionized calcium falls","C":"Hyperventilation lowers total calcium by increasing calcium excretion in the distal tubule","D":"Respiratory alkalosis suppresses PTH, so bone stops releasing calcium within a few minutes","E":"Low PaCO2 constricts cerebral vessels, causing tingling that is unrelated to plasma calcium"},"explanation":"Albumin has negatively charged sites that bind either hydrogen ions or calcium. In alkalemia there are fewer hydrogen ions, so more sites are free to bind calcium; the free, ionized calcium concentration falls even though total calcium is unchanged, and symptoms of hypocalcemia such as paresthesias and carpopedal spasm appear. Calcium release from albumin with a rise in ionized calcium is what happens in acidemia, not alkalemia. Her total calcium of 9.5 mg/dL is normal, so urinary losses have not lowered it; only the distribution between bound and free forms has changed. A fall in ionized calcium stimulates, rather than suppresses, PTH secretion. Carpopedal spasm is a sign of low ionized calcium, so it cannot be explained as tingling unrelated to calcium.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Higher pH increases Ca2+ binding to albumin, lowering ionized Ca2+ (cramps, paresthesias, carpopedal spasm) and raising PTH; lower pH raises ionized Ca2+. Total calcium does not change. COMLEX: Perioral tingling and carpopedal spasm during hyperventilation reflect reduced ionized calcium despite a normal total calcium.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 3 — Parathyroid chief cells sense ionized calcium, so alkalemia increases PTH secretion","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"Calcium release from albumin w","C":"Her total calcium of 9.5 mg/dL","D":"A fall in ionized calcium stim"}},{"id":"sdl36_n14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand how calcium and phosphate are distributed and regulated in extracellular fluid and plasma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with nephrotic syndrome has a total serum calcium of 7.6 mg/dL (normal about 9.4). Serum albumin is 1.9 g/dL. He has no paresthesias or muscle cramps, Chvostek sign is absent, ionized calcium is within the normal range, and PTH is normal. Which of the following best explains these findings?","choices":{"A":"The low albumin reduces ionized calcium, but PTH resistance keeps him entirely free of symptoms","B":"Urinary calcium losses have depleted bone stores and lowered his total body calcium","C":"The low albumin reduces only the protein-bound calcium, so ionized calcium stays normal","D":"The low albumin shifts calcium onto citrate, which inactivates the remaining calcium","E":"The low albumin stimulates calcitonin release, which prevents symptoms of low calcium"},"explanation":"Changes in plasma protein concentration change total calcium in the same direction, but when they develop slowly they do not produce a parallel change in ionized calcium, because PTH-based regulation corrects any transient change in the ionized form. Only the albumin-bound fraction is reduced here, so ionized calcium, the active form, is normal; he has no symptoms and PTH is unchanged, consistent with First Aid's point that albumin changes do not alter PTH secretion. His measured ionized calcium is normal, so there is no ionized deficit for PTH resistance to explain. Depleted bone stores would not leave ionized calcium and PTH normal. More calcium complexed with anions would lower ionized calcium, which is not the case. Calcitonin is secreted in response to high, not low, calcium and has little role in normal calcium homeostasis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hypoalbuminemia lowers total Ca2+ but not ionized Ca2+, and PTH is unchanged. COMLEX: In nephrotic syndrome, cirrhosis, or malnutrition, confirm true hypocalcemia with an ionized calcium before treating.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 1 — Hypoparathyroidism produces a true fall in ionized calcium with tetany","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"His measured ionized calcium i","D":"More calcium complexed with an"}},{"id":"sdl36_n03","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the physiologic effects of calcium and phosphate in the bone and understand the process of bone remodeling","batch":1,"stem":"Bone resists both stretching and crushing forces. Which pairing correctly matches each property with the bone component mainly responsible for it?","choices":{"A":"Hydroxyapatite salts provide tensile strength, and collagen fibers provide compressional strength","B":"Proteoglycans provide tensile strength, and amorphous calcium salts provide compressional strength","C":"Osteocytes provide tensile strength, and the haversian canal system provides compressional strength","D":"Collagen fibers provide tensile strength, and hydroxyapatite salts provide compressional strength","E":"Chondroitin sulfate provides tensile strength, and hyaluronic acid provides compressional strength"},"explanation":"The organic matrix of bone is 90% to 95% collagen fibers, which run along lines of tensional force and give bone its tensile strength. The crystalline salts, mainly hydroxyapatite, Ca10(PO4)6(OH)2, have great compressional strength. Tight bonding of the crystals to the collagen fibers prevents shear and gives bone both properties at once. The reversed pairing assigns each property to the wrong component. Proteoglycans of the ground substance, such as chondroitin sulfate and hyaluronic acid, help control deposition of calcium salts and aid repair; they are not the main source of either strength. Amorphous salts are a small, rapidly exchangeable pool. Osteocytes are osteoblasts entrapped in bone, and the haversian canal is the vascular channel left at the center of each osteon; neither supplies mechanical strength.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Type I collagen is the main collagen of bone; defective type I collagen in osteogenesis imperfecta produces fragile bone, whereas defective mineralization produces rickets or osteomalacia. COMLEX: Osteomalacia in adults presents with bone pain and muscle weakness.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — Growth hormone stimulates osteoblastic deposition of new bone matrix","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"The reversed pairing assigns e"}},{"id":"sdl36_n04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the physiologic effects of calcium and phosphate in the bone and understand the process of bone remodeling","batch":1,"stem":"Osteoblasts produce osteoprotegerin as well as RANKL. Which of the following best describes how osteoprotegerin limits bone resorption?","choices":{"A":"It binds RANKL as a decoy receptor, so RANKL cannot activate RANK on preosteoclasts","B":"It blocks PTH receptors on mature osteoclasts, so PTH cannot stimulate them directly","C":"It degrades macrophage colony-stimulating factor before it reaches osteoclast precursors","D":"It inhibits acid secretion at the ruffled border of osteoclasts that are already active","E":"It hydrolyzes pyrophosphate in the osteoid, so new hydroxyapatite replaces resorbed bone"},"explanation":"Osteoprotegerin, also called osteoclastogenesis inhibitory factor, acts as a decoy: it binds RANKL and prevents RANKL from interacting with its receptor RANK on preosteoclasts, so fewer preosteoclasts differentiate into mature, bone-resorbing osteoclasts. Osteoclasts do not have PTH receptors; PTH acts on osteoblasts, which raise RANKL and lower osteoprotegerin. Macrophage colony-stimulating factor is a second osteoblast product needed for osteoclast formation, and osteoprotegerin does not degrade it. The acids and enzymes released at the ruffled border are the effector step of resorption, not the target of osteoprotegerin. Breakdown of pyrophosphate in osteoid is the job of tissue-nonspecific alkaline phosphatase, which permits mineralization.","boardPrep":"Step 1: RANKL (osteoblasts) binds RANK (osteoclast precursors) to drive osteoclastogenesis; OPG is the decoy. Denosumab is a monoclonal antibody against RANKL used for osteoporosis. COMLEX: Glucocorticoids raise RANKL and lower OPG, one mechanism of steroid-induced bone loss; estrogen raises OPG.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 2 — PTH stimulates osteoclastic resorption indirectly by raising RANKL and lowering OPG on osteoblasts","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A"},{"id":"sdl36_n15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the physiologic effects of calcium and phosphate in the bone and understand the process of bone remodeling","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old man has his left lower leg immobilized in a cast for 8 weeks after a tibial fracture, while he continues to bear weight on his right leg. Follow-up radiographs show marked demineralization of the left tibia, whereas the right tibia appears dense. Serum calcium, phosphate, PTH, and vitamin D levels are normal. Which of the following best explains the difference between the legs?","choices":{"A":"Immobilization raises PTH receptor density on osteoclasts, so the casted tibia is resorbed faster","B":"Reduced blood flow under the cast deprives the tibia of vitamin D needed for any bone deposition","C":"The healing callus draws calcium out of the rest of the casted tibia and into new woven bone","D":"Weight bearing makes the right tibia secrete calcitonin locally, protecting it from resorption","E":"Loss of compressional load on the casted bone reduces osteoblastic deposition as resorption continues"},"explanation":"Bone is deposited in proportion to the compressional load it must carry, and continual physical stress stimulates osteoblastic deposition and calcification. Guyton gives this exact example: if one leg is in a cast while the person walks on the other leg, the bone of the casted leg becomes thin and up to 30% decalcified within a few weeks, while the opposite bone stays thick. His systemic calcium, phosphate, PTH, and vitamin D are normal, which points to a local mechanical cause. Osteoclasts lack PTH receptors, and his PTH is normal. Vitamin D is a circulating hormone at normal levels, so the casted bone is not deprived of it. A fracture callus is a local zone of new osteoblastic tissue at the break and does not explain loss throughout the bone. Calcitonin is secreted by the parafollicular cells of the thyroid, not by bone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lack of physical stress is a cause of osteoporosis, a loss of bone matrix, which differs from osteomalacia and rickets, defects of mineralization. COMLEX: Weight-bearing activity maintains bone mass, and fixation devices that allow early use of a fractured bone accelerate healing.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 3 — Growth hormone and mechanical stress both drive osteoblastic bone deposition","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"C":"A fracture callus is a local z","D":"Calcitonin is secreted by the "}},{"id":"sdl36_n16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the physiologic effects of calcium and phosphate in the bone and understand the process of bone remodeling","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-year-old boy is evaluated for bowed legs and delayed walking. Radiographs show poorly mineralized bones. Serum calcium and phosphate are normal, but serum alkaline phosphatase is markedly low. Genetic testing shows loss-of-function mutations in the gene for tissue-nonspecific alkaline phosphatase. Which of the following best explains the defective mineralization?","choices":{"A":"Pyrophosphate is not broken down in the osteoid, so it blocks hydroxyapatite crystallization","B":"Osteoblasts cannot secrete collagen, so no osteoid forms on which calcium salts can deposit","C":"Pyrophosphate is depleted in the osteoid, so calcium salts deposit in tendons instead of bone","D":"Osteoclasts cannot acidify the ruffled border, so old bone is never replaced by new bone","E":"Calbindin synthesis requires alkaline phosphatase, so intestinal calcium absorption fails"},"explanation":"Pyrophosphate inhibits hydroxyapatite crystallization. Osteoblasts secrete tissue-nonspecific alkaline phosphatase into the osteoid, where it breaks down pyrophosphate; once pyrophosphate is neutralized, the affinity of collagen for calcium salts allows crystallization. Genetic deficiency of this enzyme lets pyrophosphate rise too high, and animals with the deficiency are born with soft, inadequately calcified bones. Osteoid is formed normally here; the defect is in calcifying it. Too little pyrophosphate, as with deficiency of NPP1 or the ANK transporter, causes excessive calcification, the opposite problem. A failure of osteoclast resorption would not explain poorly mineralized bone with low alkaline phosphatase. Calbindin is induced by 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol, and his normal serum calcium and phosphate show intestinal absorption is intact.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Osteoblasts build bone by secreting collagen and catalyzing mineralization through alkaline phosphatase; bone ALP is a marker of osteoblast activity. COMLEX: Distinguishing loss of bone matrix (osteoporosis) from failed calcification (rickets, osteomalacia) guides evaluation and treatment.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 1 — Elevated alkaline phosphatase in hyperparathyroid bone disease reflects compensatory osteoblastic activity","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"C":"Too little pyrophosphate, as w","D":"A failure of osteoclast resorp"}},{"id":"sdl36_n05","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the physiologic effects of calcium and phosphate outside of the bone","batch":1,"stem":"Low extracellular calcium makes peripheral nerve fibers fire spontaneously. Which of the following best explains this effect of hypocalcemia on excitable membranes?","choices":{"A":"Membrane permeability to potassium rises, so the resting membrane potential hyperpolarizes","B":"Membrane permeability to sodium rises, so action potentials start with less depolarization","C":"Presynaptic calcium entry rises, so more acetylcholine is released at each motor end plate","D":"Sarcoplasmic calcium falls, so skeletal muscle cross-bridges cycle without troponin control","E":"Membrane permeability to chloride falls, so inhibitory currents no longer stabilize the axon"},"explanation":"When extracellular calcium falls, neuronal membranes become more permeable to sodium, so action potentials are initiated easily; Costanzo describes the same effect as a lowered threshold potential, so less inward current is needed to fire. When ionized calcium falls far enough, peripheral nerve fibers discharge spontaneously and cause tetanic muscle contraction. Increased potassium permeability with hyperpolarization would make nerves less excitable, the opposite of what is seen. Transmitter release depends on calcium entry into the nerve terminal, which low extracellular calcium would reduce, not increase. The calcium that triggers cross-bridge cycling is intracellular calcium, so low extracellular calcium does not act by removing troponin control. A fall in chloride permeability is not the mechanism described for hypocalcemic excitability.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hypocalcemia: perioral tingling, hyperreflexia, Chvostek sign (facial twitch on tapping the facial nerve), Trousseau sign (carpopedal spasm with a BP cuff), seizures, QT prolongation. COMLEX: Chvostek and Trousseau signs are named CP3 physical findings of hypocalcemia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 1 — Hypoparathyroidism produces tetany through hypocalcemia-induced neuronal hyperexcitability","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"E":"A fall in chloride permeabilit"}},{"id":"sdl36_n06","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the physiologic effects of calcium and phosphate outside of the bone","batch":1,"stem":"Normal plasma calcium is about 9.4 mg/dL. At approximately what plasma calcium concentration does tetany ordinarily appear?","choices":{"A":"About 9 mg/dL, roughly 5% below normal","B":"About 8 mg/dL, roughly 15% below normal","C":"About 6 mg/dL, roughly 35% below normal","D":"About 4 mg/dL, roughly 55% below normal","E":"About 2 mg/dL, roughly 80% below normal"},"explanation":"Tetany ordinarily occurs when blood calcium falls from its normal level of 9.4 mg/dL to about 6 mg/dL, only about 35% below normal, and a level of about 4 mg/dL is usually lethal. In rickets, the classic signs of tetany likewise appear once calcium falls below about 7 mg/dL. Levels of 9 or 8 mg/dL are mild reductions that are well above the tetany threshold. A level of about 4 mg/dL is the usually lethal level, so tetany would already have developed at much higher concentrations, and 2 mg/dL lies far below the lethal range.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Guyton thresholds: tetany near 6 mg/dL, death near 4 mg/dL; hypercalcemic nervous system depression above about 12 mg/dL and calcium phosphate precipitation above about 17 mg/dL. COMLEX: Tetanic respiratory spasm can be fatal and is relieved immediately by intravenous calcium.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 3 — A falling ionized calcium is the primary stimulus for PTH secretion","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"D":"A level of about 4 mg/dL is th","E":"A level of about 4 mg/dL is th"}},{"id":"sdl36_n17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the physiologic effects of calcium and phosphate outside of the bone","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old man is admitted with confusion and weakness after taking very large daily doses of a vitamin D supplement for several months. Serum calcium is 17.8 mg/dL and phosphate is 7.9 mg/dL. Chest CT shows diffuse calcific densities in both lungs, and renal ultrasound shows tubular calcification. Which of the following best explains the soft tissue calcification?","choices":{"A":"Necrotic lung and kidney tissue attracts calcium, which deposits even when plasma levels are normal","B":"Osteoblasts migrate into the lungs and kidneys and lay down osteoid that then becomes mineralized","C":"High calcium stimulates pyrophosphate synthesis, which crystallizes as insoluble salts in tissues","D":"Calcitonin released in response to hypercalcemia drives calcium out of bone and into soft tissues","E":"Calcium and phosphate rise so far above saturation that tissue inhibitors cannot prevent precipitation"},"explanation":"Extracellular calcium and phosphate are normally supersaturated relative to hydroxyapatite, but inhibitors such as pyrophosphate in tissues and plasma prevent precipitation outside bone. When calcium rises above about 17 mg/dL together with a high phosphate, the fluids become so supersaturated that calcium phosphate crystals deposit in normal tissues, including the alveoli of the lungs, the renal tubules, the stomach mucosa, and the arterial walls. This metastatic calcification can develop within a few days. Deposition in degenerating tissue with normal plasma levels is dystrophic calcification, whereas his tissues were normal and his plasma levels are extreme. Osteoblasts do not migrate into lungs or kidneys. Pyrophosphate is an inhibitor of crystallization, not the deposited salt. Calcitonin lowers plasma calcium by inhibiting osteoclastic resorption and does not move calcium into soft tissues.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Metastatic calcification occurs in normal tissue because of high serum calcium or phosphate (hyperparathyroidism, sarcoidosis, hypervitaminosis D, CKD); dystrophic calcification occurs in damaged tissue with normal serum levels. COMLEX: Vitamin D excess causes hypercalcemia, hypercalciuria, loss of appetite, and stupor.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 1 — Severe hyperparathyroidism can cause parathyroid poisoning with metastatic calcification","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E"},{"id":"sdl36_n18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the physiologic effects of calcium and phosphate outside of the bone","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old woman with breast cancer metastatic to the spine is brought in by her daughter for 1 week of increasing drowsiness, constipation, poor appetite, and frequent urination. Deep tendon reflexes are diminished. Serum calcium is 14.6 mg/dL. An ECG is obtained. Which combination of findings is most consistent with the effects of this calcium level?","choices":{"A":"Neuromuscular hyperexcitability with tetany and a prolonged QT interval","B":"Depressed neural excitability with sluggish reflexes and a prolonged QT interval","C":"Neuromuscular hyperexcitability with hyperreflexia and a shortened QT interval","D":"Depressed neural excitability with sluggish reflexes and a shortened QT interval","E":"Normal neural excitability with peaked T waves and a widened QRS complex"},"explanation":"When calcium rises above normal, the nervous system becomes depressed and reflexes are sluggish; increased calcium also shortens the QT interval and reduces appetite and causes constipation, probably by depressing contractility of the gastrointestinal muscle walls. These effects begin above about 12 mg/dL and become marked above 15 mg/dL. Her polyuria fits the nephrogenic diabetes insipidus that hypercalcemia can cause. Tetany, hyperexcitability, and QT prolongation are features of hypocalcemia, so any choice pairing hypercalcemia with hyperexcitability or a long QT mixes the two states. Peaked T waves with a widened QRS complex point to hyperkalemia, not hypercalcemia, and her mental status is clearly not normal.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hypercalcemia: stones, bones, groans, thrones, and psychiatric overtones; hyporeflexia and a short QT. Hypocalcemia: tetany, seizures, QT prolongation. COMLEX: Polyuria in hypercalcemia reflects nephrogenic diabetes insipidus.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 1 — Malignancy-associated hypercalcemia arises from osteolytic metastases or PTHrP","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"C":"Tetany, hyperexcitability, and"}},{"id":"sdl36_n07","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe how calcium and phosphate are absorbed and excreted","batch":1,"stem":"A healthy adult ingests about 1000 mg of calcium and 1400 mg of phosphorus each day. Which statement best compares the intestinal absorption of these two minerals?","choices":{"A":"Phosphate is absorbed easily, about 80% of intake, while calcium absorption is limited to about 40%","B":"Calcium is absorbed easily, about 80% of intake, while phosphate absorption is limited to about 40%","C":"Both minerals are absorbed almost completely, so the feces normally contain neither of them","D":"Both minerals are absorbed poorly, about 10% of intake, so the urine normally contains neither","E":"Each mineral is absorbed at about 60% of intake, and neither depends on vitamin D for uptake"},"explanation":"Divalent cations such as calcium are poorly absorbed; with the help of vitamin D about 40% (400 mg/day) of ingested calcium is absorbed. Because another 200 mg/day enters the gut in digestive juices and sloughed cells, about 80% of the daily calcium intake leaves in the feces, and about 200 mg/day is excreted in the urine. Phosphate, in contrast, is absorbed easily, nearly 80% of intake, and most of the absorbed phosphate is excreted in the urine (about 900 mg/day). Reversing the two minerals contradicts these figures. Neither mineral is absorbed completely, since both appear in the stool, and neither is absorbed as poorly as 10%, since both appear in the urine. Vitamin D promotes absorption of both calcium and phosphate.","boardPrep":"Step 1: At balance, urinary calcium (about 200 mg/day) equals net intestinal absorption; most dietary calcium is lost in the feces, whereas most absorbed phosphate is excreted in the urine. COMLEX: Loop diuretics increase urinary calcium excretion (loops lose Ca2+) and are used in hypercalcemia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 2 — PTH increases intestinal calcium absorption indirectly through 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"Reversing the two minerals con","C":"Neither mineral is absorbed co","E":"Vitamin D promotes absorption "}},{"id":"sdl36_n08","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe how calcium and phosphate are absorbed and excreted","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following best describes how the kidneys normally regulate plasma phosphate concentration?","choices":{"A":"A constant fraction: about 98% of filtered phosphate is reabsorbed whatever the plasma phosphate level","B":"Tubular secretion: phosphate is actively secreted in the collecting duct whenever plasma phosphate rises","C":"A filtration barrier: phosphate is bound to albumin and is kept out of the filtrate at the glomerulus","D":"A calcium link: phosphate excretion falls whenever plasma calcium rises, so their product stays constant","E":"An overflow mechanism: below about 1 mmol/L all filtered phosphate is reabsorbed, and excretion rises above it"},"explanation":"Renal phosphate excretion is controlled by an overflow mechanism. When plasma phosphate is below a critical value of about 1 mmol/L, all filtered phosphate is reabsorbed and none appears in the urine; above that value, phosphate loss rises in proportion to the excess. Because most diets contain substantial milk and meat, plasma phosphate usually stays above the threshold and about 10% to 15% of filtered phosphate is excreted. PTH can greatly increase phosphate excretion. Reabsorption of about 98% of the filtered load describes calcium, not phosphate. Regulation works through the reabsorptive threshold rather than through collecting duct secretion. Plasma inorganic phosphate circulates as HPO4 2- and H2PO4- ions that are filtered, not as an albumin-bound form. The kidney adjusts phosphate excretion to plasma phosphate and to PTH, not to a fixed calcium-phosphate product.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PTH is the Phosphate-Trashing Hormone: it decreases PO4 reabsorption in the PCT, raising urine phosphate. COMLEX: In chronic kidney disease, hyperphosphatemia lowers calcium and drives secondary hyperparathyroidism.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 2 — PTH reduces proximal tubular phosphate reabsorption, producing phosphaturia","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"C":"Plasma inorganic phosphate cir","D":"The kidney adjusts phosphate e"}},{"id":"sdl36_n19","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe how calcium and phosphate are absorbed and excreted","batch":2,"stem":"A 35-year-old man takes calcium supplements well above the recommended dose for several weeks. His plasma calcium rises only slightly above normal, but his 24-hour urinary calcium increases markedly. Where along the nephron is calcium reabsorption mainly adjusted to produce this change?","choices":{"A":"The proximal tubule, where reabsorption of the filtered calcium falls in step with each rise","B":"The late distal tubule and early collecting duct, where PTH governs the final 10% of reabsorption","C":"The glomerulus, where filtration of the protein-bound calcium fraction rises with supplementation","D":"The thin descending limb, where calcitonin directly blocks calcium channels to increase excretion","E":"The medullary collecting duct, where ADH-driven water loss carries calcium passively into urine"},"explanation":"The renal tubules normally reabsorb about 98% of filtered calcium. At least 90% is reabsorbed in the proximal tubules, loops of Henle, and early distal tubules, while reabsorption of the remaining 10% in the late distal tubules and early collecting ducts varies with blood calcium. Even a minute increase in blood calcium above normal markedly increases calcium excretion, and PTH is the most important factor controlling this distal reabsorption. The bulk proximal reabsorption is not the segment that fine-tunes calcium excretion. Protein-bound calcium is not filtered at the glomerulus at all. Calcitonin acts mainly by inhibiting osteoclastic bone resorption, not on the thin descending limb. ADH regulates water reabsorption and does not set calcium excretion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PTH increases Ca2+ reabsorption in the DCT and decreases PO4 reabsorption in the PCT. Loop diuretics increase calcium excretion (loops lose Ca2+). COMLEX: Hypercalciuria raises the risk of calcium phosphate and calcium oxalate kidney stones.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 2 — PTH acts on the distal nephron to increase calcium reabsorption","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"The bulk proximal reabsorption","E":"ADH regulates water reabsorpti"}},{"id":"sdl36_n20","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe how calcium and phosphate are absorbed and excreted","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old man with chronic pancreatitis has had greasy, foul-smelling stools for 2 years. He reports diffuse bone pain and proximal muscle weakness. Serum calcium is 8.1 mg/dL, phosphate is 2.2 mg/dL, alkaline phosphatase is elevated, and PTH is elevated. Which of the following best explains his poor calcium absorption?","choices":{"A":"Pancreatic enzymes are needed to free calcium from dietary protein before it can be absorbed","B":"Elevated PTH directly blocks calcium entry across the brush border of the intestinal epithelium","C":"Rapid fatty transit blocks phosphate absorption alone, while calcium absorption stays normal","D":"Unabsorbed fat binds calcium as insoluble soaps and carries fat-soluble vitamin D into the stool","E":"Chronic pancreatitis destroys the cells that secrete calcitonin, so calcium is lost in the feces"},"explanation":"In steatorrhea, vitamin D, which is fat soluble, and calcium, which forms insoluble soaps with fat, both pass into the feces. The resulting poor calcium and phosphate absorption causes osteomalacia, adult rickets, which presents with bone pain and muscle weakness. The low calcium drives compensatory secondary hyperparathyroidism, and the high PTH increases urinary phosphate loss, explaining his low phosphate and high alkaline phosphatase. The mechanism of calcium loss is fat binding and vitamin D loss, not a need for pancreatic enzymes to free calcium. PTH increases intestinal calcium absorption indirectly by promoting active vitamin D formation; it does not block uptake. His calcium is low, so calcium absorption has not stayed normal. Calcitonin comes from the parafollicular cells of the thyroid, not the pancreas.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, K) depend on bile, pancreatic secretions, and an intact ileum; steatorrhea causes their deficiency. Vitamin D deficiency gives low Ca2+, low PO4, high PTH, high ALP. COMLEX: Patients with fat malabsorption need fat-soluble vitamin supplementation, including vitamin D.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 1 — Vitamin D deficiency is a cause of secondary hyperparathyroidism","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"B":"PTH increases intestinal calci","E":"Calcitonin comes from the para"}},{"id":"sdl36_n09","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain the main physiologic functions of Vitamin D","batch":1,"stem":"A person's dietary vitamin D intake increases severalfold, yet plasma 25-hydroxycholecalciferol rises only slightly. Which of the following best explains this observation?","choices":{"A":"The kidney converts the excess 25-hydroxycholecalciferol to the active hormone within minutes","B":"25-Hydroxycholecalciferol feedback-inhibits its own formation in the liver, so the excess stays stored","C":"Dietary vitamin D is chemically inactive and cannot be converted to 25-hydroxycholecalciferol","D":"PTH secretion rises with vitamin D intake and directly blocks hepatic 25-hydroxylation","E":"Excess vitamin D is water soluble, so it is filtered and excreted unchanged in the urine"},"explanation":"The first activation step, conversion of cholecalciferol to 25-hydroxycholecalciferol in the liver, is limited because 25-hydroxycholecalciferol has a feedback inhibitory effect on the conversion. As a result, intake of vitamin D can increase many times while plasma 25-hydroxycholecalciferol remains nearly normal. This feedback also conserves vitamin D, which can be stored in the liver for months, whereas 25-hydroxycholecalciferol persists for only a few weeks. Renal conversion to 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol is regulated by PTH and plasma calcium and does not hide a rise in intake. Ingested vitamin D compounds are functionally the same as the cholecalciferol made in the skin. PTH acts on the renal step, not the hepatic step. Vitamin D is fat soluble, which is why it is lost in steatorrhea, and it is stored rather than excreted unchanged in urine.","boardPrep":"Step 1: D3 from skin (7-dehydrocholesterol plus UV) or diet is hydroxylated in the liver to 25-OH D3, the storage form, then in the kidney to 1,25-(OH)2 D3, the active form. COMLEX: Darker skin, prematurity, limited sun exposure, malabsorption, CKD, and advanced liver disease predispose to vitamin D deficiency.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 2 — PTH stimulates the renal step that converts 25-hydroxycholecalciferol to the active hormone","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"Renal conversion to 1,25-dihyd","C":"Ingested vitamin D compounds a","D":"PTH acts on the renal step, no"}},{"id":"sdl36_n10","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain the main physiologic functions of Vitamin D","batch":1,"stem":"When plasma calcium is above normal and PTH secretion is suppressed, how is 25-hydroxycholecalciferol mainly handled by the kidney?","choices":{"A":"It is converted to 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol at a faster rate to raise calcium further","B":"It is converted back to cholecalciferol and returned to the liver for long-term storage","C":"It is converted to 24,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol, which has almost no vitamin D activity","D":"It is converted to 7-dehydrocholesterol and carried to the skin for later reactivation","E":"It is excreted unchanged in the urine because the proximal tubule cannot take it up"},"explanation":"Formation of 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol in the proximal tubule requires PTH. When plasma calcium rises above about 9 to 10 mg/dL, PTH secretion is greatly suppressed, and 25-hydroxycholecalciferol is instead converted to 24,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol, a compound with almost no vitamin D effect. Calcium ions also have a slight direct effect that opposes formation of the active hormone. The resulting fall in 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol reduces calcium absorption and helps return plasma calcium toward normal. Increasing active hormone production in hypercalcemia would be the opposite of this feedback. Conversion back to cholecalciferol or to 7-dehydrocholesterol is not a pathway the kidney uses, and the proximal tubule does take up and hydroxylate 25-hydroxycholecalciferol.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Renal 1alpha-hydroxylase is stimulated by high PTH, low Ca2+, and low PO4; 1,25-(OH)2 D3 feedback-inhibits its own production. COMLEX: In hypercalcemia from a cause other than PTH or vitamin D, expect suppressed PTH and depressed formation of active vitamin D.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 3 — A rising plasma calcium suppresses PTH secretion","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"Increasing active hormone prod"}},{"id":"sdl36_n21","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain the main physiologic functions of Vitamin D","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman with end-stage renal disease has been on hemodialysis for 6 years. She reports bone pain and has had two low-trauma fractures. Serum calcium is 8.0 mg/dL, phosphate is 6.8 mg/dL, PTH is markedly elevated, 25-hydroxyvitamin D is normal, and 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D is low. Which of the following best explains her low active vitamin D?","choices":{"A":"Uremic liver injury prevents 25-hydroxylation of the cholecalciferol absorbed from her diet","B":"Hemodialysis removes 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol faster than her kidneys can produce it","C":"Loss of functioning proximal tubule cells prevents 1-hydroxylation of 25-hydroxycholecalciferol","D":"Her high PTH diverts 25-hydroxycholecalciferol into the inactive 24,25-dihydroxy compound","E":"Reduced synthesis from 7-dehydrocholesterol in her skin limits substrate for the kidney"},"explanation":"The final activation of vitamin D, conversion of 25-hydroxycholecalciferol to 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol, occurs in the proximal tubules of the kidneys, and without functioning kidneys vitamin D loses almost all of its effectiveness. Renal rickets and osteomalacia result mainly from failure of damaged kidneys to form the active hormone, a problem that can be severe in patients on hemodialysis. Her normal 25-hydroxyvitamin D shows that skin synthesis, dietary intake, and hepatic 25-hydroxylation are intact, which rules out a liver or skin explanation and localizes the defect to the renal step. The textbook problem in dialysis patients is failure of formation, not removal of the hormone. PTH promotes formation of 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol; conversion to the 24,25 compound occurs when PTH is suppressed. Her high PTH is secondary hyperparathyroidism driven by low calcium and high phosphate.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CKD causes low 1,25-(OH)2 D3 and high PO4, lowering Ca2+ and driving secondary and later tertiary hyperparathyroidism and renal osteodystrophy. COMLEX: In CKD, phosphate is initially kept normal by rising FGF23, which promotes renal phosphate excretion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 1 — Secondary and tertiary hyperparathyroidism in chronic kidney disease","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"Her normal 25-hydroxyvitamin D","B":"The textbook problem in dialys","E":"Her normal 25-hydroxyvitamin D"}},{"id":"sdl36_n22","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain the main physiologic functions of Vitamin D","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman has a dry cough, bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy, and noncaseating granulomas on transbronchial biopsy. Serum calcium is 11.9 mg/dL, phosphate is high-normal, PTH is suppressed, and 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D is elevated. Which of the following best explains her hypercalcemia?","choices":{"A":"Granulomas secrete PTH-related peptide, which raises calcium by binding the PTH receptor","B":"Granuloma macrophages activate vitamin D independently of PTH, raising the active hormone","C":"Suppressed PTH increases renal formation of 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D through lost feedback","D":"Granulomas infiltrate the parathyroid glands and release their stored PTH into the blood","E":"Hilar lymph nodes store calcium as hydroxyapatite and release it when they become inflamed"},"explanation":"In sarcoidosis, noncaseating granulomas contain activated macrophages with increased 1alpha-hydroxylase activity, so vitamin D is activated outside the kidney and without PTH control. The high 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D increases intestinal calcium absorption, and the resulting hypercalcemia suppresses PTH. PTH-related peptide is the mediator of hypercalcemia in many malignancies, such as squamous cell carcinoma of the lung; it would not explain a high 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D in granulomatous disease. Renal formation of 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D requires PTH, so suppressed PTH would lower, not raise, renal production. Release of stored PTH would raise PTH, but her PTH is suppressed. Lymph nodes are not calcium reservoirs; bone is.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hypercalcemia with suppressed PTH and high 1,25-(OH)2 D3 suggests granulomatous disease (sarcoidosis) or lymphoma; PTHrP-mediated hypercalcemia suggests squamous cell carcinoma, renal, bladder, breast, or ovarian cancer. COMLEX: Sarcoidosis shows bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy, noncaseating granulomas, and elevated ACE and calcium.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 1 — PTH-independent hypercalcemia is distinguished from primary hyperparathyroidism by a suppressed PTH","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B"},{"id":"sdl36_n11","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain Vitamin D's role in calcium absorption from the intestinal tract, bone deposition, and bone resorption.","batch":1,"stem":"1,25-Dihydroxycholecalciferol increases intestinal calcium absorption mainly by inducing calbindin in the epithelial cells. Which feature of this action explains why calcium absorption stays elevated for weeks after the hormone is withdrawn?","choices":{"A":"Calbindin is secreted into the lumen, where it holds dietary calcium for several weeks","B":"The hormone is stored inside enterocytes and is released slowly over several weeks","C":"Calbindin permanently converts enterocytes into a calcium-secreting cell population","D":"Calbindin forms within seconds and then acts as a permanent brush border channel","E":"Calbindin forms over about 2 days and then remains in the cells for several weeks"},"explanation":"1,25-Dihydroxycholecalciferol acts like a hormone on intestinal epithelial cells, increasing the formation of calbindin, a calcium-binding protein, over a period of about 2 days. Calbindin works at the brush border to move calcium into the cytoplasm, and calcium then crosses the basolateral membrane by facilitated diffusion; the rate of absorption is proportional to the amount of calbindin. Because the protein remains in the cells for several weeks after the hormone has been removed, calcium absorption stays elevated long after the hormone is gone. Calbindin works within the epithelial cells rather than as a luminal binder. The persistence comes from the protein, not from hormone stored in enterocytes. Calbindin increases absorption rather than converting cells to secretion, and its induction takes about 2 days, not seconds.","boardPrep":"Step 1: 1,25-(OH)2 D3 binds a nuclear vitamin D receptor partnered with the retinoid X receptor and changes gene transcription, so its effect on absorption develops over days. COMLEX: Correcting vitamin D deficiency raises calcium absorption gradually; symptomatic hypocalcemic tetany needs intravenous calcium.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 2 — Lipophilic hormones such as calcitriol act on intracellular receptors that regulate transcription","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"A":"Calbindin works within the epi","C":"Calbindin increases absorption"}},{"id":"sdl36_n12","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain Vitamin D's role in calcium absorption from the intestinal tract, bone deposition, and bone resorption.","batch":1,"stem":"Vitamin D has dose-dependent effects on bone. Which of the following best describes these effects?","choices":{"A":"Physiologic amounts cause bone resorption, whereas excess amounts promote mineralization","B":"All amounts promote mineralization, and vitamin D never contributes to bone resorption","C":"All amounts cause bone resorption, and vitamin D never contributes to bone mineralization","D":"Physiologic amounts promote mineralization, whereas excess amounts cause bone resorption","E":"No amount affects bone directly, because the small intestine is its only target organ"},"explanation":"In smaller quantities, vitamin D promotes bone calcification, partly by increasing calcium and phosphate absorption and partly by a direct effect on mineralization. Excess vitamin D causes resorption of bone, and in the absence of vitamin D the bone-resorbing effect of PTH is greatly reduced. First Aid summarizes the same pattern: increased mineralization at low levels and increased resorption at higher levels. The reversed statement contradicts this. The two all-or-none statements each ignore one of the two documented actions. Vitamin D has effects on bone and kidney as well as on the intestine, so it is not limited to the gut.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Vitamin D deficiency causes rickets in children and osteomalacia in adults; vitamin D excess causes hypercalcemia, hypercalciuria, loss of appetite, and stupor. COMLEX: Granulomatous diseases such as sarcoidosis cause hypercalcemia through increased activation of vitamin D by macrophages.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 2 — PTH-driven bone resorption depends on the presence of vitamin D","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"The reversed statement contrad","B":"The two all-or-none statements","C":"The two all-or-none statements","E":"Vitamin D has effects on bone "}},{"id":"sdl36_n23","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain Vitamin D's role in calcium absorption from the intestinal tract, bone deposition, and bone resorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 2-year-old boy who spent the winter almost entirely indoors is brought in during early spring for bowing of the legs. He drinks little milk and takes no supplements. Radiographs show widened, poorly mineralized growth plates. Serum calcium is 8.6 mg/dL (slightly low), phosphate is markedly low, alkaline phosphatase is high, and PTH is elevated. Which of the following best explains why his phosphate is much lower than his calcium?","choices":{"A":"Vitamin D deficiency blocks phosphate absorption completely but leaves calcium uptake intact","B":"Growing bone takes up phosphate selectively and leaves calcium behind in the extracellular fluid","C":"Calcitonin rises in rickets and lowers phosphate by moving it into the osteoid of growth plates","D":"Compensatory PTH holds calcium up by bone resorption while it raises urinary phosphate loss","E":"Low phosphate is the primary defect, and it is the low phosphate that causes the elevated PTH"},"explanation":"In vitamin D deficiency rickets, plasma calcium is only slightly depressed but phosphate is greatly depressed. The parathyroid glands prevent calcium from falling by promoting bone resorption whenever it begins to fall, but no comparable system defends phosphate, and the increased PTH actually increases phosphate excretion in the urine. Rickets typically appears in children kept indoors through winter, often in spring once the vitamin D stored from the previous summer is used up. Vitamin D promotes absorption of both calcium and phosphate, and calcium absorption depends on it heavily, so calcium uptake is not spared. Growing, poorly calcified bone is not selectively taking up phosphate; the osteoid fails to calcify for lack of calcium and phosphate. Calcitonin is not the driver here. A low phosphate does not stimulate PTH; a high phosphate does, and here PTH is responding to the tendency toward low calcium.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Rickets labs: low or low-normal Ca2+, low PO4, high PTH, high ALP; genu varum (bowlegs). Treatment combines adequate calcium and phosphate with large doses of vitamin D. COMLEX: Give oral vitamin D to breastfed infants; darker skin and prematurity predispose to deficiency.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 1 — Secondary hyperparathyroidism develops as compensation for vitamin D deficiency","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"Vitamin D promotes absorption ","C":"Calcitonin is not the driver h","E":"A low phosphate does not stimu"}},{"id":"sdl36_n24","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain Vitamin D's role in calcium absorption from the intestinal tract, bone deposition, and bone resorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old man has taken 50,000 IU of vitamin D daily for 4 months after reading about it online. He presents with anorexia, constipation, polyuria, and lethargy. Serum calcium is 13.4 mg/dL, PTH is suppressed, and 24-hour urinary calcium is high. Which of the following best explains his hypercalcemia?","choices":{"A":"Excess vitamin D activity increases intestinal calcium absorption and promotes bone resorption","B":"Excess vitamin D activity raises PTH secretion, which then drives osteoclastic bone resorption","C":"Excess vitamin D activity blocks glomerular filtration of calcium, so calcium builds up in plasma","D":"Excess vitamin D activity suppresses calcitonin, removing the main defense against high calcium","E":"Excess vitamin D activity makes renal calcium reabsorption its dominant effect on plasma calcium"},"explanation":"Vitamin D increases intestinal calcium absorption, mainly by inducing calbindin, and excess vitamin D causes resorption of bone; together these raise plasma calcium. First Aid lists the expected picture of vitamin D excess: hypercalcemia, hypercalciuria, loss of appetite, and stupor. The hypercalcemia suppresses PTH, so PTH is not the driver. His urinary calcium is high, so calcium is being filtered and excreted rather than retained by a filtration block. Calcitonin is not important in normal calcium homeostasis, so its suppression would not be the main cause. The effect of vitamin D on renal calcium reabsorption is weak and not of major importance in regulating plasma calcium.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PTH-independent hypercalcemia (suppressed PTH): excess calcium intake, cancer, increased vitamin D. Vitamin D excess causes hypercalcemia, hypercalciuria, anorexia, and stupor. COMLEX: Fat-soluble vitamins accumulate in fat, so toxicity is more common than with water-soluble vitamins; ask about supplement use in unexplained hypercalcemia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 3 — Hypercalcemia suppresses PTH secretion through the calcium-sensing receptor","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"The hypercalcemia suppresses P"}}]};
